      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>t LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NirT.T,TE, Some of the new blouses are made with trans. parent yokes, in preparation for the hot weather. In many of these the open work is carried to an extreme. The whole of the top of the arms shows through, as well as a considerable portion of the neck and shoulders. Sometimes lace, sometimes muslin, embroidery forms this open-work, upon which the silk of the blouse is hung in tucks alternating with the silk open-stitch that has been so largely used of late years. Narrow black velvet is used by the mile in trimming tea,gowns and blouses, edging frills, and rows of lace inser- tion. The width of this velvet is about a fourth of and inch in width. Another trimming that is used by the furlong is liar:ow black and white ribbon, either wholly silk, or a mixture of silk and velvet. A blouse made of black and white striped silk has a white poult-de-soic sailor collar, bor- dered with three rows of black and -white ribbon, with a little dotted pattern up the centre. The ends of the handkerchief are hemmed round with it. This handkerchief," in most of the sailor blouses, exists only in its ends. The rest of it would be only in the way under the collar and revers.</p>
        <p>The hats which I have recently :term show how great is the variety in chapeaux just now. The first I came across was a toque of ciel blue tulle, veiled with coarse-meshed white net, and raised at the side with frosted white and silver leaves. The next was a piquant shape in coarse burnt straw, with large white ostrich plumes on the ,crown and under the brim; there is no other trim- ming. The third hat was in white .r-ice straw, -veiled with lace of an ecru shade, with large bows of vert de printemps ribbon covering the crown, from which springs a white gourra plume, and bunches of cowslips are under the brim. The fourth hat was of puffed black chiffon, with roses of blue areophane, and narrow bows of black eatin ribbon lightly poised above the brim.</p>
        <p>Buttons are a great feature of dress just now. Those worn will include those in paste, fine-cue steel, and silver, such as used to be worn on the velvet coats of the gentlemen who took snuff as part of their social behaviour. Old-fashioned jewelled buttons may sometimes be picked up cheaply enough, and refurbished for use. Ame- thysts, coral, turquoise, garnets, and other of the less expensive stones are occasionally to be had cheap. The dainty little Watteau buttons with miniatures set in a frame of steel or silver are again in favour. Irish harps and shamrocks rule the fancy for the moment. They are to be seen in enamel or white or green ground. Belts are fastened with buttons at the left side, and the cost of these is often as great as that of the entire cos- tume. At the same time pretty buttons can be picked up cheaply enough. Steel buttons are very fashionable again, in every size. Others are in steel and jet mixed. On blouses and dresses small buttons are often placed in groups of threes, and tucks are also arranged in threes.</p>
        <p>There will be many cycling costumes to be seen '.is season in khaki colouring, and here at least, common-senee and sentiment may shake -110.nds, for the traces of dust so apparent on our costumes of darker hue will no longer vex our souls, and we may ride with an easy mind over roads deep in the dust, which rises in clouds at the passing of the scorcher. Nothing could be more warmly recommended for a gown to be donned for a ride on a motor-car, for loud and grievous have hitherto been the complaints re- garding the materials already in use. ' Indeed, there is no mistake regarding the fact that our military authorities displayed much shrewdness in the selection of a colour and material for the garb of the soldier, as khaki is singularly inconspicuous, and can brave any amount of contact with dust or mud without betraying the fact very notice ably.</p>
        <p>Your loving,</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
