      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The new Duke of Argyll, succeeds tn more titles than be can easily remember. It seems strange to speak!of the Marquis of Lcrne, and that Princess Louise as the Duke and Duchess of Argyll, and it will be some time before we become accustempd to the chluge. But, no one, a! least, outside cf Sootlsnd, would recognise the Marquis of Lorne in most of the dztn or more titles to which Ist succeeds. _ _</p>
        <p>Besides being Duke, Marquis, and Earl of Argyll,be is Earl of Campbell. and Cewae Visceunt Lochow arid Glenilla, Lord of Inverary, Mull. Morvern and Tiry, Lord Hameton,and Baron Sundridge. This is only part of the full style and title of the Duke, and in addition he bolds many offices such as that of the Hereditary Master of Her Majesty's Household in Scotland, and Keeper cal: the Great Seal of Scotland. The Duke owns about 175,000 acres in Argyllshire. and Dumbarton, with it is said, a rent el about £50,000 a year. Sir William MacCormac, and Mr. Treves both agree in praising the excellent hospital and medical arrangemen:a which have been provided for the wounded in South Africa. They hsve just returnsd after rendering great services at the frost.aed they say that never before were the wounded so well cared for. This gratifying state of things, is one of the brightest features of the war, as is also the splendid conduct of the British soldier of whom both Sir William MacCormac and Mr Treves speak in terms of the highest praise. With one or two exceptions, Mr. Troves seems to have ' been satisfied with everything be saw. There were, he says, only two plagues in South Atrica—the plague of flies, and the plague of women. The flies were net so bad, but the women were" really and absceutely a terror." Sir Alfred Milner, it will be rem,mbered, re- , cently issued a public protest against the invasion in , South Africa, of visitors in general, and women in par- , ticular. But Mr. Treves has more in mind, these women ' who "having xhaueted every other form of excitement go out in the guise of hospital nurses." Everyone will recognise the class of women to which Mr. Treves refers —these who play at nursing without soiling their fingers and who think infinitely more of their picnics and sneamer toileWs. There have been many complaints from the Cape of those useless pesple overcrowding the heseitals, thotieh 'boy. ef ceurse, Talkie devote tbeir , attention to the officers' quarters. The scene of war ts I no place for dainty ernes( ur nurses, tboegh it is only fair to say of these to whom Mr. Treves so scathingly refers, that many of them went out because they have relatives at the front. Quite the most fashionable weddino ef the season tcok place the ether day at the Russian Chapel in London, when Mdlle. de Steal. and Count Davidoff were married in the presence of the Prince of Wales, and a dis— tingniahed compeny. The bride is the only daughter of the Russian Ambessacler, and Madame de Steal, and has been a maid of Honour to the Empress of Russia. The bridegroom's family also occupies high positions of State at the Cetart of St. Petersburg. The marriage service was celebrated accerdine to Ow rites of the Greek Church, which are both solemn and quaint. The bride and briee- groom both carry a lighted taper, two rings are used, which are exchanged, and at one point, after the priest has joined their hande, they step on to a piece of carpet laid at their feet. This is a moment of ereat interest because it is supposed that the one who firat steps upon the carpet will be the ruler of the household. What however, strikes one very forcibly in the cere- mony, is the danger from the lighted tapers, and the wedding of the Count and Countess Davideff might easily have ended in a dire calamity for this reason, During the exchange of the rings, the bride set firs to her veil, which instantly blazed up. The accident seems to have been not altogether unexpected, and thanks to ready assistance, she escaped with nothing worse than a little singeing of the hair, and the uneeremenious removal of the burning veil. It was a most unfortunate occurrence, but everyone felt releved when they realised what a narrow escape they had had from far worse con- sequences. In America, " Hints to Worshippers "are often drawn , up and exhibited et the Church doors. This example,l it is stated, is being followed at All Saints' Church, Bel- faet,wbere such advice is offered as "Be in time." DOTO, talk or lounge or look round to see who is camine in." "If the sermon is dull,and you cannot attend to it,thinh of your own faults and preach ycurself a sermon." No one can dispute that this is excellent advice for both old and you're, and possibly we may some day Fee this system generally adopted in our churches and chapels. There is another innovation coming into use. in America, and that is the provisien of eloak rooms at places of worship. There is perhaps En nerd of these in the country, but in London and other large cities, there have been many complaints of robberies of wearing ap- parel in the churches. While one goes up to Crnernunion leaving such articles as a cloak and umbrella in the pew another walks them off. There have been RO many cases of this kind in the West End of London during the past Lent that in several churches private detectives have been employed to protect the worshippers against robbery. It is evident that the Baptiste are in a fair way of rsomplel.ing their Twentieth Century Fund, which,as the Rev. J. H. Shatresosare announced, has reached a total of more than 2135,000. As mieht have been expscted, London, Yorkshire, and Lancashire, have furnished the lareest contributions, the respective amounts !mine £27,000. £lB,OOO, and £14,000. Notwithstandine the success which has been achieved. the Baptists do not in- tend to rest contented, and purpose, in the time which remit ins, making a great effort to bring the scheme to a triumphant issue: In the millinery world it was, ones upon a time an unwritten law, that no kind of fruit or flower, should be worn, eave that in season. We have changed all that. We take no notice of the seasons nowadays,adorning our headgear with poppies at Christmas, and holly berries at Midsummer, if it pleases the pewees that be, to say it is cerreet." The cherries at the moment en much ex evidence are scarcely rt alistic. One woeld hardly he seized with a desire to consume them their teeny aspect giving one a woolly teete in one's mouth. The cuerante, and grapes, are happier imitatiens while flowers and foliage this year, are more exquisite than ever before. Shk gowns for day wear are a failure. Modistes have tried hard to revive this particular mode, but sw, will have none of it, and wisely. Unless made to parfectien, the silk gown is a hopelessinatitution, moreover directly tbe first freshness has worn off, it is impossible it should be suejected to the searching light of day. Further the idea is an extravagant one and a thoroughly unsatisfac- tory cne, from every point of view. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
