      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MRS. G. HUBI-NEWCOMBE.</head>
        <p>MRS. G. HUBI-NEWCOMBE.</p>
        <p>No name is more familiar to all music lovers than that of G. Hubi-Newcombe,which is inscribed on so many a title page. Yet it is possible that to many people outside the charmed mueical circle the ' name couveye a mistaken conception of the owner's sex. There is nothing in Mrs Newcombe' e composi- tions to warrant any conelueion on the subject one way or the other, for while her dainty lyrics and musicianly compositions betray a feminine delicacy and gracefulness of style, they are characterised also by a breadth and freedom of treatment which is associated with a manly habit. Yet their author is not only of the gentler s.-x, but a woman whose natural femininity is intensified by a frailty of coil- stitution which presents a constant check upon the too actively vigorous bruin. Latterly we have come to connect Mrs. Hubi- Newcombe'e name more particularly with lyrical eesupositien, for she devotes herself now-a-days chiefly to this work. "For the last ten or twelve years," Mrs. Huhi- Newcombe confesses, " it has been all my pen could do to keep pace with tee demand for my lyrics, and to supply the many composers who ask for my words' for musical setting. 1 have prodaced, at, a rough gates, between four and five hundred lyrics, most of which have been set' by the most promi- nent composers of the present day." Before becoming a lyric author, Mrs. Huhi-New- combs was a proliic musical composer, producing, in addition to songs, pianoforte musiceirgan,choral, and church music, a number of instrumental works ef a very high order, which have been—and are beine—performed by all the leading orchestral and military bands in the kingdom. Mrs. Hubi-Newcombe combines,with her wonder- ful creative faculties, brilliant powers of execution She was at, one time the organist of St. James's, Chureh,T.atchford but had to relinquish this position owing to her health giving way under the strain of prot-ssional awl musical work. "To my great grief," she says. I had to give up my organ, though I love the" kirg of instru- ments "beyond expression. Unfortunately I have never sufficiently regained strength to take it up again ; indeed, I am more or less an invalid, though a hard working one." Mrs. Idubi-Newcombe is indeed a hardworking invalid, her wonderful vitality and mental power enabling her to produce an incredible amount of work under conditions net merely of chronic weak- ness, but often of acute physical pain. Nature,how- ever. revenges herself for this persistent, defiance of her limitations, and a frequent total collapse is the result, body and brain alike exhausted. But Mrs. Hubi-Newcombe is incorrigible in her energy in spite of these repeated reprimends. " Write I must when my muse inspires," she de- clares, " when ideas flow fast and fascinatingly,and will not be denied utterance ; and, really, it is my happiest time when I can forget the outside worries of this weary world in the beauties of an imaginary one, and in the absorption of creating." Mrs, ewcorabe's admirable work is familiar to our readers, for many dainty lyrics from her pen have from time to time adorned the peges of the " Gentleman's Journal." Her versatility is no less delightful than the melodious and easy gracefulness of expression which characterises all her produc- tions. One of her meet stirring poems, " Hurrah ! for our Soldiers" (now being produced as a song by Messrs. Willcocks, of Berners-street), was inspired hy the heroism of the British troops in South Africa, and appeared in the February issue of this Journal. Its fervent expression and martial swing convey to perfection the spirit which animates every loyal Briton at the present moment, end the poem is a worthy tribute to the gallant soldiers in whose honour it is written. In striking contrast with this, almosit, braggadocio spirit is the tender passion of the love-song," Just, for to-night, love." and the reverent. pathes of those touching lines," A Voice from Heaven." Truly this talented writer can be relegated to no particular class or branch, but stande right, in the front rank of all I yrical authors.—The Gentleman's Journal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>DON'T DILLY DALLY.</head>
