      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>THE EXAMINEE:, FRIDAY, MAY 4 1900</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>STEVE, THE OUTLANDER.</p>
        <p>Dr ARTHUR LAYCOCIC, Autboz The Young Gaffer," "Seaside Sketches,'"</p>
        <p>"Lancashire Sketches," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.</p>
        <p>-At no= on the following day the Canary Islands were sighlted.' and ere eight bells struck the vessel dropped: einehor off Las Palmas. As passengets were permitted to go ashore during the brief stay, Percival decided to avail himself /k the opportunity, and asked Ella to accompany him. "The Tem is cheap," he said, "and the girls are pretty, the steward informs me'; but not one is prettier than you, I'll warrant.. Come and see ' " She declined to go, however.* He descendeu the lumbering rowing-boat, one of a score or swarming about the ship. As Elk% looked down at the little craft, she saw, that the gaunt, tall Barnard was among Ralph's companions, his pale visage contrasting sharply with the bronzed faces of the swarthy Spanish oarsmen. Percival did not return to the ship until near midnight. Ella was looking out for him, and was alarmed to see him come aboard with his right hand swathed in a bandage. "Been in a bit of a row," he explained, as he reeled int; the saloon. "Went round the town, saw the sie„,7s, had a few drinks, and while I was talking tc a glrl, one of those murdering Spaniards rushed up. and commenced jabbering. S'pose the girl was eag senorita. I wasn't going to stand his interferemm ; so we set to, and he jabbed me with • his knife. I should have been gone for, I believe, if Barnari.l hadn't come up. He-just came in time, and bowle4 that villain over with the first blow. Not bad for an invalid, eh? Then he stuck me in a mule-cart, and brought me along." The shies surgeon attended to the wound, which was slie-4, being merely a surface 'scratch. As Ralph wee in an inebriated state, the stewards con- ducted MT) to his cabin and put him in his bunk. In less than an hour the ship was again under way. For seiwel days following the heat was most oppressiTe 123 the steamship gradually neared tho Equator. Day and night alike were torrid and sultry. Down in the cabins where the steerage passengers were quartered, the heat was suffocating. At fast the "line" was crossed, and the ship, ever steanyin southward, ran into cooler latitudes. It was wall very hot during the day; but the nights were rettsAingly cool. The sea was gloriously calm, and each evening, as the phosphorescent light I played about the ship, the passengers assembled on the upper decks and sang and danced right merrily. The first and second-class passengers held their concerts in the large, airy saloon, and danced on the after-deck to the strains of the stewards' orchestra. Ella, who had settled down comfortably in her improved quarters, greatly enjoyed the al fresco balls. She danced with Percival on the spacious deck, and, as she looked at the tranquil ocean below, and the clear, starry heavens above, felt supremely content with her lot. She thought with horror of the crowded cabins forward, of the crying baby, the weary mother, the coughing con- sumptive. How dreadful it must have been in that part of the ship during the hot, stifling days and nights! She had much to thank Ralph for. But for him she would have been in torture now amongst those people forward. It was, indeed, thoughtless of Steve to send her such a small amount of money— scarce suffic:ont to take her to South Africa at the cheapest rate, Was it thoughtlessness or was it something else? He was perhaps stingy, miserly. Ralph had told her that these miners acquired gold fever, were infected with a love of gold which drove out love for aught else. Perhaps Steve was thus fever-smitten; if so, she could not love him ! Ralph, with all his faults, was not miserly, any- how ! Indeed, he went to the other extreme; squandered his money recklessly. He studied her every need, anticipated every desire. After a dance he would order iced drinks, fruit, the best to be obtained aboard. Rare delicacies he delighted to procure for her at table. He was ever kind and attentive. And his kindness had been most disinterested, she assured herself. In their first long chat together, when he had brought her to the after-part of the ship, she had told him of her fixed deter- mination to marry Steve; told him that after their last meeting in Southport she had resolved to be true to Steve, and it was that final resolve which had caused her to flee so hurriedly away. Percival had listened to her avowal without com- ment, and Ella believed that he was now resigned to his unlucky fate, and had no desire to again endeavour to turn her from her purpose. But Ella lacked discrimination. She could not see beneath the surface, could not comprehend the subtle cunning of Ralph Percival. They sat on the promenade-deck one evening watching the lightning flash up fitfully Azom the horizon. Both were in contemplative mood, and for a time no word passed between them. Percival, as usual, was smoking. They had chosen a secluded nook, and no passengers were in their vicinity. Occasionally a steward, bearing a salver, passed, and disappeared into the brilliantly-lighted saloon, where a concert was in progress. The night was gloriously calm and serene, giving one the impression of profound peace end restful- ness. Not a breath of wind stirred, not a ripple was on the face of the waters. From time to time the lightning flashes lit up the illimitable ocean with rare beauty, then all was dark again. At length Percival turned abruptly and quizzically regarded his companion. "We have spent many happy hours together on this ship, Miss Rayburn," he observed. He had often addressed her as "Ella " of late; "such familiarity," he said, "being preferable on ship- board between friends.' " She herself had been dwelling on the thought he expressed. "Many very happy hours, Mr. Cart- leigh—l beg your pardon, Mr. Percival," she said. "Excuse the slip; for the moment I was taken back to other happy times when you were Mr. Cartleigh." "And you were Ella Rayburn, the weaver lass," he added. "I'm glad that you sometimes think of certain happy times we have spent together in tbeo past, as well as the more recent happy times." A lengthy pause ensued, during which Percival devoted all his attention to the Teneriffe cigar. "Are you glad that I came aboard ? " he asked. abruptly. "Glad? Yes." Then, in a tone of sincere ,regret: "But I'm also sorry. For your sake," she added, answering his questioning look. "For my sake ? " "Yes. It would have been better for you if you had not come aboard to me. The parting will be very painful—for both of us." "For both of us," he repeated to himself. "Ah, she feels the pang. She still clings to me. She is yet undecided. By Heaven! I'll win her!" As he did not speak to her at once, she thought he was sorrowing at her words. it was too dark to see his face, else could she but have seen the cruel, gloating expression written on it. she might, have taken warning in time. "I'm sorry, Ralph—Mr. Percival; you have been very kind to me." "Oh, don't/trouble about that," he said, in well- disguised ton's, affecting unconcern. "And it seems wrong that I should accept your many kindnesses and attentions, knowing that—" She hesitated. "Knowing 'hat?" "Knowing that we must part for ever at Cape- town." "But we slip not part!" "Mr. Perciial!" "I speak of what I know. We shall not part, T repeat.. I know your feelings better than you your. self do." "I'm going away. I--" - She was about to rise from her seat, but he heE..., her. "Stay a moinent, please. Now, do! Don't a::: away because I speak out." "But you dist not speak so." "Hear me, then do as you will."</p>
        <p>She feebly gave way. "Don't be alarmed. You've nothing to fear; I'm not such a fiend. I'm a lover—l'm a lover, Ella, and you know me as such. You have recog- tised me as such." "I ? " she exclaimed. "You have more than half-admitted your love for me. If you dared trust yourself, you would say that you loved me now. I am your lover, Ella, and I alone. I have been your lover long. I have sacrificed much for you. None other has loved you as I. You were mine a few days ago when we were at Southport. You were mine then; more truly mine than you have ever been another's at any time. And, by Heaven you shall be mine again. F.'t still now; don't be alarmed. . . • I am not to be thwarted, Ella. Think 17011 I haVO</p>
        <p>sought you these two years for naught? Think you that I have left England at a moment's noft.ce-- yea, left without saying it good-bye to my kindred,' and all this for naught? No, Ella. 1 have done this for you, for you! Say you will marry me. Say it, and we will be happy for evermore!" His impassioned speech ended, he waited im- patiently to see what effect it had produced. A low, gasping sob broke from the girl. As he heard it, Percival felt more gratified and content. He understood perfectly then the state of her mind and heart. - He sat closer to her, and, insidiously, his arm encircled her waist. "Don't! Oh, don't I must go away ! " she sobbed, drawing away from him. "But you'll promise, Ella? Promise, and put aa end once and for all to your misery, and mine." "Not to-night," she said, tremulously. "Nat to-night." "You have disappointed me before, Ella. you'll be mine, Ella!" "Not to-night. To-morrow," and she hurried</p>
        <p>Sa9</p>
        <p>away. In the quietude of her cabin the irresolute girl wrestled with her weak self throughout the long night, and at last; worn out and weary, she fell asleep. When morning broke, warm and sunny, and the refreshing breeze swept through the port-hole of her cabin, Ella awoke with a feeling of dread mingled with pleasure—a strange, indefinable sensation. Then she remembered that ere she had lapsed into unconsciousness she had decided to make the final plunge, and cast her future lot with Ralph Percival. The breakfast-bell was loudly ringing, and she hurriedly completed her toilet. When she entered the dining-saloon the stewards were already ministering to the wants of the passengers. Ralph Percival, busy with knife arid fork, was eating his eggs and bacon with evident relish. He gave Ella a cheery "good-morning," as she sat beside him, and as she raised her eyes, still red with much weeping, he looked for an answer in them. Ella turned her head, and try as he would he could not induce her to look•him in the face. The few remarks that passed between them were of the usual inconsequential, conventional character indulged in at table. The meal ended Ralph followed his companiel as she made her way to the upper deck. "Well, sweetest, what is it? " "I am yours, Ralph," she replied, briefly. "Good ! " he cried, exultingly, and would have kissed her there and then had they been out of observation. "I'll go below now and put on my hat," she said. "When I come back you mustn't talk to me much. I must be quiet to-day." - "You'll have a drink of wine," he said, on her return. "We'll drink to our engagement." "No, thanks, I'll not have anything. I want nothing only to be left alone." "She shall be left alone," he said, as he descended to the bar. He understood her condi- tion. "She's not quite sure where she is. In a bit of a fog yet. She'll be right in a day or so. Ah ! Steve, the Outlander, must take a back seat now. •He's out of the play altogether. We've dispensed with the hero, and the villain alone will occupy the stage until the curtain falls on the last act.' They saw little of each other during the remainder of the day. Percival spent most of his time below drinking heavily. Ella passed the time in her cabin, on the promenade deck, and in the saloon, ill at ease wherever she happened to be. The next morning she was in her normal condi- tion again. She was now reconciled to her fate. Percival did not put in an appearance at the breakfast-table. But Ella was not surprised at this •as she guessed that after the previous day's disal. pation he was not in a fit state to be seen. As she walked about on the upper deck later Barnard, the consumptive, entered into converse- ' tion with her. Despite his dour visage he was a cheery fellow, and Ella found in him a most entertaining companion. To and fro they paced the deck, chatting animatedly. "You are travelling to South Africa for the good of your health, I suppose ? " Ella remarked. "Yes, for the good of my health, as you say, and for the despoliation of my pocket. I'd give every farthing I possess to be as healthy as your friend. "Health is indeed the greatest wealth. Yet it is not all." Barnard could not understand this last observa- tion. What did she mean? Could this pretty coquette know anything of life's tragedy as he knew it? He remembered how her "friend" had conducted himself at Las Palmas. "Ah! Perhaps she does know something," he thought. "No, health may not be all in life," he observed, "but it is everything to a sick man." Percival now came forward and regarded Ella with a suspicious, jealous eye. He still smelt strongly of liquor, and Barnard, noting his half- dazed condition, recalled the girl's words: "Health is not all." "Are you two looking out for land ? " said Percival. "We are longing for it," said Barnard, "am everyone else aboard is, I imagine." "Ah, yes, we're all longing for it, aren't we, Ella? We are longing for it. Good old terra- firma. When we get ashore we'll stop there, eh, my girl ? " He took her arm and Barnard, recognising that "two's company three's none," left them." "Yes, I'm longing for it, sweetest. I've been longing for it all night and all yesterday, and every hour in fact since you promised to be mine." "S-s-sh, not so loud!" she whispered, fearing that his words, would be overheard. "No, no, I'll not keep mum! Why should I? I kept quiet all day yesterday just because you wished me to. I can't bottle myself up for ever, though I bottled myself up yesterday in a different sense. No, I must speak, I must shout, I must sing, I must dance, I—" "Well, do all that to your heart's content when you get ashore; not here." "I've nothing to be afraid of here. Why should I be quiet? I'm not afraid of the best man aboard here knowing that you are mine—you are mine. Stay I What were you doing flirting with that ghost Barnard? I shan't allow that, you know. No followers now." "Bah! We were only talking commonplaces. Then Mr. Barnard began telling me all sorts of things, and I asked him where he was going." "Why, I could have told you that. He is going to his grave," and Percival laughed cruelly. "0 Ralph! Shocking "Nothing shocking about it. It's plain, solernta fact. That fellow won't live twelve months. You can see it in hitt; face. Where did he say he was going?" "To the Orange Free State, on a farm near Bloemfontein." "Well, he'll be in a freer state before long, where he'll not need farm produce." "Ralph! You are shocking ! " "Not I, indeed. That's another solid fact. He'll not cost much to keep. I guess his landlady will make a good profit out of him as long as he lasts. He'll not be a boarder long. He'll be boarded elsewhere between boards soon." Ella changed the subject. She could not bear to hear his flippant, vulgar words. "When do you think we shall arrive at Cape. town ? " "About midnight, the officers say. Of course we can't go ashore at that untimely hour, though I wouldn't mind if you are agreeable." "I shall not be agreeable," she replied. fact, I don't want to go ashore at Capetown." "Don't want what ? " he exclaimed in amaze. "Haven't you had enough of this weary sailing, sailing, day after day, week after week? I thought you were longing, hungering for land ? " "I am," she answered, meekly, and her head drooped sadly. "I am longing for land, but--" "11'm I I see," he observed, with a jeer. "Do you want us to go forward, then ? " "Yes. Where can we go? What other pu..4',1 does the boat sail to ? " "Port Elizabeth, East London, Durban." "Durban! Where is that ? " "Natal. Thousand miles up the coast," he answered, shortly. "Go there if you like." "All right," Ella replied, quietly. Percival was in a rage. He had looked forward to landing at Capetown. "Of course," he reflected, "I know why she doesn't want to land there. She's thinking of that fool Hart, Steve the Outlander. Egad I I would like to see him as he comes down to the quay look- ing for his girl. At noon the South African coast came into view, and the elated passengers crowded on deck to look on the land of promise. Nearer and nearer the ship drew to the Cape, but darknese had set in long before Table Bay was reached. Ella and Ralph Percival remained on deck until near midnight in the expectation of seeing the harbour lights ere they retired to their cabins. But in the later hours a strong sou'west wind prevailed and rain descended in torrents. Then the two</p>
        <p>"In</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>watchers went below, but one, at least, did not sleep in her bunk that night. Ella Rayburn sat in her cabin with a blank sheet Jf notepaper, laid on an improvised desk, before her. An inkstand was there also. In her trembling hand she held a pen. "1 can't do it! Oh! 1 can't do it!" She cried, as for the third time she essayed to write. "Oh! It is too cruel! It will break his heart. 0 Steve! What have I done ? "</p>
        <p>Hot, blinding tears streamed down her haggard face as her form shook with convulsive sobs. Hour after hour she sat there, and as the dawn broke her dimmed eyes were yet riveted on the paper before her, but not.one word had she written. At last she rose wearily to her feet and surveyed her tear-stained face in the mirror. Her long, brown hair was loose and fell forward over her shoulders. A sad, wan face it was that-looked at her from the glass. "It is no use," she cried, turning away, "I must do it. I look in the glass and that face that I see is the reflection of my real self. To-morrow morning—nay this morning—l shall be my other self, that self that I have given to Ralph Cartleigh. I must say good-bye to the real self whose reflection is there in the glass and which pleads for Steve— poor Steve I and I must harden myself. Oh, I must not give way. It is too late now." She sat down and hurriedly scrawled the missive. "Steve, forgive me. I have wronged you. You have been deceived in me. I am now pledged to another. Good-bye—nay, I cannot wish you 'good' while I rend your heart so. Forget me; forgive me, if you can. There are better women, y A RAYBURN."u will find, than L Forgive me, Steve. —ELL A</p>
        <p>She nervously folded the note and placed it in an envelope which she addressed to Mr. Stephen Hart, care of the purser. Then she fell back on the settee and sobbed bitterly. And thus the stewardess found her at six o'clock, face buried in hands, a most pitiful looking object. "What is it, Miss Rayburn ? " asked the woman, kindly. "What is it, dear? Cheer up ! Why, we are in the harbour now, and all the folks ere rejoicing, while here you are weeping as if you were sorry to leave the ship. Cheer up, dear! Are you ill ? ' _</p>
        <p>_ "Oh, no, I am all right. It is nothing," she said, rising with a half-dazed expression on her face. "It is nothing. I shall be all right soon. Please give this to the purser, will you, and ask him to give it to—to any person who may happen to come aboard and ask for me." _ _</p>
        <p>She gave the letter to the stewardess, who took it in surprise. "Aren't you leaving us here, Miss Rayburn ? " "No, I'm going forward to Natal. Tell the purser I cannot see anyone who may come to inquire for me." tell him, Miss Rayburn," and the stewardess went on her errand. _ _ .</p>
        <p>There was much bustle and tumnit aboard the vessel now. All the passengers had risen early, and breakfast was served half-an-hour sooner than usual. Looking out of the port-hole, Ella could see majestic Table Mountain towering up above the town and bay. "Steve will be about the quay now," she thought, "anxiously waiting for the ship to come alongside. Oh, it is torturing to think of! I must forget it. I must go to the saloon, on deck, anywhere, to forget." "Are you coming to breakfast, Miss Rayburn ? " the stewardess asked, opening the cabin door.</p>
        <p>"Yes, in a moment—in a moment." "I gave your letter to the purser. Mr. Percival was chatting with him. Mr. Percival is now wait- ing for you in the saloon." "I'm coming," said Ella, - and followed the stewardess out. The dining-saloon was almost deserted. Most of the passengers had breakfasted, and were now preparing to go ashore. "Good gracious!" exclaimed Percival, as Ella came up to him. "What a time you've been! All the folks have gone nearly. Been up all night, eh? And all morning, too, I think, by the tired look of those eyes. Bad for the appetite, that, my girl. I was up early—not so early as you—and I'll warrant my appetite is better." Ile certainly had the better appetite of the two. He disposed of a chop, an omelette, and two poached eggs, whilst Ella nibbled at a small piece of dry toast. _</p>
        <p>"-You'll 'have to pick up, my girl. This will never do. Keep a good heart, and you will have good digestion. Look at me l" She did not look at him, and, perceiving that she was not in a talkative humour, he scowled, but ceased to trouble her further, and the meal was concluded in silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CHAPTER XXII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII.</p>
        <p>Stephen Hart rose early on Friday morning, and was the first person astir in the Bree-street boarding-house, at Capetown, with the exception of the Hottentot domestic, Dinah, who had wakened him from slumber according to instructions he had given her the night before. The hale, hearty young fellow, bronzed and brown, with the cute, sharp look of the seasoned colonial traveller, bore little resemblance to the unsophisticated young piecer who had come to this town two years before. As he regarded himself in the cracked mirror, a smile of satisfaction lighted up his radiant face. "Guess Ella will scarcely recogniselme. I can scarce recognise myself as the chap who lodged in this very house two years since. But I'll recognise her. Ah, yes! She'll be the same; the same bonny, sweet, true, faithful lassie as of yore." He looked out of the uncurtained, old-fashioned Dutch window. _</p>
        <p>"Ah, there's the flag flying on Signal Hill. Hurrah The boat is in sight. The Braemar Castle will be alongside in an hour. In less than an hour and five minutes I'll be alongside my lassie, clasp- ing her in my arms. Hurrah! I'll smother her with kisses. I'll-" "Helloa, old chap! What's the racket Who're you jawin' at ? " a sleepy voice asked, and from the far corner of the room a tousled head popped up in bed. "Oh, all right, old man. I'd quite forgot you were there. Believe me; I'd forgot everything for the moment."</p>
        <p>"I think you had. Seems to me you're love-sick, and you've got it very bad." "Right you are; dropped on it first guess." "And it's more fatal than dysentry or dingue- fever," continued the voice from the bed. "Well, so long! C4:-..,0d luck, old man! Send us a bit of the cake," and the tousled head fell back on the pillow as Steve bounded from the room. It was eight o'clock when he reached. the quay and joined the throng of people waiting for the liner to come alongside. For a full hour he waited, then slowly the big vessel drew up, and he was elbowed and jostled by the harbour officials and coloured porters, who rushed to and fro •along the quay-side. Then he took part in the jostling, and worked his way towards the big gangways. Everyone was anxious to get as near the ship as possible. Friends aboard were signalling to friends ashore, handkerchiefs were flying, and as the ship swung round and came closer to the quay, shouts of recognition were heard on all sides. Steve eagerly scanned the line of faces nearest to him, but no familiar face was there. Then he dis- covered that these were first and second-class passengers. Ella would be amongst the thirds. lie made his way through the crowd, and hurried up the quay to gain a view of the forward part of the ship. Here a gangway was already out, and in a moment, the passengers began to swarm across it. Steve scanned every face. Strange faces all. No sign 9f Ella. Where could she be ? He had expected that she would have leen on the look-out for him: Was she ill? Or had she missed the boat, or what ?</p>
        <p>---- - The stream of people continued to pass before him. Cornish miners, sturdy Scotsmen, Irish labourers, whose pronounced brogue was plainly heard above the babel of tongues; Jews, Italians, Germans, Norwegians, Roumanians, Frenchmen, all nationalities, but all of no interest to him. There was one missing; where was she? He rushed back to the other gangway aft. Porters and carmen were quarrelling with each other as each sought their human prey. A Malay Jehu carrying a large trunk and shouting loudly, roughly pushed Steve aside. In his wake walked the owner of the trunk, a tall, spare man, who looked at Steve in a friendly sort of way. "Excuse me," said Steve, addressing him. "Do you happen to know whether a Miss Rayburn has travelled by this boat? " "Miss Rayburn I " repeated Barnard. "Oh, yes, tat be sure. She is amongst the first-class pamengers." "he first-class," said Steve in surprise, then, overjtved by the discovery that she was really aboard, he pushed forward, leaving Barnard staring after him with a look of pity on his pale face. Steve reached the gangway, but as he attempted to cross it the ship's officers prevented him. "Make way for the passengers, there! Stand back Stand back ? " _ . .</p>
        <p>He was pushed aside, and had perforce to wait until the bulk of the passengers had come ashore.</p>
        <p>At last he was allowed to go forward. "Miss Rayburn?" he cried, acco.ting the first steward he met. "Can you tell me where she is?" The man, who happened to be a third-class stinvard, stared at his excited interrogator in</p>
        <p>amusement. "Eh? I don't know; / don't know all the women aboard. Besides, most of 'em are off now*, 1 don't know where she is." Then, as a coifl wee slipped into his hand: "You'd better ask the purser, or the chief steward, sir. Here I Stop 1" as Steve was moving away. "What name did you say?" "Rayburn." "Oh, yes; come to think of it, she is aboard —or was. I remember now, she was, in our part, and shifted into the first. You'd better see the purser, sir." After many inquiries, Steve found the official. c4.3iiss Rayburn? Yes, she's aboard. Is your name Hart—Stephen Hart?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir." - "This note is for you—from Miss Rayburn." ' Steve tremblingly tore open the missive, and, ag he grasped the meaning of the cruel words, an agonised cry burst from him. "Curse her Curse her!" he cried, aloud, then, rushing like a drunken man, he stumbled along tho halloway muttering to himself. • "Guess that fellow's been touched with the beat,g observed the third-class steward, as he watched the tottering figure re-cross the gangway. A woman's shriek, shrill and penetrating, rent the air,. and as the steward glanced along the decl he saw Ella Rayburn swoon into the arms of Ralph _ _</p>
        <p>Percival. "Another touched with the heat, eh? Ah That's the girl, Miss Rayburn, who was in our part. That's her the Johnny wanted just now. Ah see through it. The old tale. She's cast off the old love and took on with the new. Regular woman's game, that. They all do it on this journey. Well, it's all in the voyage 1"</p>
        <p>Through the streets of Capetown the broken" hearted man wandered aimlessly all day. He Ic3! like one demented. The Kaffirs and other coloured folk who saw him, grinned expansively and tapped their foreheads knowingly, as if they knew wha,t ailed the frenzied "bans."</p>
        <p>He was heedless of the jibes and jeers of black folks and white alike. He heard nothing, he saw nothing, only a sheet of white paper and a black scrawl- straggled over it—a very black scrawl, a horrible scrawl! This flashed before his 'eyes all day; in the fierce light of the sun he saw it, pun even at night,' when darkness was over all, that horrible piece of paper danced diabolically befora his jaded eyes. Far away up the TSloof-road he wandered, and to the slopes of Lion's Head, where grew the silver trees with their pretty glistening white leaves. "Those pretty silver leaves," he murmured. plucked some for her less than two months ago, and sent them in my letter. And now she sends Die this leaf; this letter. 0 God I 0 God! It is too cruel ! "</p>
        <p>. That night he slept amongst the trees at the fool; of Table Mountain. It was cool and pleasant oot there, and he felt that there was comfort in that mighty mountain which had reared its giant forra aloft through the centuries. "The eternal mountain I " he cried, as he looked tip the rugged steeps in the bright clear light of morn. "The eternal mountain! Changeless I Changeless! Men may come, men may go; womea may be false; hearts may be broken; lives may be cursed; all may be vanity, falseness, but the eternal mountain is changeless 1" Slowly, yet with deliberate steps, he walked into the town, his eyes ever turning aside to the right and sweeping the broad, serrated face of the giant Table. "Yes, changeless!" he mused. "As I also will, be; God witness it! I, too will be changeless• Though she has been false, though she has rivets my heart in twain, I'll be true to her, I'll be changeless as the mountain! Yesterday icursed, her; to-day I say God forgive her ! " It was yet early. The Malay hawkers, fish all milk dealers, were driving townwards in their cartt ever and anon tootling on their blatant Freaal horns to apprise customers of their approach. As Steve turned into the avenue of oak tree% skirting the park, a clock in an adjacent tower was striking the hour of eight. He was yell hungry; not a morsel of food had he eaten for twenty-four hours. At the bottom of the avenue he paused a moment, then walked towards 'Dein. street. He reflected that if he went to the boarding house now, the other boarders would be at break'; fast. He did not wish to encounter any of thenv or be subjected to any questioning by any of the folks at the house. He would go there later, pal kis bill, and fetch his things away. He entered a confectioner's shop and bought a few small buns and cakes which he ate ravenousbri•l Then he passed into the street again, and sauntered towards a little square. Here, seated beneath huge tree, was an old nigger who purveyed fruit', Steve purchased a bunch of grapes, and as the 00, was now oppressively hot, he proceeded to the shadt park. _ - - -</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
