        <p>The proposed Small-pox Hospital at Sankey Objection by Councillor Tinnion. Ths Health Comtnitt,e held a meeting on April 4th. 4t WaS resolved "That the Chairmam and Medical Officer Health be appointed to attend the Congress of the .11e3tal Institute of Public Health to be held in Aberdeen al August next." The Town Clerk having reported that fri• Hutton Wright had agreed to sell the farm at San- isq Consisting of about U acres of land with the prem- thereon it was resolved " That, subject to the L'OProval of the Local Government Board the premis, s Purchased at the price named" and" That a cplic ,tion 4":11 raade to the Local Gov, rninent Board for their sane- "nta to the above putchase." On April 186 a further ttleeking was held, when it was resolved that the tender 'lf Mr. Lewis Hill, of Liverpool, to provide and fix fire 4DOllances at the fever hospital fer the sum of £4B be o42CePtPd• subject to the couplings bring permanently annected with the hydrant ; that the Borough Surveyor • tain tenders for the painting and cleaning of the Hos- that the terms provisionally arranged by Mr. • illiatn Owen for the purchase of the land adjoining the °aPital be agreed to. ...Alderman SUTTON propoced tho confirmation of the -"lVltes. and tittle lltltyoß Fefyincied. ,douncillor BENNRTT asked the chairman of the COM- 4littee far some information with regard to the proposed ItZall-pox hospital. He had no particular knowledge of Particular farm, and they should know something bav,4ll„t, it, particularly in regard to isolation, and the pro- aiFutY of it e continuanee in the future. There were vv- Jections from many quarters to the old site, and he eglid like to be certain that there would not be similar "nations to this one. ...rho MAYOR : You may leave that to the Local Govern- :neat Board. They will sea that the conditions neees- lrY to a glace of this kind are properlv complied with. eiddernaan SUTTON said it did not matter wherever they w,:e,tel a piece ef laud thero was some objec-ion. It pr7uld ho perfecaly impoaaihle to oelect land for a small- bemPital without some objections such g wee n'brred tie by Councillor Bennett. They had nu,rerous to3ertione to t'e old intended site. The committee ?tiiciercd it was rot a suitable place, and decided that tiu_enevar they had an opportunity of purnhasing a better W, of land they would do so. They all knew the eorn- biatt" bad been trying net only for weeks and tYlettil9 .4„ctually fnr years to obtain a piece nf snit:Vele land. noraaerr reavieat the Committea and their offici- Is ear,-:ed that 'hie was a most imitable sire, and he lodsred himself that the were obtaining it at a very ha re. As the ',solution st +red, they would have to th,, ° the saaction of the Local Government Boorcl before clule sscure it. When that c0,..5 done they would thr:l3re Plans, and everything wontd be gone into before la. agr, ed to what the Committer., proposed to it wa:" regarrled the iaolation of the land, he thought DißiatVl mur h isolated a a it was passible to obtain any land in Lancoshire 4r Cheshire within a reason- mat Of ail, town. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>th HARPF_N. THE APPETITE PI -6',L01n.4, the neer awl •ronlf. fol dize.tive " Thcy make el,erthing. taste abeltlist:)ne a dose, leo doses In pocktt vial Is of Qqoe, " ; or, by past of Messrs. NEITBICIIT, 27. Charterhouse %e London,</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT : What about the old land? Alderman SUTTON: That belongs to the Water Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: We only rented it. Cuencaler Tewerfox asked if the whole matter wonldbe seeled and signed if they passed this resolution? The MAYOR: It will be purchased provided we obtain the sanction of the Local Govereenent Board. Councillor TINNION : Does it Come forward again for confirm ti, n before the Council. The MAYOR': If the Council pass this reiolution and the Lecal Government Board approve the site the matter is at an end. Ceunaillor TiwerfoN said he felt bound to move that th( reenlution be not oonfirtned. He most take this as the only opportunity that would present itself to eav on be- half of his constituents that this was not a stitable or oesirable site. It would be a great mistake to put an hospital on tha west side of the town, because the pre- vailing wind came from there, and he believed the che- eese could be car, Wel. He could not give an rxoert epinien, but repretonted the feelings of some of his con- stituents in the matter. There was no seconder to the amendment which fell to the ground. Ald. mean SIITT')N said he did not expect anyone to 89cond the resolution. Councillor Tinnion had tad them that he spoke for hi; constituents. How many con- stituents did he represent at Sankey ?</p>
        <p>Councillor Toiler EVANS : Three miles away. I Alderman Surreli : Then he gave his reeson that the land was undesirable, although he said he knew nothing at all about it ef his own knowledge. (Laughter.) Couneiller TeNereox . I said nothing of the sort. Alderman SUTTON: I understood that. Councillor TINNION: I said I had no expert knowledge . which would lead me te come to a decision. I don't suppose the alderman had any either. (Laughter.) Alderman'SoTroer said that all he could say about that was that if he had no keowledge he ought to have eome —(heir, hea.r)—end if necessary perhaps he could ee- lighten one or two of them es to whether he knew any- thing about a proper site fora hospital or not. The Com- mittee had had so many difEcultiee in selecting a site, and it was surprising that when they did select one nehich wee likely to answer all their requirements they had semeene objecting although confessing in a rhe'iNUTO to knowing nothing at all about it. (Leugh;er.) He (Alderman Sutton), knew the land well, and it was the most suitable place that ceuid possibly be had. He 'earned from the most experienced authorities in the kbeedorn that a hospital need not be so isolated as many people thought. Competent authorities field it was net necassery at all in order to prevent the spread of the disease to have the hospital isolated at all infiarnneh as it depended upon the construction of the building whether the disease spread. There would he complaints no matter where they selected a site. He was surprised the.; anyone should come there and object while at the council same time confessing that he knew nothing at all about it. (Laughter.) The MAYOR said it was quite true that the committee recommended the previous site sanctioned by the Coun ci!, but it was simply because it was the only site they could possibly get. The present sire fulfilled the com- mittee's requirements. Ile wished, hoover, to say that the committee did not think the price a low one. Alderman SUTTON: I did not say they did. I said I considered it was. The MAYOR : The majority of the committee are of a contrary opinion. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were thee confirmel. Sanitary Works Committee. - -</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee hold on April 4th, it was resolved That the tender of Mentrs Joseph Adam- son and Co. for new dryer at £430 to accepted." A. meeting wee also held on April 18-h, when it was ro riolved "That the Chairman or Deputy-Chairman and Mr. Dees be appointed to attend the) Conroe of the Reval Institute of Public Health to be brld in Aberdeen in August next." The minutes were confirmed on the 'notion of Coun- cillor MONKS, seconded by Councillor Joins EVANS.</p>
