        <head>COMPLAINT AGAINST THE BOROUGH SURVEYOR.</head>
        <p>COMPLAINT AGAINST THE BOROUGH SURVEYOR.</p>
        <p>Alleged Disregard of instructions.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Paving and Sewerage aonarnittem were proposed for adoption by Councillor Heaton, and seconded by Councillor Smethurst. Councillor MONKS said he moved an ameniment in re cire to brine a Certain matter before the notice of the Council, andlie was a little in:doubt as to whether this was the proper time, but the Town Clerk advised him to bring it forward mew rather than later. The metter had reference to the proceedings of the Rivers Cemmittee but had also reference to the Paving and Seweeseen Committee 'locative it was included in the payment of accounts. He was sorry the Borough Survever was not present, inasmuch as the matter referred to him, and if he had known he would not have been present he would have written him and told him that he intended to bring it forward. Some time ago a man named Greenwood was employed to take the level of the tides at Think Quay, and a!! a meeting of the Rivers Comnsittee an March Mb it was decidei that the man's services qbnui,l be dispeneed with. The Borough Surveyor was present at the time,and althourth no resolution seemed to have been entered on the minutes of the committee it Was distinctly underetood that the services of the man should be dispensed with at once. He (Councillor Monks) noticed that the man wag till doing the work en the leth of April. 30 detel after, and h. epoke to the Town Clerk, but nothing was done u-itil the following Tuesday, Apr'l 211.`h when the man eyes still engterd on the work. Be (Councillor Menlo!) celled at the Borough Treasurer's office to eet some in- ferme" ion. le order to get it be had oeeesion to go to the Borough fierveyor'e office, with the result that the man was stopped the following day. April 25th. It did not Seem a proper thing that definite instruntione elven by a committee should be ignored be eny of the efileiels, and he felt bound to bring this matter to the notice of the Council. The amount was small nevi I9nt worth tiltr- ine about hut heerished to raise the question of nrincinle as to how far an official of the Corporation was :liertified in ignoring definite inetruclone given by any cernneit..ep such as were given in this partieular else. Re Proposed an Amendment to the effect that the accounts he passed with the exerption of the wages of thie particular man. Aldermen Kline-Loewe see.onrieti. Cour ill e....0r SMETHURST PAW the Betrctieh Surveyor had hen away from drttv a fortnight or three weeks, and tbat might account for the man net twing stepped at the proper time. It was hardly fair to the man to stop his wee.; s. Mclennan SUTTON said it would be scarcely fir to nese the amendment without having en exelenetion from the Beroeet Serveeor. The Surveyor might: have an explanation to give. At all events the eommitt ei nfelat receive from hies the reaeon the order ef the Itivere Committee was not attended to, amid if that were net setiefeetory then would be the time to let him mi- lderetarld that such conduct could not ho allowed Uri- t9 that explanation had been given it would not he fair 1 to nags the amendment. 1 Alderman Boe:roN said this wRI at th 2 pnwr time to thrash the nea'ter out. The man wee a servant and ' had earned his waere. and eceoreling to law he cenld Iclaim them. The Corpereioe were liable to him for them. The matter should be settled in committee. Ho supported the minutes.</p>
