        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 4, 1900.</p>
        <p>Court&amp;llor HEATON : lam rather at a loss. There is ',within!z on the minutes. Toe MAYOR : It is included en the accounts. Councillor lIRAION . We have us information, and if the men was appointed by the Rivers Commit- hee he was entirely under the control of the Borough Surveyor. The MAYOR said the man was employed by the Faviag and Sewerage Committee and under the direction of the eeroueh Serveyor did work for the Rivers Committee tipon which they set very little value. The qeestion raised by Councillor Moeks was not of money but of Irineiplo, and was whether the instructions of a committee were to be set aside by an official of the 7orperatien. Councillor HEATON : Should not that be hrought up n the proceedings of the Rivers Committee ? 1,,- MAYOR: No. He was not in the servlc3 of that Committee. eecerticelor HneTON ilid [la lute that there was anything for his committee to euswer: The MAYOR: This amendment is not a reflection upon the committee at all. Aldermaa BOLTON : We cannot get the money hack. Alderman HUTCHINSON : If the Rivers Committee have nothing to do with the man, why did they order his discherge ? Alderrnao SUTTON : Because he had been doing some work fer the committee under the instructions of the Borough Surveyor. Mclennan I-TUTCHINSON : He was not a servant of the , Committee. Why should they interfere ? Councillor TINNION said if they expected the Boroagh Surveyor to pay he would not agree to that. He did not care for the way the matter had been brought forward. If it was of such special importance the proper and better way to brine it forward would have been by some reso- lution. He failed to see that tle matter of principle was SO very greet. If they were making a surcharge on the Committee, the Borough Treasurer, or Surveyor, it woe a very unfair way of bringine it forward. It might have been brought forward without rendering any official of the Corporation liable to a surcharge. He wi,hed to know if it had been definitely stated te the Surveyor. The MAYOR : 17Ps it had. Councillor TINNION said he was prepared to support the principle of the thing, but he though" the matter at the present time might be dropped, and that Councillor Menks rnielat rest satisfied with the Council agreeing with him on the matter of priseiple. Councillor HEATON said be would be glad if Councillor Monks could see his way to postpone the matter to the next Council. It had been sprune on the committee., and in a motath's time it could probably be explained. Alderman FRANCOMB sake i whether Mr. Longriin had received instructions to bring the man b-fore the raving and Sewerage Committee, and whether he was brought testere that committee. If Mr. Lengdin made complaints th, y were the people to discharge tbis man and not an Ceeneillor F. R. Reimers said it was clear the Rivers Committee had no right to diacherge this man and it was doubtful whether ths Surveyor would have been justified in acting on their itirtructions. Be was certain also that they had no right to surcharge the Boroueu Surveyor. It would be I very illegal and improper pro- ceeding. This, was neither the proper time nor proper way to carry out this principle. The MAYOR, as cheirm ru of the Riven; Committee, said when tire question wag raised hey found they were paying for work done for them by a man they bad sever eneaged at all. If they had had choice or selection he would never have been in the service of the committee, and beceuse of the manner in which his work was done —he had to watch the sewers to see there was no de- posit of any solid material, and also to wetela the tides —they would never kve eneweed him. He was an eld servant of the Paving end Sesverage Committee and h d been put on by Mr. Lonedin. Very important litigation --might follow in connection with his work, and it was of rho utmost importance that their witnosser should not enly be reliable. but else men of ability. Mr. Long:din told Mr Monks the man had fiuished his work for the P‘ering and Sewerage Committee, and they asked that his services for them might terminate at the same time. ele was sore y the matter had been put forward in Mr. Lenge:it-es absence, and he thought it would be wall if Counailler Mottles would withdraw the amendment. Councillor Motexs, in explanation of the course he bad taken, said he brought the matter up because he would otherwise have lost the opportunity of doing an. It it had been the first case against the Surveyor he would not have thought so much about it, but there were two or three other casts Of a similar character. Ha was Trill- ing to withdrew the amendment. He would bring it forward again by special resolution. Alderman Bs:lemon Any complaints should go to the General Purposes Committee. Alderman FAIRCLOUGH : This ix not the first time this has happened, and when a man hen done wrongly he ought to be brought over the coals. Alderman SUTTON : Quite right. Councillor BENNETT. referring to the reference to Howley Brook, asked when it would be covered up alto- gether ? Ceoueillor HEA.TON said it would ultimately be covered up, and was being done as necessity arose. The minutes were approved. Museum, Library, Arts, and Technical Instruction Committee.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee a letter was road from ths Warringten Fielo Club thanking the Committee and applying for leave to meet at the Ilusmrn as before, during the coming year, and it was resolve.' that the application be granted; It was reselved " Iliac the tender of Mr. S Mose to supply and fix a new boiler at the Museum at a cost of £4l 104. be accepted." An est'mate of receipts and expenditure on the Museum Amount was submitted and approYed. Os the motion et the Maxon, sscoodsd by Councillor GREENING, the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. Monthly Meeting. The monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council wag held at the Waggon and Horses Inn Buttermarket-Areet, on Toes ay evening. THE LIGHTING OF VERICLES. At the last meeting of the Council a letter eras read from the Lancashire Federation of the Trads and Labour Councils drawing attention to an injustice under which the carters of the county suffer in respect to a byelaw of the County Council referring to lights upen vehicles. Carters desirous of carting out the byelew, and having fell regard to the safety of the public had been penalised for travelling without a lighathe &amp;been°e of which bad been frequently °awed through the Nulty character of the lamp, or the inferior quality of the oil supplied. Under these circumstances the following resolution was p l,sed with a recriest that each council put themselves into communication with their local representatives upon the Couety Council and endeavour to bring about a change : "That the County Council of Lancashire be respectfully requestel to consider the advisability of adopting lamp' of uniform and suitable pattern to be used by carters on their vehieles during the hours gaited in the County Council Bye-Law. Under the cenditiceis which obtain at present, carters are often puni;lied for travelling without a light, the extinguishing of which has been frequently caused by the faulty construction or dam- aged condition of the lamp, or the inferior quality of the oil supplied." Toe Seneetary (tdr. William Bullock) was instructed to write to the representatives of the town on the County Council. Messrs. F tdonks, W. Pierpoiat and Ad 'mean Froe comb. The SECRETARY read the replies which he had received Mr. Monks said that the matter lay in the hands of the committee who had charge of theirs matters, and their beat plan yi ould be to approach the committee itself and lay before the members the erieva.nce to weicit carters are subj,et. It weuld effect both the Lancashire and Cheshire C linty Councils, as Warrington carters were affected by lath. Perhaps a letter stating their views sad lea ha the Clerk of the Peace for both counties might have some effect.—Mr W Pierpoint's opinion was that while the Lancashire County Council had power to enforce their bye-law as to the lighteog of the vehicles, toe description of light to be used most he left to the owner of the vehicles.—Alderman Franeornb said the Ceuncil might rely upon his bringing the matter be. for the County Authorities upon the earliest Occasion. HOUSING OF THE POOR. Mr. Brown and Mr. Battereby attendee. a Conference held in Manchester with reference to tee above ques- tion. Mr. Brooms related what took place at the meeting,and at the close of his Tomer km, announced with regret that he was leaving the district. A hearty vo'e of thanks was accorded to Mr. Brewpt for the services he has rendered to the Council during the time he has lived in Warrington. THE DISCUSSION ON CONSCRIPTION.—MR. BATTERSEY'S REMARKS. The SECRETARY read a letter which he had received from the LincAn Trades and Labour Council thanking the 13 members of the Warrington Council who voted in OuPilert of the step which they as a Council had taken re conscription. From the report of the meeting on February 6th which appearel la the " Examiner" they found that Mr. Battersby in supporting the motion that the circular oe conscription received from the Lincoln Trades and Labour Courson should lie so the tablesfirst charged the Lincoln Cermoil with being more energetic in political questions than trade ques- tions, secondly,he said that they could not send a penny to assist the filesmiths when on strike, and thirdly, that as &amp; council they had taken no active part in trade Union principles. Front the subsequent remarks of Mr. rattersby they assumed that he Was a delegate from the filesmiths' society and that being so he oeteld not have much knowledge of the business of that society or he would have known that the Lincoln. Council had made a grant of 10s to assist the fileamiths when on strike on their second appeal and would have dine so the first time had the Council been in a position to have done so In reference to politics, the Council recognised no politics but labour. The past record of the Council would prove that they had always been to the front to help their fellow trade unionists in distress The letter went on to ray that the Council con- tributed 334 lie I.d to assist the Yorkshire and Derty- shire miners when on strike in 1894 and during the engineers' lock out they colleeted a runt of £163 4; 4d. also during the engineers' dispute on the Clyde they col looted £34. The above they theuekt would prove that Mr. Battersby was most cruelly unjust in his statements. It was a libel en the good name of the Council and they asked the Council to give the letter publicity and ask Mr. Battereby to correct his statements. Mr. BATTERSBY said he regretted that he made the mistake of saying that the Lincoln Council had not con- tributed anything to the filesmitlas, but so far as the other portions of his remarks were cencereed, he kept to them in their entirety. The Council spent money in taking uo the question, and it was more for a political purpoce than a trades unionist purpose. The latter por- tion of the letter said he accused them of doing nothing for trades unionises. That was a deliberate falsehood. It would have been folly to accuse them of doing nathing for trades enionism generally, when he did not knee. what they had done for Derbyshire or any ahere else. It was evident they had done more for Derbyshire than for Warrington, when they contributed thee hundred pounds for them and 106 for Warrington. Be Was speak- ing of the filesmiths only, and not of what action the Council had taken as regarded other sesieties. He regret- ted making the mistake with regard to the 10$. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Cemetery Committee</head>
        <p>Cemetery Committee</p>
        <p>I meeting of the Cemetery Committee was held on April 23, when the report of the Regi ztrar was read, from which it appeared that the interments for the past five weeks were—Adults, 63 ; miners, 46 ; stillborn and premature, 11.. Corresponding period last year—Adults 57 ; minors, T 2 ; stillborn and premature, 16. I was resolved " That Councillors J 10209 Evans and John Wil- kinson be appointed rnekute,s of this committee, and that the tender ef Messrs. Alfred Grindrod and Co. to supply and fix high-pressure hot • water heating :appara- tus to the three cNapals for £56 be accepted." The annual report of the Registrar which appeared in Satur- day's" Examiner " was read. The miautes wore passed. on the motion nf Councillor Join; Evixs, seconded by Councillor WILKINSON.</p>
        <p>Town Hall, Park and Baths Corrsrnittee. Inopportune Painting of the Baths. At a meeting of this eomt-nitt?e the revort of the bath- kever was read, from which it appeared that the in- come from the baths for the past two weeks was £5 13s 41, and for the corresponding periocl last year £ll 12s 21; decrease,2s 184 103. At a further meeting, held ,an April 15th, it was resolved " That at the request of the Board of Management of the Infirmary the commit- tee undertake to provide shrubs for, and keep in order, the small garden at the Infirmary." Anather meeting was held on April 23rd, when it was resolved "That the render of Messrs. Itand C.Chandley to paint the Walker fountain,accerding to specification, at £6l,be acoepted." It W4S also resolved " That the tender of Mr. William Fletcher to paint the baths at £49 las be accepted." Alderman BOLTON moved the passing of the minute. Councillor SMETHURST seconded, and the motion was carried. Cluncil!or JAX3S EVANS asked whether the present was the most oppcxtu.ne for the painting of the baths. They had been closed daring the winter, and now they were opened, tlr.ty were being painted. He thought it waq a most inopportune time, and that there would be firmneial loss. Alderman BOLTON' explained that they had instructed the Surveyor to get some painters to it in January. mad to have it done by day work ; no painters,hov.;everecould be got,and they had to advertise for tenders, which had all taken time. They hsd berm as sharp as they could</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Rivers Committee.</head>
        <p>Rivers Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this committee was heid on April 23rd, tow). the Acting Conservator of the Mersey reported that he had sanctioned the construction of a wha:i at Bank Quay for the S. Relfns C6,b10 COmpiny, Limited. Mr. R. 1. Fairc,lough, the representative of the Corpor- ation on the Upper Mersey Commigtion, reported that the charts prodoced at the meetia Fhowed no change in the main ch..2nt,is of the river het%een Warrington and Runcorti,hut that below Runcore they had changed con- aiderab, now ?running about halt a mile nearer the sore at Male Head. The Mayoa moved, and Ccuec7,llor nEATON scconded, the adoption of t minutrs. The motion was earried.</p>
