        <p>THE LATE ALDERMAN WRIGHT. THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Tile grave closed over the remains of the late Alderman John fildward Wright, J.P., on Saturday</p>
        <p>Seldom, if ever have the ob,equiss of a townsman been attended with such pronounced respect aryl sympathy as W 414 the case when Alderman Wright's remains were interred in the Warrington Cemetery. The route to the Cetnetry was lined with spectators and the funeral procession which must have been about a quarter of a mile in length and included over forty carriages, presentel a deeply impressive appearance as it slowly wended its way through the streets. Tee deceased gentleman touched the aubhic life of the town in so many of its aspects that it WAS not surprising to find a large number of gentlemen representing various bodies present. The Mayor and Corporation and officials commenced to asserede at the Town Hall, where carriages were in waiting, _ _ .</p>
        <p>shortly after 11 o'clock and forming into proc,s- sional order proceed down the we6t3rn drive along Arplev-,treet to the late residence of the deceased in Wilson Patten-street where the hearse</p>
        <p>and the funeral party joined in the procession which proceeded along 80ld-street, Sankey-street, Horse- market-street, Church-street to the Paris() Church</p>
        <p>where the fret portion of the burial service was con-</p>
        <p>ducted in the presence of a large congregation. At the close the procession was reformed amt proceeded to the Cemetery where the latter portion of the ser- vice was gone through. A lar,•e nusabtr responded to the invitation of ttp. 111</p>
        <p>_4yor to attend the funeral.Arnongst those present were the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), the Roc? John Yonge (liayor's chadlain),Al&amp;rman Francomh, Alderman Fair- clough, APerman Sutton,Alderman Hutchinson, Coun- cillors Shaw (ire, rt,Heaton,hllison.Tionion,Davies,B4i A J Jolley, James Evans, A Jolley, Jlbu Evans._ F_ R</p>
        <p>Roberts, Lc.i, John Roberts, Benn-ait, Sinethurat,Mookl, Rogers, Shillcoca, Pierpoint. Peacock and Duncta ; Mr</p>
        <p>_ . . J Lyon Whi to (tewn clerk), Mr S Wilson (deputy town cler-k), Mr J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr G F F White (mcgistrates' clerk), Mr Luke Talbot (chief cnnetable), Mr J Sutcliffe (assistant purveyor) Mr Madelpy (ibratian), Mr W S Haddock (gas engineer), Mr J Dees (water engineer), Mr J Kinsley (gymnasium in,tructor), Mr W S Howard (g4B department), Mr R Norman (nuisance ini.pector) and Messrs H P Brierley, J Booth, J Smith and W Woods (borough. surveyor's _ . _</p>
        <p>department) ; Messrs. R T Faircl ' ough Armitage, T Birchal!, W Uayward, and S Wright (inu4istratea) ; Messrs. W Ricitardsr.,n and Char'es Leigh (representing the Warrington Crick4t and Bowling Club), and Wesrs. T Mac, J Andrews, M Knowlei, J C Thompson, C Wood, T tinilgeth, J F Ferguson. J C Lnwles., W Winatanley. W Jeffrey, J Strange, W Nrtington, E Remo, J Gandy, .1 Titchett, J Iddrin. W W We3twell, S B Stinger, G Wallington, J Dixon, and E S Garland ; Th. Revs Fl V Fleet, J $ Eiughes, W H Drummond, and _ _ _ . .</p>
        <p>H G Rnberta, Dr White, Dr Braide and Dr Smith; Messrs J Potter, 0 Silverstone, J Bolton, W Stout, R Yong, W Honghton, Sereeent Pricea W Fowler, W D Jeaus, J Fleerrinee Taylor, R R Pearson, G Wright, W Diegle, ii Watkins, Jscele Bailey, Dr N ;trill (representing the C )a- serval lye Club); W Rol braes, I B Taylor, George Jolley, Captain Waring, W H J Jenkins, end W W Wsst well (rsprementing the Conservative Association) ; H Hayes (representing the Junior Conrarvative Association and New Club) ; Messrs W T Forehaw and Alexander Neil, Pendleton (representing Messrs Pletcher, Russell and Co.) : Seger Orson, R Curran and T Curran (architects) ; C W Davenpart, T Woodward, and T Davies (repraseuting the Builders' Assoeiation) ; Peter Chriales (represeetieg the Old Men's Dinner and the Aged Wm:eerie Tea Committee). The carriages containing the Mayor. Aldermen, Coun- cillors, and other gentlemen left the Town Hall at twenty minutes past eleven preceded by a bely of police with whom were Chief Inspector Priinips and lespector Rewbotham, a number sof firemen under Suporintendeetr Tamer and a depetetion of workmen from the different depart:mate of the Peying and Sewerage Committee o welch the deceased alderman was chairman. The mem- bers cf the Corporation, polite, and workmen preceded the hearse. Following was a brougham coetainirg the wreaths • Than came the carriages in which were the chief mourners. They were as follows :—Messrs. J Wright. Stanley Wright, Thomas Wright (brother), Thomas Birtles. Frederick Taylor, A Brownei,J E Birtlee. J C Bate, F G Tayler, Wilfrid Taylor, Arthur Taylor, W Gill, John Walton, T Longdin, W A Reid, A laetten, E Clare, C R Burton, J F Jackson, C Puns- ton, Jams Dunn and John Shorrocks (Burnley). At the church gates the coffin was met by the Rev. Canon Willis (rector), the Revs. E J rlifteocke, EC E Carleton, G W Dickenson, and A F White, Messrs. J Dewhurst, J G Crailr, end W Prior) church &amp;Seers, and the choir. The first portion of the service was read by the Rev E 1 Wilcock', and the seemed by the Rev E C E Carleton. Pseliaa sc. and the hymn" Now the labourers task is o'er were sung. The organist (Mr W S Woods) played the "Dead Marsh" as the coffin was borne from the &amp;laurel'. At the cemetery, the choir preceded the hearse along the drive singing a hymn. The service at the graveside was read by the Rector, and as the coffin was lowered into its last resting plane the choir sang a hymn. There was a large number of beeutiful wreaths. A very handsome one was sent" from home," and others were from Mr and Mrs Tfleirtles and fantly. Mr and Mrs Frederick Taylor, Mr J E Birtier, Mr A Brower), Mr J Bate, the Mayor and Maynress,tir and Mrs John Hrsketh Mr and Mrs W Gill,Mr and Mrs John Walton and family, Mr and Mrs C R Burton, Mr W A Reid, Alderman and Mrs Fransoreb, Councillor and Mrs Shaw Green, Cows- °Mar and Mrs Smethurst, Councillor and Mrs William Pierpoint, Councillor and Mrs Baircl,Mrs Goulden (Black- burn), Mrs Nutter (Southpart), Mr and Mrs B Mather (Southport), Mrs and Miss ?Divert (Southport), Mr and Mrs Curran, Mr and Mrs Garside (AM:tan-under- ' Lynn), Mr aid Mrs Longdin, Mr and Mrs Partingtan, Mr and Mrs Ferguson. Mr and Mrs Forshaw. Mr and Mrs Mainwaring, Mr and Mrs Samuel Taylor, Mr and Mrs Stanley Wil ' sou Mr and Mrs Ralph Jobnems, Mr and Mrs William Riceard4on,Mr and Mrs James Richardson, Alderman and Mrs Suttee, Mrs Pickesere, Alderman Bolton, Mrs W Fairchough, Mrs C B Aylward, Mrs Kiernan, Miss Gaskell, Mrs Reed and family, Mrs Page, Mr Joseph Appleton, Mr Joseph R Williams, Mrs Fleming anti family, Mr Themse Cowden, Mr It Garnett (St scktou Heath), Messrs W and S Owen, Messrs Dunn, Jackson, Plinsten and Hannah, Messrs Cackett and Burns. Dick and C A Hindle, the Gas Ds- partment officials. Conservative Club Committee, Parish Church Council, members of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club, Warrington arid District Master Builders' Association, Old Church Choristers' Association, the Commi tee of the Old People's Treat, " Elizabeth and Rachel " (47, Wilson Patten-street), and " Minnie and Martha" (27, Wilson Patten-street). The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. Ralph Johnsen, Bridge-street. REFERENCES AT THE TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tues- day evening several references were made to tho death of Alderman Wright. The MAYOR MlVNl:—"Th9t this Council receives with deep regret the announcement of the death of John Ede. ard Wright, Esquire, an Alderman and a Jus ice of the Peace, and formerly Mayor of the Boroueb.desiros to record the great loss that the borough has sustained by his death, and to express its appreciation of his long and valuable public services ; and that the Town Clerk be requested to convey to Mrs. Wright and other members of the family the sincere sympathy of the enured with them in their bereavement." Be said: The news of Alder- man Wright's death came upen us all as a great shock, and with a armee Of a deep personal lose. While we knew that for some time past his health bad been failing, we all hoped that he would have tree witn us yet for some time to help us in the goverment of the town to which he Wild so devotedly attached, For myself, I can say with all sincerity that, during the many years I have had intimate, personal, and very consiebrable business relations with hirn,l always found him to he a thee ()trebly capable arid a fair-minded men, kindly dispcsed, invari- ably courteous, and always ready to do a eoed turn to all with whom he was brought into contact His close and devoted attention to the aff sirs of this bereugh was a most pronsrune.ed feature in his public life. From the time he first entered the Council, praetically te the day of his death, he was in full harness and while we deplore with feelings of deep reeret his early decease, his example will in We newt he one which, I trust and believe, will be warmly emulated by those of us who remain. We cannot forget the universal courtesy which charanteriz-d his conduct while he held the pesitina of chief magistrate of our town, and how , gracefully he as sepported in his manifold and onerous mates by his devoted wire. Our sympathy goes out strenely to her in her present and sad bereavement, and we offer to her and to all the members of the faini,y of eur late frieed and colleague our sincere toadolente its their present great sorrow. Alderman SUTTON said he could sympatlfze with everything that the Mayor had said with reference to the less they had sustained in the death of Alderman Wright. As Chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee, and se a member of the S'reet Improver meat Committee, his experience and prectica knowledge of the work in those departments were es, high lied great value, and.it would be a 'ere eifficur matter to ronlaee him or refill his position. His heart was in the work, sad whatever sacrifice it. might tie to him reentOnelly he never failed in his appointments. A; an enieearce of the time he devoted to the work con- riPcted wit 4 the Paving and Sewerage Committee, it was only reeceemary for them to remember the great amount of cfreet-makine and the improvements in t'se paving of their streeta. They would be able to understand the value of his services in that direction alone. ttjh regard to his qualifications and general dispoeition, he (the eneatter) Iran known him very well and intirriateiy the ',not pert ot his life, arid he cerild speak with authority. His beait was filled with symplthy and kiedly disposition</p>
        <p>Ito every one in trouble. That had always been one of the test points in his nature. In was very sad to less the services of sa v.duable a colleague. ad especially that throve loss.s had occurred so frequently as they had deam lately. It was only that evening he was looking at tits photographs in the committez-room adjlining. They were awAre ttnt iii ¶hoe phonographs had bden taken about 13 years ago. Since 1887 tee fouud that they had 03L by or inctpacita..ed from workonore the one had ot the 36 eaembers which coestituted the eJlincil in tat year. They were very serious losses,and a great loss t. th tawn fle secondei the resolution. Councillor HEATON said as a colieague of Mr. Wright's, he could eadorse all that had been said. He was sure his loss was a very great one to the Council and he endeavoured to do all he could on commitct es. He was very nice to the mm iers at all times. Alderman FRANCOMB said that in the late alderman h had lest the deare,t friend he ever had. He had been intimately assoc,ated with him for 25 years, and had never la td an unkild word from 11 IC, He was spite certain the rece'ution would be end,us-!d by everybody. Alderman Wright was kindness itself. During the speskar's late illossi Mr. Wright had never missed a single week in which he had not paid him a visit,and he came to look forward with longing ter his visit, to hear whit WAS going on. He could not say very mueh, as he felt extremely their loss. _ _</p>
        <p>COUUCiIIOr -SMETHURST deSilTd tJ adi his word of con- dolence and support to what had been said. Alderman Wright had been his personal friend since be came to the town, and he had been closely associate i with his (the speaker's) family. sHe could ssmre them he had lost the dearest friend he had ever had. Alderman liurcattrsoN said that he had been associ- ated with Air. Wright for 21 years, in the Council and other matters. His death, so early ha life, was very distressing. The resolution was carried in silence.</p>
      </div>
