      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>CONDOLENCE FROM THE LIBERAL</head>
        <p>CONDOLENCE FROM THE LIBERAL</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Lt the annual meeting or the tielaeral Council of the Warrington Liberal Association on Monday evening, Mr. JOHN GANDY said he rose with cansiderable feeling to make apr position. The people of Warrington had suetairvd a great loss in the death of their Conservative friend, John Eiward Wright. (Hear, hear.) Eie thought it would be a very fi:ting thing for the Liberal Assam- tion of Warrington to send a kindly message of sympathy to his wife and family. (Hear. hear.) It was one of those things that Eaglishinau prided therseives on— that whilst they were partisaas they were always Englishmen and always respected one another whatever "colour" they might be. Alderman Wright had served the town of Warrington a good many years and given a great amount of time, attention ann eervice to its interests, which probably fee, except business men, could appreciate. Ho tad known Mr. Wright for nearly 30 years aud he could truly say that he always found bum a very amiable and kiedly man. (Hear, hear.) He thought his loss was a loss to Llbeials as well as Con- servatives,and he hoped they weuld agree with him that at this, their first meeting, it WAS a fitting thing that they should send his wife and fa:nily a kindly message ot sympathy. Hear, hear.) Mr. Dixoll seeeeded and alladed to his long acquaint- ance with the late Alderman. When he first came to War- rington Mr. Wri4ht was a lil uoy gni gto toe National School and from then to the time of his death there had been an intimacy be: ween then which it was difficult to explain to anyoee. The resolution would convey to his family the deep sympathy they felt for them in the very great less the, bed sustained. (Float, hear.) Mr. T. BIRCHALL (the chairman) acid he could not help adding his testimony to the value and persanal worth of the late Alderman Wright. They had been friends for many year.; and he sli paid not forge: thet some eiht years ago Mr. Wright was the first Conservative magis- trate to congratulate heaa on his s.ccersion to the Beach. Tee motion was carried unanimously, and Mr. Clayton was instructed to cou-; ay the mesaage to hlre.Wright and fatai:y.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PULPIT REFERENCE.</head>
        <p>PULPIT REFERENCE.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning the Recor(the Rev. Canon Willis) made a reference in his somata at the Pariah Church to the death of Aidermm Wright. He said that he had held the highest office in the borough, that of chief magistrate f..r two years in succession, which in itself was a tribute to his merits. He had also been an officer of the Parish Church for many years past,and had taken the deepest interest in all the affairs of the church, the missions and the schools, and had been a vary energetic supporter and liberal con: ributor to the paroehial needs. He had always been distinguished by his great kindness of heart and generosity, and was always desirous of speaking kindly of everyone and doing a good turn to anyone wherever it lay in his pow6r. The largely at- tended and very impressive funeral which took place en Saturday, and the many tokens of affection presented in the shape of wroth,lz., all testified ta the respect and affection entertained for the late Alderman. la accord mace wittl the beautiful hymn they sang at the service, they lett him, their brother, in the hands of God, who was just and merciful, and who knew that none of them were perfect and all had need to examine their own hearts and strive to be prepared for the call which must come seaner or later ti all of tom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>AMATEUR DRAMATICS AT LATCH FORD.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR DRAMATICS AT LATCH FORD.</p>
        <p>The needs of Christ Church, Latchford, which is to be enlarged, were met to sore extent last Thursday evening by the proceeds of a largely at- tended amateur dramatic and muaical performance in the Parish Room. A number of ladies and gentle- men connected with the church had organised the scheme and their efforts were attended with com- plete success. The programme consisted principally of the duologue" A Pair of Lunatics," and the farce " A Little Sentinel." here were musical items in addition however, songs by Miss Pierpeint, Mr. Gilbert Carter, the Rev F A Screeton, a pianoforte wet by the Misses Fairclough, and a humorous selection from str 11 M Ashton being much appre- ciated. " A Pair of Lunatics," Miss Bessie Pier- point and Mr CII Bond had decidedly trying ' parts the difficulties of assuming madness being considerab'e. Both came through the ordeal creditably however, the principal fault being lowness of articulation This was, we believe, Miss B Pierpoiut's debut in amateur dramatics and she is to be complimented on her self-possessed acting. The farce "A Little :Sentinel " was a more preten- tious piece, the cast being as follows :—Mr Whoa- dletou Coaxer (an elderly lady killer), Mr A Lewis; Captain Clarence Courtington, tilr C II Bond ; Sim (a young farmer), Mr Harry Mosley; betty Haw- thorne (proprietress of a farm), Miss A M Bond May (her friend, " The Little Sentinel "), Miss lst; Pierpoint. A really excellent interpretation of the piece was Riven, the whole thing going with a swing and naturalness which highly delightei the audience. There was little to chooe between the artistes, all did sio well,but the greatest praise must be given to Miss A M Bond and Mr Harry Mosley who displayed considerable histrionic ability in their representation of the parts entrusted to them. Miss M; Pierpoint, too, was never at fault, and her vivacious rendering of the title role was particularly pleasing. She had plenty told° in keeping off the amorous" butterflies" who hovered round betty Hawthorne, but she acted the part of " sentinel " with the most praiseworthy determination. Messrs. Bond and Lewis impersona- ted the " butterflies " capitally, the gallant captain of" Dvsarsous " being, we hone for the sake of the regiment, more than true to life. It was all very good and very amusing and the large audience pre- sent had spent an enjoyablo, evening when votes of thanks and the ~,trains of the `• National Anthem " closed the proceedings. We should acid that Mrs C. J. Holmes acted efficiently as pianist, and Mr Frank Proud filled the office of stage manager,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>School Board Election_ at _Whitley,</head>
        <p>School Board Election_ at _Whitley,</p>
        <p>The election of a chool Board for v.ithitley United District, which took place last Thursday, excited considerable interest. Five members were required and eight candidates presented themselves. Mr W B Hough acted as deputy-returning officer for Mr George F Ashton (clerk to the Runcorn Board of Guardians, and he was ably asfisted as poll-clerk by Mr Albert Hulse. (RuncJrn). The poll was considered a heavy ono. about five-sixths of the electorate recording their voters The counting was smartly conducted and the result made known at about 830 as fellows :---11enry Nield, 151 ; Charlesl Rumlney, 8G; Alfred Barker GG ; Charles Moun- field, SG; John Henry Clarke, 50 ' • Samuel Lewis, 33 ; William Fle:.cher. 27 ; Baton Bretheroti, 25 The first five were declared duly elacted.—Afrer the declaration a vote of thanks was moved to the deputy-returning officer for the admirable manner in which the election had been conducted, on the propositiln of Mr W Fletcher, secwnded by Mr Charles E Rumlney. This was suitably acknow- ledged.</p>
