        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF THE GENERAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pierpoint Resigns Officer</p>
        <p>rue annual meeting of the General Council of the Warrington Liboral As&amp;,eiatiou was held on Monday evening at the Liberal club. Mr. T Birahall J.F., pre- sided and among those present were Mr. Furness (hon. tleasurer), Mr. Clayton (secretary) ; Councillors Tinnion Wilkinson and James Evaus, Messrs. J Gandy, Haley,W Wicatanley, E Plinston, S Dunbobbin, T B Malkin, W Ford, Hill, and Wollenden.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said nad to apologise for the absence of their esteemed president, Mr. John Crosfield, who was confined to his house by illness, Mr. A. H. Crosfield was also unable to be present as he was in Franco and would probably remain there for two or three weeks. Councillor Shaw Green had also been seriously ill and was doing as little work as possible. Their first business that night was the election of President and he had great pleasure in a-:aits proposing Bar. John Crosfield. The Crosfields, as they knew, had always been the backbone of Liberalism in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) He could remember when he was a lad, Mr. John Crt.sfield's lather, who was one of the few sturdy Liberals in tho borough. The whole elestot ate then did not number 300, of whieh the speaker's father I happened to be ode. He often heard " old Joe Crosfield" spoken of as a staunco Lineral, who always stood up for the right. Mr. JOHN GANDY seconded, and the motion was sari tied unanimously.</p>
        <p>THE VICA-PANSIDEMPS.-RBTIRIMBNT OP MR. PIERPOINT.</p>
        <p>Mr. MALKIN then tonvesl Lae ra-e'eotion of vice-prezi.. dents, with the exQeption of Councillor Pierpoiat, who resigned ofLle, after 35 years of active service. Tne ()Jamaican explained that Mr. Plerpoiat was as good a Liberal as ever he was, lou:; he thought he could be of as much service to the party without holding office.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION seeonded, and while he regretted the notion Mr. Pierpoint had taken. he did not think it would it any way afect 13'.s position in the party, or leseen kis keen interest in its workings. He proposed ttiat Mr. T. &amp;rebell be elected a vice-president in the place of Mr. Pierpoint. -</p>
        <p>Mr. name seconded, and the election of vice. prestdents was confirmed, Mr. Birchall returning thank.</p>
        <p>lON. TRIASIIHSR AND HON. SICHETANY.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Pattie... 3 and the Mayor, ware unanim- onsly re-elected hon. treaeurer and hon. secretary res- pectively. Mr. Forams in reiponae remarked that the outlook of the party financially Wai mush pleasanter than it had been for years. He also alluded so the recent revision, and said they came out much better than is the previous year. Tatty might compliment themselves on being in a uniqtie position. There had been a complete revision ef the whole town. All the removals had been got out, and at any moment they were in a position to take up the cudgels and engage in a Parliamentary contest. (tp- pla.use.) This reflected great credit upon the Secretary Mr. Clayton. (Hear, hear.) Mr. HIIGKES thought an election was not tfar distant, and it was time the organisatien of workers in the wards was attended to.</p>
        <p>A. desultory discuesion on Use prospects of an election followed, several speakers impressing the opinion that it was tine a sandidate vras secured.</p>
        <p>Eventually it was dicided to call a special meeting for the discussion of this important mattsr.</p>
        <p>OTEDIR ELBCTIONS.</p>
        <p>The thirty co-optai.ive members of the general coun- cil were re-elected, with the exseption of thp Rev. G. Harbottle, and the names of the Rev. H. G. Roberts,W. Bibby, and J. Worrall were added. Five co-optative members of the executive committee were elected, Councillor Wilkinson's name being added to tlaa list.</p>
        <p>VOTE OF TRiNIC3 TO MR. PIERPOINT.</p>
        <p>Mr. O'BRIEN. proposd tdiat a vote of thanks be ten. dered to Mr. Pwrpoint for his valuable services to the Liberal Association (luring his eliGeial connection with it. Mr. EILTGEISS seconded, COLI/WillOr TINNION, Mr. MALKIN acid Mr. DIXON supported and Oar) naotion was. earned with acclantltion.</p>
        <p>THE LATE ALDERMAN WRIGGT.</p>
        <p>A resolution of sympituy with the family of the late Alderman Wright was also passed. A report of the speeches made will be found in another column. The proceedings closed with a rote of thanks to the Chairman for presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>BOWLING. LANCISEIRE AND CHESHIRE ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>BOWLING. LANCISEIRE AND CHESHIRE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Tne Council of this association mot on Tuesday even- ing at the Spread Eagle Hotel to make the draw for the thirteenth annual championship competition, and appoint suAable greens for the same, Mr. J. WG. Coombs, vice-president, in the chair. Prior to the Cord. mencement of the draw the secretary gave notice he had received a communication from the Warwickshire and Worcestershire Association asking to meet the Lanca- shire and Yorkanire team in Manche.ter on the 16t12 June, and at Birmingham on the 21st July. The second date was agreed to, but the 16:b 01 June having been accepted by Yorkshire, another date (2nd June) was a,kod for. Toe result of the draw and of the greens selected are given hereunder :—Leigh Railway v Cheet- ham Temple at Urmston, Heatley v Harpurhey at Urmstnn, HeAtley v Flarpurhey at Timperley (Pelican), , Radcliffe v Westhoughton at Wigan, Warring- ton South v Lower Broughton at Irlsin, Whitefiald v Timperley at Old Trafford (Dog and Partridge), Irlam V Urinsion (Vietori,) at Hs.rpurhey, Broughton Limited v lllnllinwood at Radcliffe, hail, Slrdlord v Dpuglas at Sef ton Park, Didsburs. v Prestwich at Trafford Hotel, Kersal Moor v Sefton Park at New oft-le-Willows, INewton-le-Willows v Pendleton at Leigh (Limited), Ashen-on-Mersev v Wgrrington North at fleatlev, Old Trafford (Friday) v Wigan at Warrington, Leigh a bye.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Serious Fire at a Warrington Works.</head>
        <p>Serious Fire at a Warrington Works.</p>
        <p>A serious tire occurred on Tuesday ;afternoon at Messrs. T Locker and Co's. wire and brush works, Ellesmere-street. It appears that shortly belo7e 2 o'clock some boys in the employ of the firm noticed smoke issuing from the brush... making room, on the top floor of the centre block of buildings. Ora enter- ing the works it was discovered that some tar was i.znited and as the lire sp7ead rapidly the Fire Brigade were summoned. Superintendent. Turner and a complement of men promptly turned out but on their arrival at the scene it was found that the fire had got a complete hold of the top fleer. The b igade had only been at work a few minutes when the roof fell in, but no one was injured. Efforts were directed to saving the small buildine adjoin- ing, welch included the patent rooms, manhinery and engine room', end a wire-weaving room After considerable difficulty the last-named bnillings were saved. The nffice was darnaged,but the books, &amp;c., were removtd safely. The stores ware corn- . plstely gutted and the valuable contents spoiled. The origin of the tire is unknown, and the damage, which is covered by insurance is estimated at</p>
