      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>T EX,A;AfN E ,</head>
        <p>T EX,A;AfN E ,</p>
        <p>MAY 4Ta 1900,</p>
        <p>THE opponents of tax- Taxing Land ing land values are making Values,</p>
        <p>mountains out of mole hills. First it is said that a policy of this kind would involve a most difficult valuation, and land would have to be valued apart from buildings in towns, contrary to custom, and that valuers could not do the work. Of course, there might be some novelty in this part of the business, but it is not so novel as is imagined. Every. time an insanitary area is cleared, or a new street is made, some of the land is sold again by the local authority for building. There is never any difficulty in arriving at the value of the land so disposed of and though it may vary in different parts of a town it ought to be easy enough to settle: first, the net value of the land for the time being, and, secondly, the ' unearned increment due to local improvements, extending over any period that might be selected, fifty or twenty-five years. A second allegation made is that this unearned increment is already rated, and therefore no injustice is done to the community at large. There can be.no dispute as to the rating because it is part of the case for change. The expenditure of local rates improves the owner's property,and the occupier has-to pay rates on the enchanced value. The owner obtains an increased rent as the result, and he does nothing for it. This is an injustice to the occupier, and to the community at large, and it is desirable to reach the owner. The third allegation is that the owner cannot be reached. This is pure nonsense. He can begot at as easily as the occupier. A charge can be put upon his property, which may be called a tax or a rate without material 7difference, and commuhities are almost unanimous in desiring the power to impose this burden. They would speedily discover an effective way of doing it if they had the power, and checks would not be difficult to devise preventing any attempt of the owner to shift the responsibility. If he at once increased the rent to cover the liability, he would be increasing his own payments in proportion in subsequent years. If he attempted Eto make the tenant pay the charge by boldly adding it to his rent, the law would have to prevent this injury by enabling the tenant to deduct the value-tax from his rent, as in the case of property and income tax. The new impost, it is, further said, would not amount to a relief so substantial as to warrant the trouble of the change, and the State might declare a maximuni, beyond which a locality could not go. The fixing of a maximum would be unjust, and an inter- ference with municipalities. The yield of such a tax on land values as is proposed might not be great, but that is a matter for the localities themselves. It would vary in proportion to the recent outlay upon improvements, and their nature, but it would bring in new income and that is the chief thing. Would it check the rise in land and site values? Would in that case, lower the rents upon buildings ? Experts think not. Values rise automatically, and rents do the same. They also decline in parts of towns and cities, whilst rising in other parts. The general average is a rising one. In any case, the taxation of land values would remove an injustice, and it is demanded by municipal reformers. It is no answer to this demand to say that new income is made by tramways and such like innovations. They do not remedy the grievance; and even sentimental grievances are bad,though th 3 unearned increment s anything but a sentimental matter.</p>
        <p>SOME of the difficulties in- The Free State Farmers. herent in war in an enemy's</p>
        <p>country are now besetting Lord ROBERTS. His disposition is all on the side of leniency. He has permitted farmers who have taken the oath of allegiance and handed over their arms to resume their ordinary life. No one can blame him for these conciliatory tactics. They are the right way of dealing with an enemy who has ceased to be a combatant ; and wherever this ces- sation has been honourable no difficulty has occurred,and no one would honestly desire a change. But the Boers are not an organised army, wearin</p>
        <p>a special uniform, and totally o.istic,ct from the' civil population. Hence they may imagine that they can resume the combatant attitude whenever it is convanient for them to do so,and that nothing will happen to them when they are caught with Mauser r;fles in their hands,or their farm buildings are used as fortifications, or they are concerned 1.12 giving information of our movements to their brethren still in the field. Lord ROBERTS has suffered many, inconveniences from these tactics, and he is determined to put a stop to them. The movements of our troops to the south and east, in the recent attempt to surround the forces operating against Wepener, were made known bY these farmers, and some of them fought against us, in the field, and from their farmhouses, flYieg the white flag. The houses were at once rendered incapable of future use in the same way, and some of the farmers have been punished. Where weapons are discovered on the properties of men who profess to have surrendered their rifles the men are punished. An army must protect itself against conspiracies of this kind, and in all civil- ised war the most rigorous measures are adopted to prevent men being combatants one day and the next posing as peaceful civilians. It is II I painful necessity, and Lord ROBERTS is beginning to see that there are bounds to leniency as there are limits to laxity. He is compelled to act as if the country were all hostile and every male inhabi- tant was to be suspected. Events have imposed upon him this new attitude, and we are extremelY sorry to see that the behaviour of the Free State farmers is so bad. Excuses may be offered for them, and possibly they dn not know any better. The British an 3 Colonial troops are in their country, and all is said to be fair in love and war. But the strong man must take his measures accordingly, for the safety of his own men, our countrymen and kills' men, and we cannot complain because Lord ROBERTS is becoming more severe and his re- straints are more rigid. The Boers are fighting against us as if we were an uncivilised foe,towards whom any ruse is permissible. They have to dis- cover that honour is a good thing, and that ae army cannot tolerate the practices which might pass against savage tribes. The result of the nevi rules will probably be a lesson to the careless, and we shall find that our leniency is a mistake when it is abused. To prevent its abuse more nrfir cautions will have to be taken, and if they are irk- some the Boers have only themselves to blame.</p>
        <p>There was a disposition shown by the rank and file at the meeting of the Warrington Liberal Association on Monday to know the why and the wherefore of the party possessing no adopted can' didate. The general opinion with regard to the election appeared to be that it was not far distant, and many of the workers present were dissatisfien with the unpreparedness of the party for a, fight! unpreparedness solely duo to the lack of a candr date. One speaker put the matter in a nutshell hi advancing the proposition that if Mr. P. B. ScGtV could do so well with only a few days in which t° make the acquaintance of his constituents lan! much better could another man do who had weell or months in which to" nurse " the constituennY The advantage of having a candidate in hand is au once convincingly apparent, and the sooner leaders of the party put their heads together an decide upon the individual who must fight the battle of Liberalism in Warrington and the bat° it will be. The rank and file, on whom, as in the war, everything depends, are clearly becorana4 restive under the inactivity and uncertainty Or_ posed upon them with the clouds of election hove! ring near. The men who have to fight want oo° knowing who to fight for and it will be unwise t° keep them in the dark any longer. Let us have $ candidate before the eyes of the electorate,and ere the day of election arrives his cause can be 5° strengthened that success will be not only possibio, but probable. Our friends the enemy were alarine° at the support the late Mr. Scott obtained in s° short a time, and from their alarm should spring our confidence. There is one satisfactory featurg about the state of Liberal preparedness an", that is the completion of the revision of v0t0,1% lists and the checking-off of removals, etc. has been urgently needed for years and now that 1: is accomplished the party ought to be able to pm its full strength in the field.</p>
        <p>It is very fortunate for the pastime of cyelill that fatalities such as that which occurred .9,1 Latchford on Monday are rare. Accidents Wl' happen, of course, but the sufferer must he a Par. ticularly unfortunate individual to be killed in el unexpected and simple way which was the fate 94, Mr. John Tinsley. He was a careful rider, IP,: given to "scorching," and at the time of his cleat' was riding in such a fashion as would seem to pro; dude the possibility of serious accident. But fi one moment he was off his guard, he had turTe his:head to one side, and the next instant 11,,, crashed into a trap which was coming toriarr. him. The result was terrible and poor unfortun Tinsley only lived a few hours. So sad an at naturally caused quite a gloom in the town vfb,9"-0 he was well-known and respected, and for a WO, the story was the topic of sympathetic convers? tion. Collisions have occurred before in the trict, but never has there been ona with .500 shocking results to a cyclist as in this case. accident emphasises the need for the utel°:' caution and vigilance when cycling where there any traffic on the road, and it is quite certain 0311,,, local devotees of the wheel will take warning frt'a the sad fateiof their townsman, whose relatives ell friends we all sympathise with.</p>
        <p>-- The observation made some months ago, see: after the beginning of the war, by Mr. Side!' Lew, as to the encouragement of settlement Britishers in South Africa is beginning e us( bear fruit. Sir Blundell Maple suggested days since that the Government should offer men and and other soldiers on temporary service v", ferred tickets for the homeland, instead of brie ing them back in transports all together when war is over. This would induce some to proer:i. around on the chance of finding a desirable 004 co pation, and as a good many would not returncr all, but would take up holdings or other e ployment, the chances are that the difference between the cost of steerage on ordinary pasesq,r vessels for the tardy returners, and the trfloo port price would be saved. The ques;jo is, however, whether the Government ell°„;j1 not offer further inducements for the Inivic": ing up of the loyal pavulation in affected parts. A civilian citizen is worth seveottt soldiers by his influence, and we want to makt3tor armed possession nf the country as undetnensol tive as possible. The soldiers who remain on gbillieit will be only units returning back as soon es v,ina time is up. But farmers would probably marr.ft their families will grow up to radiate Britiebo • fluence through wider circles. A grant Ithe settler would be a good investment, provideutiot settlement were lasting; but; the trouble is wiles (10 Englishmen would care to farm as severely !, the Boers, and be content with as small a preLL'for may be that although men would not emigrs' the such a prospect, it may look more desirable (rtions spot, as contrasted with the depressing eon,,,,di,are" of City life. Moreover, the fact that the nable meat will he settled will be an element fav3ll to colonisation. ad In spite of everything that has been said , done, the Indian Famine Fund contuluss'e to languish. How long the Goveroment proll°ool, rely upon a national charity which has tha roe itself is not yot apparent; nut tho pressinVitli for help becomes more pitiful every day. urge been said that too many projects have been TV on the attention of the public this yes.r.... various Twentieth Century Funds and tnvitil# , Funds are believed to have extracted the ROD "</p>
      </div>
