      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>In 1896 an Act was passed extending the jurisdic- "en of quarter Sessions to trials for burglary, and lt,tterobted burglary, and other serious matters. frene the criminal statistics which have just been Presented to Parliament by the Home Office, it 4PPears that, while in 1891, out of 480 such cases en,1113, four went to e.eseions, in 1896 there were 66 out 468, and in 1898 there were 362 tried in those and only 159 at Assizee. The most pleasing kekture of the annual report is that which points to It substantial decrease of crime throughout the, je(lellentrY. A comparism of the figures for the year the period to vaich they are brought down, 'T• U 1 those of the previous year might lead to iltLe conclusion that crime is slightly increasing. Vlt, as has often been demonstrated, the annual "illations are an insufficient basis for any general Ca „--11, elusions. In order to arrive at trustworthy re- -11:4,,,1t5, it is necessary to pass in review a consider- 4"4erable number of years, and over ae wide an area tia2Pessible of criminal acts. An examination on „ ese lines makes it clear that crime in the widest geh. ro're, So far as it is dealt with by the courts, has t• the last thirty years shown considerable diminu- r„._ ion Igoe proportion of crime to Deputation. accord- -11 to the report, has decreased from 8937 in the huu,ooo in 1863 to 505 9 in 1898, the diminution been continuous. Crime reached a tilliZiraum in the sixties. Since then t there has t,....411 a steady decline in the total and also in the wo leading divisions—crimes against the person t Itith• Violence and crimes egainst property without ience, It is recognised in regard to the whole ttilluiect bf crime and its present diminution, that th,ae two considerations to be made, namely. Unproved police arrangements make detection re easy and that many offences have been tech ni- eli'Y Created by recent legislation. On the whole, ag, :report and statistics are highly satisfactory, tirorted as they are by useful maps showing both a general and local hearings of the subject. 44, enabers of the Bridgewater Town Council having the courage of their convictions, or the temerity th Others term it, to ignore the expressed wish of atepavers, " in public meeting assembled" by 14• 'eavouring to promote a scheme of electric light- Which of 'which many disapprove, appear to have "Ught about a tug-of-war which should be "made Attitt°t: Of "by other communities sinaqarly situated. rnolic meting attended by seven or eight bun- () ratepayers,the action of the Corporatrn found ttl about six or seven supporters, and statements 147,? by the mover and seconder of an amendment • "POort of the Council to the effect that there ett no obligation on the part of that body to con- t Ip. the ratepayers concerning inKi ters of this kind, Very strongly resented. For the opposition it ee 5 held that many Corporations had been ills- espoi tited with their experience of electric light- that the present time was inopportune, end the ratepayers generally insisted upon the th• -"nment of the Provisional Order, it is for R(livn Council to make the next trove, • tbere are probably persons who do not know et ee t,hB exist‘.,,nce of the United British Women's te7,lgration Association. But b appears to e v- rs alive and under most. distinguished patronage to of the Princess of Wales. Its chief object is all,Zailße for the protection of suitable persons of especially women," who wish to co to 4t4 .oritieh Colony, during he journey and upon trit„V,.al• These persons are met on landing and veoluions found for them,if rovirel, by the cerres- teet:lents of the association,to whom copies of their pi ellzonials. etc., are Pent in advarce. The Bishop tho rtillihia pr,side at a meeting to be held at the al, Perial Inse,itute on Wednesdny next, when of the asseciatien will be made known zileicooseidbio:btht e,wvaahrullie malyaydvocated. Th,ro is cer- -44t1 37 need for greater information on the eubj he enlightened as to the rtrigin and ultimate aims of the movement. On Dureinualtion of the whoishle emigration of women Joicerhts/drople, there are probably two opinions. lAN "all noels, assuredly, the long purse, the ,nrerlitile,,a' and all the geeerosity he is usually JAW tiel`" with being master of, if he is to meet only the claims at present demaadel of him : With Pitrainartn.:B branches of the War Fund, the Indian -8 litle• and the Ottawa Fund he may be</p>
        <p>truly said to have his hands full. It is not often that so many cabs are made upon him simultane- ously. nor is it desirable that it should be so. Of all calamities war is generally regarded as the worst but famine, to the extent to which it prevails in India at the present moment, is every wait as bad, while fire, as it raged in and about Ottawa last week, proved as destructive of human interests, if not of unman life, as either. In order t:.) make good, as far as is humanly possible, the damage sustained and the misery caused by the combined agencies of war, famine, and fire John Bull has loosened his purse strings, as only he knows how, in a way and a purpose that is felt to the ends of the earth. The money is pouring into the Mansion House Funds an thence being disseminated throughout the country, in South Africa, and in Canada, with a promptitude, plenitude, and effect that must be regarded by the thousands upon thousands of reci- pients, and justly so, as marvellous. By way of 'winging the matter nearer home to ourselves, what we have to do now is for each to ask what he has done, towards bringing about these wonderful results.</p>
        <p>The tobacconist is ?lbw actually paying more than his share of the recently increased duty. Could the force of patriotism further go? The gingerbread loses a little of its gilt, however, when it has to be added as in truth it must be, that it is all the fault of the publican, and only tinsel patriotism after all. A brave effort was made to put up the three- penny proprietary tobaccos to threepence farthing. Quite a number of the trade in some towns agreed, but the publicans continued to sell at the old prices, as might have been expected of a class usually so "conservative," So that, one by one,the poor and perhaps not over patriotic tobacconists have had to give way on the ground of "unfair com- petition." But there are those who find their cus- tomers true patriots,since they do not ceraplain,and therefore are " allowed " to continue paying the farthing extra. With one or two exceptions, the higher class preprietory tobaccos are expected to remain at the increased charges. It is curious, and not very agreeable, to find a , marked increase in the number of tradesmen in some of the provincial towns, summoned of late for giving short weight in coal. Of course, this is a matter which directly a ffects the poor,at a time,too, when coal is very dear. These persons who gener- ally purchase by the hundredweight, have to pay a higher price for their fuel than persons who can afford to purchase a ton or two at a time, and the least the authorities can do is to protect them from fraud at such times as the present. In cases where the offence is clearly established, it is a question whether the justices should give an offender the option of a fine. As is fairly well-known, it some- times pays an unprincipled dealer to incur the risk of a simple fine, andithe bench should make an ex- ample of a flagtant offender by sending him to prison, as Voltaire would have said, for the " en- couragement " of others. It will interest those readers who feel interested in the Boys' Brigade to know that their numbers have•reached the splendid total of 70,000 boys with 5,500 officers. There has been a proposal for the adoption of a quinquennial census before the public as long as most of us can remember, and the fact that a letter from the Royal Statistical Society is just now going the round of the various Town Councils, urging its necessity, is another proof, if proof were needed, of the natural conservatism of the English race, It is held by medical officers and others engaged in statistical computations that the value of a more frequent census would be very great, and it is not believed that the expense would be so heavy as that of a ten years' census. Moreover, it would not be a local charge. Still the expense is the stumbling block. In the opinion of some the quinquennial census is not desirable, and would involve an ex- pense not required. There are persons who, for one reason or another, would have no census at all. In certain provinces of Turkey, the census can only be taken with the aid of the military, on account of the violent opposition of the ladies, who object to questions relating to their ages and other matters. Fifty years ago the Census in this country was of a more inquisitorial character than it is now. The tendency has been to narrow it down, rather than to widen it and extend the inquiry to matters of belief, orquestions of what particular religious or other body we belong to. The census will be taken next year on almost precisely the lines of the census of 1891. This the whole country was given to understand some weeks since,and until the letter appeared from the Royal Statistical Seciety,scsreely a word has been heard of disapproval. But this may be partly accounted for in the same way that so many other things have for the last six months been accounted for—by the attention so largely monopolised by the war.</p>
      </div>
