        <p>oa sto miles to )1305°Eed hatl. ad 'itll.-04 ..138/1 U e r. st</p>
        <p>•ble coin from the purses of the charitable. If that ' .3e TD, there has beee a deplorable lack of discrimi- nation in the almsgiving. Thousands cf English People living at ease in England cierive a large part ef their income, directly or indirectly, from India, and if they had all contributedto the Famine Fund generouAy it would not have been necessary to ltike these constant appeals to sentiment or con- What of the slaarehol.ders of the Mysore CT.old Mining Company, with their 140 per cent. dividends in a time of famine, or of other coos- Parnee bringing untold riches from India to persons 111 England who never saw the land? A list of shareholders in Indian comoani, e•mtaining the rtionnts they have contributed to the Famine nd, would be a remarkably interesting doeu- taent.</p>
        <p>--- -- We are in some doubt a 3 to whether we should .r;',Xpress our deep gratitude to the President of the 13a ptist Union for an unexpected and high honour rhas done the Press, or whether we should ens- himg with our pen for forgetting his duty to re Master he serves. The Almighty is almighty 11 the estimation of the humble3t of us, so that it owas with surprise we discovered that the Rev. W thilif feared the eye of the Press more than at of his Maker. This sad conclusion we ec'tne to after reading the report of last Thurs- da 9 S meeting of the Baptist Union, when Xu r• Clifford edified his hearers—and made ileja hungry --by,an address which lasted until a4f-past one. The termination of it saw the inner illah triumphant over decorum, for the members, 8° We are told, made sudden rushes for their hats ccats. "Brethren, Brethren" cried the Presi- I.Lent in alarm "I hope we are not going to finish 3 this. Remeuber we are in the presence of r,?Pr3ss—and of the Almighty." The rebuke was en to heart, but whether from fear of the „Fess or the Almighty we do flat know. Perhaps 17r9 Cuff could tell us. In the meantime we thank 4.JA for honouring the Press to a greater extent 'lan we have ever before heard of.</p>
        <p>in re-electing Mr. A J Jolley to the position of llairman of the Warrington Board of Guardians, he members have adopted a wise course. It would difficult to find a more capable man for the Position or one more deeply interested in poor law ministration. He is a reformer, it may be of a YerY mild type, but still as reform is greatly needed 14 Poor Law work it is some satisfaction to see a °former of some kind .1t the head of affairs. In Noting through the classification scheme which is til°97 in operation at the Warrington Union Work- Mr. Jolley displayed a thorough know- pp of the requirements, and in intro- such a scheme whereby the deserv- w"g poor were more humanely treated he I,B's proceeding on truly progressive lines. " 'nay seem somewhat anomalous that one who refers to be called a Conservative should perforce in reality a reformer. To these eccentricities o can, however, afford to be blind provided, of nraei that the reforms introduced are of the ,IRght kind. In commencing a second year of office, Jolley is to be congratulated upon so cona. Pletely th gaining the conFdence of the members of g. ° Board, and it is to be hoped that his second for", of office may be even more productive of re- to rc, jo poor law administration in the Warring- than the first has been.</p>
        <p>The competition was a little keener for the krition of junior vice-chairman of the Board. Ir• hY a junior vice.chairman as well as a senior tre-chairman is needed, is not altogether clear. aflltter, the honour, such as it is, is keenly sought f as was evidenced by the competition /7 it. Mr. J. W. Knowles was the retiring 1:10r" and the country Guardians again brought fcrward as their candidate whilst the town lEteajdians supported the nomination of Mr. Wm. thlel,e• In the end, the town Guardians carried Ic,;„lni an by ten vote,: to eight, and Mr. J. W. detin-ri"„Ae; finds himself once again a mere Guardian V-i`,„-',"eu of the little authority which a Junior the—,:kjilairman occasionally possses. One of e.i,rl,ta cr brought forward against Mr. Knowles s ceiv,ttcm was that the honours ought to be re- the to bY those who regularly attend to the work t iSeemed t The majerity of the members --Lickm„°b of this opinion, for in electing Mr. bard -7:",ttley certainly showed preference for a -"°rEing Guardian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By A CORRESPONDENT]. The deliberations of the Runcorn Urban District Council do not give rise to very much comment The acquisition of new books, however, by the Library Committee, brings before me the question of " Catalogue." For years past the public have been promised a catalogue, and it is natural to suppose that someone or other has spent that length of time preparing one. It may be a sora of odd moments; do a bit now and again task! but it is time it was finished.</p>
        <p>The proposition to open a subscription list for the Inuian Famine Fund comes to us as a very natural one. It's our Englishman's duty to support anyone in distress,and as it is the privilege of Run- corn folk to boast of their Englishness 'tie but natural to expect them to do their duty. Past history shows that Runcorn people are generous to a fault; may future historians still go on recording that.</p>
        <p>The dinner given to the Football Club by Mr. S. Tayler, the president, was a huge success. The largs room of the Wilson's hotel was crowded with about 75 guests, and the whole place presented a jovial appearance. The presentation of medals to the President, officers and players, was a very interest- ing function ; though, perhaps, the most pleasing thing of all was the warm hospitality of the host, who was in immediate touch with the whole of his guests, and who spared no pains to see all were thoroughly provided for. One result of the banquet will be to knit the Runcorn players more firmly to their native soil, and as Houghton puts it" make them all determined to deserve such a president."</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Liberal Club rooms, in Regent-st., which have recently been beautified and renovated were re-opened on Tuesday. The proceedings com- menced with a dinner; a public meeting,and wound up with a concert. Amongst the speakers were Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P., and his son Mr R Brunner, Mr S Taylor, J.P., Mr A R Norman, and Ar RII Posra,,tt. But,as a report of those appears elsewhere, it is more particularly to the rooms I would call attention. They are commodious, well- arranged, and comfortable, almost reaching in arrangement the perfection of an ideal club, All Liberals would, therefore, do well to pay them a visit, after which I will predict they will be filled with a desire to share in their benefits, by becoming members of the club.</p>
        <p>There were rumours afloat the other day,that the boy Sargeant, who is alleged to have been nep. rly strangled by a boatman, was dead. The statement is not correct, and so far as I can ascertain he is now progressing favourably tlwards recovery. As is well known, the man who is alleged to have mal- treated the boy is in custody but will be brought before the Runcorn Bench of Magistrates on Tues- day morning next. I give these facts, as there ap- pears to be more than passing intarest taken in the case.</p>
        <p>The Hearing of Defended Cases at the County Court. Before hearing the defended cases at the War- rington County Court on Thursday, his Honour Judge Gwynne James, said it would be a great con- venience to solicitors who practiced at the court and had to come from a distance and also to those who carried on business in the town if there was some definite time when defended cases should be heard He proposed in the future Le, make an order that any defended cases in whiffli counsel or solicitori appeared for the plaintiff or defendant would not be heard by the Judge before 11.15 provided that notice of such caseq defended be received by the Registrar on or before the returning day. That would enable solicitors who re.4ided in Manchester to start from there at about 104 instead of very much earlier and the same would equally apply to Liverpool. Having made the order for the conveni- ence of those who practised before him he trusted they would assist him in carrying it out by sending the requisite notices to the registrar.</p>
        <p>' IiNSBED COMPOUND' (Trade Mark) gives ex- pectoration without strain, 91d., 131 d. Sold by Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 4, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 4, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WiDNES.</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER AND THE RATING OF THE GROUND VALUES.</p>
        <p>We understand that at the request of the Muni- cipal Councils Association, Sir John Brunner is shortly going to introduce into Parliament a Bill proviuing for the rating of ground values. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUB</head>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUB</p>
        <p>The Wednesday section of the above cycle club, under the care ot Captain J. H. Jones turned out in f ir strength and rode to iitockport and back (56 mils). The run was a most enjoyable oae, both roads and weather being favourable.</p>
        <p>SEARCH FOR MISSING FRIENDS.-A MYSTERIOUS MESSAGE. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The other day, a boatman handed to Inspector Ilick3,of the Runcorn Palice,a piece of paper which he picked up in a bottle floating in the Bridgewater Canal near Sale on the 26,-1 inst. The piece ot paper blre the following inscription :—" By the time you find this bottle my end will have come at Run3orn. Your son Joe." Police inquiries,have so far elicited that no one of the name of Joe is missing from Runcorn. Any information which would throw light upon the subject would be welcome.</p>
        <p>MILTON SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY, WIDNER. This event was celebrated on Sunday. To xev W II Jeffries, of St. Helens, preached two excelleut sermons, the one in the morning being devote I to the children, whilst the discourse in the evening related to parental duties. In the afternoon the c ,oir rendered the sacred cantata, "Guided dome," the narrative being read by Mr W J Stainer. The Chair was occupied by Councillor G Davies, Misses Jessie Thomas and Hulme interpreted solos, the latter taking part in a duet with Miss M Harrison, Mr F Smith delivered the bass solo with good effect. The whole service was exceedingly well carrie I our,. Mr W J Baggeridge acted as conductor, Messrs Bar- trop and H Lacy presided at the organ anu piano, and Mr T Lacy accompanied on the flute. The ser- vices,at which the children sang appropriate hymns, were well attended. The collections realised about ten guineas. A CHILD RESCUED FROM DR9WNING.</p>
        <p>A gallant action was performed last Saturday by a Runcorn youth named Joseph Savage, Ivy-road, which was no less than plunging into the Bridge- water canal and rescuing a child. It appears that young Savage was standing on the Deiph Bridge awaiting a friend, when he saw a little child tall into the canal immediately below the Bridge. He ran down the steps and divesting himself of hie coat and hat plunged into the water and secured the child. Being weighed down with his clothes and boet4, Savage called out for help to a man on the bridge, but received no response. He, after a des- perate struggle, however, succeeded in landing the child en the bank and scrambled out. He went to the Navigation Inn, and was there given a change of clothing and some brandy. Considering that Savage had only recently recovered from a severe attack of bronchitis his action was most heroic. The chill belonged to Mr. Richardson. of the Bull Inn. ASSAULT ON INSPECTOR STRICKLAND AT WIDNES. Patrick Farrell, 35, Kent-street, was summoned at Widnes, on Monday for assaulting Inspector Strickland whilst in the execution of his duty on April 29th.—Inspector Strickland stated that about one o'clock on Sunday morning he was in Albert- road when he heard some windows being smashed in Kent-street. He went to prisoner's house and saw the bottom squares broken. The man was in the house with a light. He was'cursing and using most violent language. He remonstrated with him through the window, and noticed that his arm was cut. Witness asked him to open the door,and upon the prisoner acquiescing he went into the house. Upon ,examining the man's arm he found it was not so bad and he informed him to this effect where- upon prisoner became more furious and enraged. Picking up a knife from the table he threatened to rip his wife open, and witness had no doubt he would have carried out his threat had he been able to get at her. The knife was taken from hica,and he said he would have a blew at some one. He struck witness violently on the chest with his clenched fiets. Witness closed with him and took him into custody. There was a great crowd of people about at the time. Every Saturday night his wife had to get out of his way in consequence of his violence when drunk.—P.C. Jackson corroborated. Defend- ant said he did not remember anything about the affair.—The Mayor, in fining the defendant 103 and costs, said you ought to be thankful to Inspector Strickland that you are not here on a more serious charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SALE OF WORK AT THE</head>
        <p>THE SALE OF WORK AT THE</p>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION.</p>
        <p>The sale of work at the above mission was conclude on Friday afternoon and evening last. The thy was I fittingly described as the children's day, and was pat- ronised by a large number of adults as well as juveniles. The opening ceremony was performed shortly after four o'cicek by Mrs. Stakes, of Rock Savage, Runcorn. She was supported on the platform by the Revs E Lerribert (senior chaplain of the Mersey Mission), and H E Elwell, Mr W Shaw (missioner), and others. Miss Stokes was also amongst those present. The R-e;, E Lambert (who presided), said he had been asked to perform the very pleasant duty of introducing Mrs Stokes. Of course, that was the third day of the sale and some might have thought that it was rather a poor honour to ask anybody to come and open the sale after so many of the good things had been disposed of, but he felt 81110 that no one would rejoice more than Mrs. Stokes at knowing se many of the things had been sold and that the efforts of the promo:ers had met with such success (Applause.) Again the third day nossessed a charm of its own, inasmuch as the workers came up to the scratch—though not so fit, fresh, and vigorous—still determined to carry on the issue to victory. (Applause.) It -reflected the greatest possible credit . upon those workers that they had been able to stick to their guns and aceomplish what they had. (Applauso.) He felt sure the result of the sale would enable them to do what was necessary to clean and re-decorate the mission. While people would have their houses nice and clean was it fit that they should have the house of God bare and wanting in the simplest decoration. (Hew., hear.) He hoped as time went on that other things re- quired would follow, especially as times mended and they had no claims of war wad famine upon them as at present. Then they could have their church es beautiful inside as it was outside. He need hardly introduce Mrs. Stokes or her husband—(applause)—who had rendered yeoman service, during the hard winter they had jest gene through, to the cause of the Mersly Mission. Having paid a high tribute to Mrs. Stokes, whom he felt sure had done all she could to encourage Mr. Stokes in his work, he had great pleasure in welcoming her as being in sympathy with the sale of work and the Mersey Mission to Seamen in Runcorn. (Applause.) Mrs. 'Stokes then said she ha i great p'easure in declar- ing the sale of work open and in hoping it would be a great seccess. Mr. Shaw deserved every help and en- couragement for the energetic way in which he worked and she was pleased to see he was being so ably assisted by so many good workers. (Applause.) Although that was the last day—the children's day—she hoped it would be a lucky one. (Applause.) Toe Rev. II E Elwell moved a vote of thanks to Mrs. Stokes for the very graceful way in which she had per- formed the opening ceremony. They wanted about £43 to make up the £lOO they weredesirous of getting,therefore, he hoped all would make a great effort to get it. (Ap- plause.) Mr. Shaw seconded the vote of thanks, and also took the opportunity of heartily thanking al the workers and those who had so generously contributed to the suc- cess of the bazaar. Ho could not speak too highly of the valuable assistance Mr. Mrs. and Mi,s Stokes heti ten- deed to the Mission,end he al3O wished to put on record the admirable services of Mr. looks, the mission hall keaper. (Applause.) There never apes a batter. (Loud applause.) Mr. Shaw else said he would most likely hold a bazaar annually, and hoped to see the day when Runcorn Mission would be entirely self supperting and Independent ef Liverpool. (Load enplause.) Th vete of thankt was accorded with three hearty cheers.—The washing competition for ladies, for which prizes were offered by Messrs. Lever Bros.. Limited,was won by Mrs Jane Cliff,and the second prize by Miss C Price. An interesting whistling competition (also for ladies) was won by Miss Garvie, and the second prize Iwas secured by Miss C M Price. The proceeds of the sale of work realisad £74 ; in addition to which Miss Naylor subscribed £5; seri about £6 was realised by lunch basket socials, etc. This is a result all may ba very highly complimented upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL SCHOOLS.—LiSt Sunday the annual sermon on bnhalf of the National Day Schools ware preached in thy Parish Church, by the Vicar the Rev. H. B. Blozg, M.A. Owing to the inclenanney of the weatt'Pr the offertories only realized £7 ss.—baing about £lO less than in the previous year. SVNI3-IFF TO FRODSILAM VOLUNTSIERS.-011 -Slttliday evening the three members of the Frodsham Vo!unteer Corps—Sergeant John Holland, Corporal E Redgemand Private H. L. Calveley—under orders for South Africa had an enthusiastic send-off dinner which was served ad- mirably at the Commercial Hotel, Major Harrison and Captain Ashton were present,with the greater number of the Carps.</p>
        <p>LINUM OATHARTICUM—PILLS, digestive, correc- tive, and agreeably aperient, 9kci„ 131 d. Of all Ohemsts.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RE-OPENING OF THE</head>
        <p>RE-OPENING OF THE</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Speech by Sir John Brunner, M.P.</p>
        <p>The premises of the Runeorn Liberal Club in Regent-street, which througe the munificence of Sir John Brunner, Bart„ M.P., have been altered and renovated to an extent almost amounting to re-building, were on Tuesday evening formally re-opened. The proceedings were inaugurated with a dinner, after weich there, was a public meeting and concert. 'Air. Samuel Taylor presided at the pub- lic meeting, and was supported by Sir J T Brunner, Bart, M.P., Hon. Mr R Brunner, Messrs A R Nor- man, and R H Posnett ; there were also present: —Messrs J M. Frith, J D Jones, T Cook, R Morris, J Jolley, T Leach, S Coventry, '2 tee, J Lea, J Stan- way, E Johnson, W Peacock, A Riley (secretary), W alillinger (assistant secretary), It Moores (treasurer), W B Brimelow (chairman of Committee), J Green, J Cragg, etc, etc. The Secretary read letters of apology for absence from Mr J It Firtb, aril Mr George Kiag (North- ' wich), and telegrams from Mr. Algernoa Fletcher and Mr, James Handley. The Chairman who was cordially received said when asked to occupy that chair, he felt that the Committee could have made choice of someone who was more fluent in language than himself, for they all knew he was not a public speaker. Itieeed in Mr Posnett and Mr Norman who were present they had tee very men, and he would much rather have seen either of them in his place. (No, no.) He could assure them, however, of how very delighted tie was at taking part in toe re-opening of "our club." (Applause). Re would try to give them a short account of the Runcorn Liberal Club's history frem the time of its formation. Some twelve years ago it was thougA by the Liberal party that Runcorn ought to have a Liberal Club,so as to get the Liberals ot the town together for poli- tical purposes as well as for their recreation. Rooms were taken in High-street, and they managed by some means—but at great inconvenience—to ar- range for a billiard table reading rooms and such like. Everyone knew it was a most ieconvenient place though they took a lease for three years. At the expiration of that time however, the committee found it necessary to look out for more suitable rooms, but there were none available at that time, and Bank Chamber rooms were taken as a tempor- ary building and occupied for a short time. Tnen fortunately for the Liberal Party, and for the Runcorn Liberal Club Sir John Brunner bought that building they were then assem- bled in. (Applause.) He spent a considerable sum upon it and somewhat improved it He was then approached ani asked whether he would let the Liberal Party have it as a club room. Well the result was that Sir John kindly offered wide his usual generosity and good feeling towards the Liberal Cause to transfer the building over to the Liberal Party for the same amount which he originally paid for it.—Mr. Pusnett : Less Mr. Taylor —Mr. Taylor: Four hundred nounds was the amount, but there was some money spent upon it by Sir John Brunner, and he did not charge us with the costs he had been put to. (Applause.) Then came the question of how the place could be made convenient and put in weed order as a club. Trustees were appointed and three hundred peunds—(et least that was the figure given to him)— were spent upon furoishine it suitably. That brought the figure up to £7OO. As time went on, he thought that every member of the club, teed every one that took an interest in it, found that building was not really suitable. There was only one room, and a read- ing room by name—(hear, hear)—and he was sorry to add it was most unfortunate that many of the club's members resigned upon that account. E very member was not a billiard player—(h .ar. hear) —and es they knew there was nothing else for them to do. But toe. Maintenance Committee, he was glad to say, had a geed deal of go and energy, and were determined that things sheuld be altered for the better. (Applause.) The Trustees and Committee were asked to meet, and it was decided that an architect should be consulted and plans obtained eAth a view to seeing what could be done w th the building ; and as he was gad to observe that in that committee they had a gold many good mei with their heads screwed on the right way. (Laughter.) The re- sult was that ultimately plans were got out and it was decided to make the alterations which had resulted in those beautiful rooms. (Applause). But, then came the question of ways and means. He always felt sorry that that question should be always cropping up. I (Laughter.) He knew so merry good causes, where everything could be done and things made very comfort- able for the people—he was not speaking of he club only —bat unfortunately the question of ways and means cropped up. They were of course in that pssition, but they looked abroad and there found a father—Sir John Brunner—(applause)—who came forward most hand- somely, and he was sure made a most generous offer. (Renewed applause.) Tno plans were shown him and Sir John felt satisfied that it was the very thing required, so he got the Committee out of its difficulty by offericg to lend them £5OO, the estimated cost, and as most of them were aware, upon such terms that they could net look behind them. (Cheers.) They had met at the re- opening and lse was sure everyone present and also Sir John Brunner would agree that they had a home for many years to come. There was a suitable billiard-room one,he was sure could not be surpassed anywhere. Then the room they were assamt:eed in could be used for lec- tures, public rneetinee, and any other purpose ; and in additien there were two or three rooms which those who did not play at billiards could adjourn to without being disturtiad, and where they could spend their time in reaeling, study, or any other pursuit. (Flear, hear.) They were delighted tn have Sir John Bruen-r present with them at the opening.(Applause.)He could assure Sir John that he made no mistake when he said that all Runcorn people—and be did not confine himself to the Liberal Party—were always delighted to see him. (Hear,hear.) There was not another constituency in the United Kingdom which was mere favoured than they in Run- corn. He would eive his reason for Saying so. Outside what Sir John Brunner had done for the welfare of the people what had he not done as the Representative of the Northwich Division. (Applause.) He (Mr. Taylor) was related with every public body in Runcorn, and knew that in all questions which had been brouelat f ore Parliament affecting those hodies,Sir John had celled the puteic bodies' attention to each and every question that affected them.—(hear, hear)—and had asked their opinion on everyone before pledging himself to anything (Applause.) Therefore as a working representative they could not get a better. (Applause.) He could rive them any number of instances but would content him- self with one. Here Mr. reclor instanced the Grenville- street school transaction, and how Sir John had accom- plished in one hour what the Schol Board of Run- corn were unable to do in a twelve month. (Ap- plause.) Ho could assure Sir John they felt proud of him—(hear, hear and applause)—as 'leech for his close connection with Runcorn, as for all he had accomplished for the Nortawich Division. (en- plans?). They were not going to part with him if he would only continue to rick to them —(eheers) —arid when the electien came round, and it was time to work, he could assure him they would work. (Loud applause). In his opinion Sir John Brunner was the only represen- tative in Cheshire. (Laugh ter.) A voice: Of the Liberal party.—Sir John : What, of the other party.— Mr Taylor : I never theueht of them. (Increased laughter.) Mr Taylor then called upon Sir John Brunner to address the meeting. Sir John Brunner, who was greeted by hearty ap- plause, after apologising for his inability to be present at dinner, in consequence of having to open a hazaar in London, eaid it was a pleasure to have heard the tele- grams and letters read, becanse they showed how heaetily in sympathy with the Liberal party the'r friends were in spite of the fact that they were not with them. Mr. Fletcher was not able to Come on account of what he hoped was a passing indiapoidtien, greatly to be re- gretted ; Mr. King's avocations were pleasant ones he knew and they could not extend sympathy to him, only regret that he was not with them. H. was sorry Mr. Handley was somewhat " ander the weather" and he hoped to hear he was entirely restored to his usual health. As they were t o have a concert which he hoped to enjoy until 11 20, he was not gnine to make a long speech. He was rather impressed with the fact that Mr Brimelew's name did not app,.r noon the pro- gramme—(laughter)—because it was Mr Brimelow who was largely responsible for the pleecant arrsueemente of that night—(applause)—and it was his characeeriet c modesty that had kept his name off the programme. (Laughter.) He remembered very well indeed a meeting be had with a number of enthusiastic Liberals in a place above that had practically disappeared, a most wretched, uncomfortable place it was, and the only thine that was warm about it was the hearts of the men be met, and the Chairman had described their enthu- siasm and their energy in such a fa,ahien that he need say no more about it,but he should like to add that they had spent the money they had in their hands a great deal better than he did name rears ago. What they had done had turned out well ' • what he did was character- isle and the Chairman had described it in fitting terms. (Laughter.) Mr. Taylor had spoken of him (Sir John) in terms of apnreciation—in terms he had no sympathy I with at all. There was no man in Cheshire who could put better feeling into better taneueee than Mr. Taylor, their chairman. One of his jokes was to describe him (Sir John) as his fathm.. tighter.) He would not accept that position, but there were same present whom he was delighted to call po:iticelly his darling goes. (Hear. hear.) Mr. Taylor had described one little piece of work he had had the pleasure of dcene for Runcorn ; ,and be booed that with she good will of the eeatlemen he saw before him and of all their neighbours in the good old town that he might be permittee—he would hi proud to be permitted to do man: other services. (Applause.) Re dare say they had heard</p>
        <p>of a private bill for making a bridge across the Money, and a vast deal more convenient he hoped it would be than the old one. The Bill had passed the third reading in the House of Commons and had gone to the House of Lords. He had been concerned in the promotion of that Bill from the beginning—(near, hear)—and he had had some correspondence with friends upon the subject as far as the enterprise affected the town of Runcorn, rine he did not think there need be any secret about it. He was greatly concerned to have this bridge adopted by the authorities and not left in the hands of a company desirous of making money out out. (Applause). He had been authorised to say on behalf of the most influen- tial members of the committee of premoteis that they were like-minded with him—they had entered intro this not in a se f-seekingsmoney making spirit, but only with public spirit and with a desire for the public conven- ience. (Applause.) He should like the public authorities , concerned Widoes Town Council,Runcorn Urban District Council,Lancashire County Council and Cheshire County Council to take the matter into their owe hands, work it out for the public good and so make quite sues that it sheuldtbe worked in no private interest but solely for the public benefit. (Hear, bear.) He made no apology for the suggestion because be was perfectly satisfied the Bridge would be no burden on the rates and as time went on the tolls might be made cheapar and cheaper until it was quite possible some day, that public spirit might arse and the bridge be made free between the two t ownri and the two counties. (Applaus ). He bad an interesting communication from the National Cyclists Uuien, who asked that the charge for a bicycle, which was 2d on the existing bridge, should be reduced to id on the new bridge. He had very li:tle doubt that it would require no persuasion on his part for his colleagues to accede to this request. Tne fact of the matter was they were all practically cyclists, end transporting a bicycle cost vary little. The owner did his own porterage and the Company owning the bridge had to find no labour for the purpose of trans- porting from one side to the other. Should lee say a few words upon the general question before be eat down. Tne whole world was ringing with the daily reports from South Africa, and there was no more important coasideration to which they could give their minds than what had been the effect of the last eight mouths upen us, and what was likely to be the effeat upon the world and especially, upon the British Empire from settee has been. He would give them a few of the ideas that had nem to him after very serious consideration, and one of them was this that he was not only heartily ceuteut with, bat thoroughly proud of the volunteer army. (Applause). riP knesi a good deal of the countries where there a'a, conscription.and he would beg of Eaglish people to suff.-r many inconvenienced, to go through many hardships rather than allow conscription to be established here and he made boel to say there was no country that less needed conscription than the United Kingdom. (Ap- plause). Ours was a volenteer army—volunteer in time of peace but far more emphatically volunteer in the time of war. He heard when staying a few weeks ago at a lovely spot in Italy, that when semi years ago soldiers from that spot, as soldiers had to go from every part of Italy to take part in the war in North West Africa, the most disaszroue that Italy ever had, the whole poem. lation was in tears, and the male portion of it went down the railway to tear up the rails rather than let the men go. Compare that with the spirit shown not only throughout Cheshire, not only throughout Eogland, but throughout the whole United Kingdom and Ireland. There was no village that had not sent forth its gallant young men and accompanied them in triumph and in sympathy and patriotism • to the station. Conscription would kill petriotism and not promote it. At times of excitement like this all sorts of creatures came to the front and amongsttho maddest of them were those who advocated cmiscription. He (Sir John) had the greatest contempt.for the notion, that conscription was required amongst the subjects of Her Majesty. His advice was that the service should be shortened and that our men and especially the officers should bh more seientifically trained and edu need and—he would put ic as respectfully as he could—that the army .9.h.1u14 not so greatly be the play- ground for officers as it haa been hi the past. (Hear, hear.) He thought there was no need to recommend to them the next idea that he had Is his nainclithat hereafter there should be far more humane,far more generous treat want of the wounded and stricken in the war than there had been hitherto. (Applause.) They were asked to des- Leine themselves as Imp:nialists. Well he had been con- sidering what our Empire was; and he had arrived at this conclusion that our Empire was tbe pure result of our generous and considerate treatment of the Colonies of the Empire. Our Empire was built upon the generous, full and complete granting of self-govern- ment to our Colonies. Tney might call tnac Imperialism if they liked, but he took it teat it was the good old rule of doing net° others as they would they should do unto them. One was considered a sentimental tool if one brought ideas of that character into public life— into the neigh and tumble of politics; but he was never ashamed of acknowledging before his fellow men that the religious teaching weich be received in his youth affected hie,politic ; every day of his life. (Hear, hear.) This war was said to be inevitable and so many people had cline to that conclusion and the opinion had been expressed by personages no highly placed that it had come to be almost a matter of bad manners for one to express a contrary opinion. As he desired that the Liberals should set an example to their opponents he would not say anything about it. His opinions were pretty well known ; he need say nothing more about it. lie wented this to be considered. Peace was quite as inevitable as war perhaps more inevitable than war. The war ought never to have been begun but now it had begun, peace was the inevitable end of it. And what after peace ? Be said the British Empire was built upon the friendly, kindly consideration the Colonies had received in the past ; therefore it the Empire was to be meint lined—he said it with a full sense of responsibility—the good old rule must rule the re- lations between Us and the Colonies as it bad done in the past sixty years. It was an obligation upon us for more reasons than one that we should be as great minded in the future as we have been in the past, even towards the Dutch in Smith Africa. (Hear,hear.) They were bound not only by their Liberal principles but by the tracking they had received throughout their lives so to act towards the Dutch in South Africa that they would be bound to us hereafter as the French Canadians were bound to us to-day, as men of all nations in Australia and New Zetland, and India were bound to us to-day. lie remembered how in January 1887, when they had given him a brief respite from political work, being in the north west of India at a place called Cohort where there had been foe a long time an English garrisoe and where there had been many a bettor and sanguinary confi ct in times gone by. lie and his son rode some 75 miles with an escort of 13 lancers for the reason that a few years ago s young lieutenant wtio had gone out alone, shooting, had his lite taken by the natives, and he found that the Indian troops took as much pleasure and pride in serving her Majesty and in working for the Eaglish people as any Englishman either in Canada or Australia could slew, He learned a lesson from that which had been with him ever since and for his part be meant to do his level bast to maintain the old-fasnioned Empire and not have in- troduced into the government of the Empire an element of force to keep people down against their will. (Hear, hoar.) He bad spoken of our Empire being the result of eelf-government in the Colonies. There had been one influence which had been potent for good and that was the influence of her Gracious Majesty the Queen. (Ap- plause.) Those who went through the celebration of the first jubilee in 1887 and the second in 1897 witnessed an uprising of lora ty and patriotism that came from all parts of the world, accompanied by the utmost veneration and the utmost regard for her Majesty the Queen; and yet one would fancy occ sionally that there werepeople who believed that it was Mr. Chamberlain who had reigned for sixty-two years. (A laugh.) When he listened to Mr. Balfour in the House of Commons, speaking of our relations with the Colonies, that was the idea present in his mind, that Mr. Balfour had got it into his head that Mr. Chamber- lain had reigned for sixty years. (&amp; laugh.) Whether they went to Canada or India—whether they went to one hemisphere or the other they would find not only amongst the English speaking people but amongst all people, fellow citizene of ours, they Would find Um dew,. tion to the great White Mother; and it was a positive fact that there had been colonists from Canada going out to fight in South Alrica,feeling themselves brothers with us, who could not even spank &amp;Wish, but who were entirely Frenchseven as w .s the Prime Minister of Canada to-day. It was only the result of Liberal policy, insti- tuted some 60 years ago that had bound Canada to us with this glorious result. When the Queen visits Ireland in her 81st year, the whole populace meets her when she reaches their shores. They knew his deep seated, fervent loyalty to the 12ueen, and he said God save the Queen long to reign over us. (Applause.) The war was said firstly to be inevi- table and secondly to be a war for equal.rigtrs. Let them hold fast as Liberals to the principle that they were not going to play bully-brag but were going to grant equal rights to all white men in South Afeica, as in all other parts of the great British Empire. Did any- one amongst them see in the political history of England during the last five years any reason for. becoming a Tory. This was a very imperrant question because most of the newspapers of the country claimed that to be patriots they must be unanimous,which meant returning none but Teries, and he asked tnem was there anything in the history of the last five years that ought to make them Tories. (" No.") Was there a man amongst them who was proud of our action towards the Armenians, who looked balk with pleasure to the shilly aballaring over the freeing of Crete. It it had not been for the rignreaus indignatien of a gallant English Admiral, Crete would be still under the infamout rule of the Turks. England never 11? td a finger towards it. None of them—no Tory at any rate—looked back with much pleasure upon the Old Age Pension cheat. They of the Liberal party had not a word' of approval for &amp;ilea to landlords or parsons and he hoped they were one and all incensed against the proposal to redue,o the grants of all good schools, at the same time raising the gran's 07. the poor schools. (Slime). He did not deprecate the increased grants , to the poor schools, because there was not a shadow of doubt that the increase would make those poor sebools , better schools ; but while raising the grants of the poor schools do not let them be so mean and shabby as to re-</p>
        <p>duce the grants of good schools by one penny piece. ' This proposal to reduce the grants to one dead level of 21e or 22s per head came from men who had been educated at the cost of old charities for the poor. It might not be generally known but all the great public schools were all charities founded for the poor and all the colleges older than fifty years were founded for poor scholars. Many statesmen boasted of attending college at Oxford and Cambridge, where the old endowment amounted to £l5O to £l6O per head per annum, and it was men educated at the cost ot these old charities who proposed to reduce the grants in the best elementary schools. Never had a more mean and despicable pro- posal come from a Tory Government. They were not p.eased, those of them who watched the moral results of the Stock Exchange, that some years ago the Tory Government granted a Charter to the British South Africa Company. They believed that company with its enormous number of millions of capital, mostly watered, had been largely the cause of our trouble; in. South Africa. Not long since the Tory Government granted a charter to the Royal Niger Company, and we had recently had to buy them up at an enormous premium, after giving us a great deal of trouble. After this war was over, be was inclined to think they would have to buy up the South African Company; and he doubted, if we bought them at the present price,whether it would be a good bargain for Us. Those who had anything to do with workingmen knew what a stupid piece of legislation the Workmen's Com- pensation Act Was; and they were not pleased with the extremely retrograde Factory Bill new before the House of Commons. They were not pleased with the enormous military expenditure which loft us utterly unprepared hr fighting 30,000 Boers in south Africa. Don't for a moment think that the Gevernment; was to be held blameless for this state of affairs. If the 'war was inevitable they were fools and criminals not to be ready hr it, and after the enor- mous expenditure the fact that they were not ready for war against 30,000 farmers wasea shame and a dis- grace to them. Re hoped the country would vieit upon them the req .isite punishment. (Hear, hear.) For the first time in restory England hid been represented in public matters by vulgarity. A. statesman when dealing with the Russian Government had spoken of needing a long spoon, had spoken of a Potentate no matter how humble, as a squeezed sponge and had told a very power- ful and friendly neighbour that she must mend her manners. Let us be too proud to descend from that level which had always been maintained by English statesmen representing a nation for whom they were proud to speak. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion Sir John expressed his pleasure at being present to open thl club , and his gratitioetion at the workmanlike manner in which the alteretioas and additions had been carried out. (Loud applause.) Mr A et Norman proposed a vote et thanks to Sir John Brunner for his attendance, and whilst endorsing all Sir John bad said concerning the treatment of the Beers after the war, did not entirely agree with all he bad said concerning the justification of the war. Mr. Rld Posuett seconded, and added that the vote should not only cover Sir John's attendance that night, but have added to it" and for all he had dons in the past." (Hear, hear.) The vote was heartily accorded, and responded to. Sir John Brunner proposed and Mr. W. B. Briinelow seconded a vote of thanks to the chairman which was most unanimously accorded. The Hon. R. Bruuner, who was also heartily received, said the subject which was nearest to all their hearts and foremost in their minis had been put before them, to his mind, in a number of new lights. rne ideas to which his father had gives expression had made him look upon the matter more closely and more deeply. He thought they would all do well to can- eider it. He knew the feeling in Runcorn about the war was very deep, as indeed it was in every town and vil- lage throughout the country. Still there was not (pits enoueti tolerance perhaps with those who held aligntly different views. (Hear, hear.) Was it honest when a man questioned the inevitability of the war to call him a pro-Boer. and even knock him down. (Hear, hear.) They could all remember the righteous letter a Quaker family had written to the papers in eonnection with the riots at Searboro'. (Applause.) Did they believe that all the Boers were scoundrels--(no,no)—and with regard to the firing upon ambulances and white flags, was it not probable it was a mistake. He laid read a very temperate letter in " To-day " by a returned officer and had reason for looking upon all these questions a bit differently. Then with regard to the publication of the Solon Kop despatches. He thought that was a most dastardly thing to do, for it destroyed confidence at home in the officers out at the Front, and also tended to destroy the confidence of the soldier in his officer. However on Friday the whole thing would see the light, and he hoped that those to blame would get the full benefit of it. (Applause). . _</p>
        <p>A coiicert than followed. 1,, whicri -the Runcorn Male Voice Party (conductor, Mr. W Leathwood), Messrs Norman Johnson, McDermott (Liverpool), E Worrall. W Snaw, W Hopwood and others contributed swigs and glees. The cclospany brok up at a late hour, Sir John Brunner leaving for London by thP midnight train.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>RUNCORN FO JTBALL CLUB'S</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FO JTBALL CLUB'S</p>
        <p>PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>In the Lancashire Senior Competition Runcorn played 26 matches 22 of which they won, and of the four others two were lost and two resulted in draws. They scored 35 goals 54 tries, or 232 point; against 6 goals, 7 tries, oe 33 points, and won the cup with an average of 7.03. In this competion the try getters were: J Butterworth 17; C Butterworth 8; J Faulkner 6 T Warder 5; Wilkinson 4; D Jones 4; S Walker 3; F Gayter 2; and Jolley, H Farmer, Lightfoot, S Houghton, and D Reece, one each. The goal-kickers were: J Jolley 11 dropped, 3 penalty and 3 place goals (17) ; J Butterworth 3 dropped goals; Farmer 1 drop: 2 penalLy, and 8 placed goal 4 (11) ; %%Taylor 2 dropped goals, and Houghton 2 dropped goals. In the cup ties Runcorn scored in seven matches (three of which were re-played ties), 7 goals (20 tries (74 points) ; against 3 goals, 4 tries (18 point). They were knockei out, of the cup by Leeds at Runcorn, by a most fluky try,which put the former 2 points ahead. In the South-West Lancashire Cup Runcorn played three matches (one a re-played tie), scoring 4 goals, 8 tries (32 points); againit. 2 tries (6 points) which St. Helens woh the cup by, after 15 minutes extra time, at Widnes, Of other matches Runcorn played three ; Hull Kingston Rovers, once; and Altrincham twice, All of these matches were won,ttuncarn scaring 8 goals 17 tries (67 points) ; against a dropped goal k 2 points). From the preceding figures it will be seen that the performance of the Runcorn team is not at all overdrawn when it ig called magnificent and deserves all the praise showered upon them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>YOUTHFUL SHOP-LIFTERS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>YOUTHFUL SHOP-LIFTERS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Three Brothers Convicted of Theft.</p>
        <p>At the Ituneorn Police Court, on Tuesday afternoon, before Mes,eis. F. J. N)rman and Stmuel Taylor, three brothers John E Iwin (141, Stanley (11), and Percy (9), sons of Mr. Frederick R 0.71653, Aleoce-street. Rune eat, were chareeed with having stolen a box of cigarettes valued at is. Bd., the propert7 of Mr. W. Shaw, Fin., Top Locks, Rancorn, on or about the Ist of May—Mr. William Shaw. said he was a tobtcceeist and lived at Top Leeks, High-street, Rancorri, where he carried on business. About 4 o'clock on Mondey afternoon he missed four packets of ciearetts,out of a larger box containing 10 which was in his shop window lying exhibited for sale. About 6 o'clock the same night he saw the three prisoners on his door step and t them to go away. At the same time he pretended to run after them and they scampered off. The following morning (Tuesd ty) he was engaged in the shoo, making a board that would fit a hole in the pattition of the slid- ing window whieh cut the shop off from ths window, when his if called his attention to the three children, who were knocking about the shop, saying she felt sure they were the boys who had stolen the cigarettes. Con- sequent upon that he watched them and saw the two bigger bob's wtlk hick cod to in front of the shop. Then he saw the little oafs come into the shop and take the six packets of cigarettes (produced). He caueht the boy and took him into the shop, and then he went and caught the other two and was bringing them in when Constable Gregory appeared on the FC9,le. He might also say that he bad missed, on going through his stcck, three quer ter pound tins of "Westward Hot" and one of "Carlton Flake."—The eldest boy said be was not there the first night.—Constable Gregory said he recsived the ft ree lads in custody from Mr. Shaw and breagnt them down to the Police Station where they made certain statements— (anbmitted)—in writing. Subseq )ently he charged them wide stealing the articles described to which they made , no reply.—The statements of the boys were an admis- Edon of the offence, only etch laid the blame upon the other for instigating the thefts.—The father said he would like to have the boys summarily dealt with.—The boys John and Stanley were stated by Insnector Hicks to have given the police some trouble ever snme hoot stealing at Widnee, for which he believed they were birched.—The Chairman having adeninisteced a severe caution to the boys, sentenced John Edwin and Stanley t one day's confinement in the cells, and a birching of 12 and ex strokes respectively before being released in the morn- ing. The younger one Percy, on promising not to have any further dealings with his wicked brothers was allowed to depart unpunished.</p>
        <p>FRIIR TO RHERINIATIO AND PARALYTIC</p>
        <p>SUFFERERS, an -Illustrated Blow on the oomplete horn Cure of these Disemes, preface by F. W. Cole. M.D., who regards the author as the highest authority. Send stamp for book to the VENO INSTITUTE, 18, DevonshlrA-4treet, Man-</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DIS- TRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly meeting of the above Council took place at the Town Hall,-,Runcorn, on Thursday morning last, there being presedt :—Messrs. IV Handley (chairman), S Taylor, E Gatlev, S Walcon, Fl H Posnett, E Imis,n, C.C., T Jones, J Pritchari, J U Gric,•e, J Sweetman, D Bisbrown, J T Dean, J H Salketcl, with F AlLake (clerk) E Ma.s iall (secretary) and James W,lding (surveyor).</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The number of de4ths ragistered during the past month amounted to 24. Six wer3 due to pneumonia, two to whooping cough, two to rheumatism, two to bronchitis, two to beim disease, two to congestion of lungs, and one each to inflammation of the lining mem- branes of brain, intestinal obstruction, convulsions, parturition, osthm t, pleurisy, erysipelas, and one to uacertain origin. The 24 deaths shower an average death rate for the twelve months of 15 per 1,000 of the esti- mated population. Fony eight births wen' registered. For the corresponding month of last year 44 births, and 15 deaths were tegistered.</p>
        <p>STREET IMPRSVENENTS.</p>
        <p>The Highways and Building Committee recimmended that the footways in the eastErn portion of Egerton-street be recor:structed and the carriaze way re-metalled : that the Lender of the Croft Granite and Concrete Co. ,for the supply and laying of 400 yards of 2in. best ssasoned Croft Adamant Flags, d.-livered a Runelrn, be accepted and that the Seal of the Council be affixed to the con. tract ; that the tenier of M?ssrs El Hays &amp; C. for tho supply of 280 yards of 12in. by 51n. dressed edgings, 280 yards 12M. by 6in. dressed channels, 10 tons of 4in. cube setts, and 33 feet 12in. by 61n. steps, be accepted, and that the Seal of the Council be affixed to the con tract. That the offer of Meisrs. P Walker and Co. to contribute 26 towards the coit of re-flagging footway opposite the Masonic Hotel. Devonshire P.ace, be accapted. The ablve minutes wererconfirmed on the motion of Mr. S. Taylor,scconded by Mr. Imison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NECESSARY PRECAUTION.</head>
        <p>A NECESSARY PRECAUTION.</p>
        <p>The Health Committ-e recommended that handbills be printed and circulated cauti ,nitig persons against puttinz liquids iFaco ashbolum and asopits. Mr. Dean in pvposing the adoption of the above thoa4tit it a most desirable step t) take.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Jones seconded and the resolution was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WATER COMIISITTEE</head>
        <p>THE WATER COMIISITTEE</p>
        <p>Rocornmended toe the sum of £lO7 I2i 53 be written off for empti-s, &amp;3., in respelt of time water rate for the quarter enied 31st ttttrcli last. That the tender of the Wigan Coal and Iron Co. to supply sDtons or West Leigh slack during the month of May, delivered at the Run- corn pumping station, be accepted. That the tender of Messrs. Simpson Davies and Sons, to supply 250 tens of Bridgewater slack during the months of May and June, delivered at the Top Lcts, Runcorn, in such quantities as may be required, be accepted ; and that the Seal of the Council b 3 affixed to the cantract. Tnat the tender of Mr. Robert Harrop, for pointing the eneine house, &amp;c., at the Halton pumping station, be accepted. That the tank at the Runcorn pumping statim be connected directly to the Weston water main at an estimated cost of £5O, for the purpose of improving the supply to Weston.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Pritchard in submitting the above, said they were all aware of the market price of fuel, so that they had only gone in for a contract that would help them over the summer a:oaths. With respect to the connec- tion of the tank at the Roncorn Pumping Station with the Wegton water main that was for the purpose of ensuring a better supply of water to Weston. (Hear. bear.) He also submitted a statement of the receipts and expenditure of the water works for the past year. Mr. Dean said he would vcond, Mr Pritchard and the recommendations were adopted.</p>
        <p>NNW BOOKS FOR THR PUBLIC;</p>
        <p>The Library Committee recommended that 133 volumes of new books, as per list submitted, be pur- chased.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Bisbrown proposed the accaptance of the above resolution. Hitherto the Library Committee had been prevented from providing new books on aceount of lack of funds. The books recornmended were mostly of the historiest class and contained some upon Chester, Chethire and Liverpool, and other interesting local dis- tricts. The cost would be £29 13s Bd.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Grice secondedl and the proposition was most unanimously received.</p>
        <p>THE INDIAN FAIIINE.--EIINCORN DECIDES TO DO ITS DUTY.</p>
        <p>A communication was read from the Cheshire County Council with respect to the Indian Famine and request- ing that the Council open a subscriptio% list in the district. The Chairman felt sure they vrer3 all in sym- pathy with the object (Hear, hear). It a was a pity that the famines in India occurred so frequently, yet he felt that the time was coming when they would be less extensive and as India was more and more opened up with railways and navigation even they &amp;gilt altogether dieappear. Whilst they existed, however, they had a duty to perform to their fellow subjects who were suffering want and starvation, and he felt they would all do their share. (Hear. hear.) He advocated that they should work upon lines somewhat similar to those they had worked upon last year, and seed out circulars to the clergy and ministers of religion askieg t hem for a collection on behalf of the cause, for by that meaes a very substan- tial sum was realised tact year. Mr. Imison agreed with the Chairman and thought he had interpreted the feelings of the Council very cor- rectly. He did not think they could do better than follow the example set them 2 or 3 years ago. He thought that was an opportunity of showing a recipro- cating feeling in return for what the Colonies had done for the mother country. They had all been taking pride in the strong bonds of feeling the Colonies had for the mother country, and this he thought was an opportunity for the mother country to show she appreciated these efforts—(hear, hear)—by making an effort on behalf of her suffering subjects. Nothing could appeal to the people of Runqorn more forcibly and he felt they would respond most heartily. Runcorn had shown a high spirit in the way it responded to the other funds, and no doubt would do its duty ha this (Hear, hear.) The Chairmen then formally prop3sed that the bank be aekei to open a subscription list, and that the Clergy and Ministers be written to. Mr. Imisen seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Posnett di r 1 win the idea of writing to the clergy and ministers. 111 thought confining it to relig- ions bodies rather lessened th 3 operation of the fands.Fle would much rather w,rk on the same principle as with the War Funds, and that employers be written to so that appeals cooll Iv male to the diqsrent works. He for r4ne woald do his bet.</p>
        <p>Mr. S Taylor etoted h nad receive a communiaation from the County Council,also with respect to the Famine Fund and would have sent hie mite on to them. He was, h3wever, more pleased to sce that the Cnairman had brought the matter heron- them and before the people of Runcorn. Everyone in Ronaorn must necessarily have the grcatest sympathy with the object, for to some ex- tent Runcorn traded witn India. (Rear, hear.) When it was put before the people of Runcorn. he had such confidence in them, that he felt sure the? would respond heartily. Speaking of the 'War Funds, he said he hoped, however, there would he no diminution in them, for it would not be until a few ye4rs hence 'lie y would know the full extent of the suffering caused hy that war. That would be when "Tommy Atkins cams home " It was question- able whr-n half of those people returned home whether they would be able to 2,t their work, the other half might drift into the Workhouse, and others would be in such a state that he would would not mention it. The Famine Fund was, however, a good object, and when it was put before the Runcorn public he hoped that some- thing good would come of it. (sear, hear). It Wa3 then unanimously deeded that the *Bank, the Clergy and Ministers of religion, and the emplofers of labour he written to. and asked to do their best for the indian Famine Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>ALLEGED STRANGLING OF A • RUNCORN YOUTH. Prisoner Remanded.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED STRANGLING OF A • RUNCORN YOUTH. Prisoner Remanded.</p>
        <p>Edwin ParkioFol2,. of rr;) reguhr employment, was clicirged in custoty with unlawfully 'and maliciously in- flicting grevous bodily harm upon one Leonard Sargeant at Runcorn, on the 28th of April. Superintendent Pearson, said be oely de3lred to sub- mit enough evidence to secure a remand for eight days.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Miller, wife of Mr. John Miller, Duke Stables, Waterloo-road, Runcorn, said her huaband was the stable keeper. Atlnut 10 G'cluck on the date in question she was in the kitetpla adjoining the stables, in con- sequence of hearing a hig, noise, she went in the direc- tion of the stanles and saw prieover there. He bad the lad (Leonard Sargea.nt), by the throat. The by was oft a form and the man was s'Andieg over him gripping him by the throat. She heard prisoner say to the boy "he would finish him." She was frightened and ran and told her husband. She then heard the man outside where he had the boy on the stones ; the man still had hold of him, and " he would murder him before he finished." Some meu then sppeared and got the lad away from prisoner. Witness had no idea what the two were bothering about. Dr. McDougall was sent for and saw the lad. She heard the doctor say that the lad'e windpipe was broken. The boy (Leonard Sergeant) was then lying in bed at their houie above the stable, taut was unfit to come out at piesent. The doctor bad seen him eince. She woulu think that the lad was about 15 years of age. The prisoner was then rmar.ded until Tuesday nest.</p>
