      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>NEGLECT OF CHILDREN AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday before Mr. Birchall and other magistrates, John Barnes, J Aley's Court, was summoned for neglecting his four children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health on Arril 23r1.—Mr. A. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. and stated that in September of last year th- defendant's wife was sum- moned by the Society f(,r neglecting the children. To their surprise it turned nut tbat the wife was the aggrieved party and it was the husband who had done wrong. At the suggestion of the Chairman of the Bench he withdrew the prosecution and it was considered wt'ether to issue a warrant against the man or not. It decided Eot to do so and the man was told that he would be closely watched and if he did not support the children tha case would be brought again,,t him. Defendant was a coffin maker and could earn good wages. He spent the money in drink. The children had been neglected in a way cal- culated to injure their healti,.—Detendant's wife was called and stated that since September she had not had more than 44 from her husband.—John Barnes, defen- dant's son, said his fa' her had turned him out at nights bscause he would not give him his wages. Witness earned 53 a week and gave it to his mother.—lnspector Hackett stated that he visited the house and found it was very clean as were also the children. They were poorly clad.—Defendant said the children were naver neglectel.—Superintendent Monaghan said that was his 17ch appearance.—The magistrates considered it a very had case and sent defendant to gaol for three mouths with bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>MINOR CASES. Warr'ngton Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. Warr'ngton Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (Alderman Roberte),Couneillor</p>
        <p>Smetnuret. Dr GOrnali. and Mr. t ephei.d.</p>
        <p>A DESERTER.—Jarnes Oliacev was ehargea with being a doserter from the 3rl Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, Cat:oiled at Orford Barracks —An officer from the Barracks ntated that the prisoner gave'himself up at the Barracks as a deserter but as th books were with the regiment at the front they were unable to trace him. lie WAS remanded to await an escort.</p>
        <p>A Row AT UANKEY BRlDES.—Elizabeth Cartwright, 122, Liverpool road was charged with beating and assaulting Eleanor Hughes, of 37, Howard-street and also her son, Edward Hughes on the 23rd inst.—Mr. Browne represented the defendant.—Mrs Hughes stated that, shortly after eleven o'clock on the date named the defendant came and smashed the shop window. Nitness's son went to her and took from her a boot which she had used to break the window. In doing so the defendant struck him in the face and gave him a black see. Witness went to his assistAr.ce and the defendant then assaulted her and injured her lip and mouth. The defendant was "nder the influence of drink at the time.—Edward Hughes alive corroborative evidence The damage to the glass was 15 4d.—For the defence Mr. Browne stated that the affair had arisen in eon- sequence of Mrs. Hughes's conduct in taking a girl into her house who had been staying at the defendant's house. There was no proper accommodation at Hughes's house for the girl, and it was all on account of her son. On the night in question. Mrs. Cartwright went for the girl, and became so enraged necause she could not gain admittance that she broke the window. Mrs Hughes then assaulted the defendant and he would ask the magistrates to say that it was a case of "six of one and half-a-dozen of another. After hearing the de- fendant, and two witnesses named Lucy Holden and Ann Bowley, the magistrates ordered the defendant to pay the costs in each case. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>WIFF: AS .LUI.T.—John Frangleton 14, Scott-street was charged with assaultiug his wifeon the 21st inst —He pleaded guiltv.—The complainant stated that the defendant hit her several times and would have given her a" good thrashing" but for the interference of a young man.—The defendant e.id he only hit her once with his open hand because she was black-guarding him. They had only beets married a few years. --The Ala,. or said it was a pity that they should mar their married life so early and advised them to get away from their moth.r-in-laws,if, as was stated, they were the cause of the quarrel.—lt was decidrd to adjourn the case for a month. and Mr. Taylor. P.C.M. was asked to use his best endeavours to bring the partirs together again. SATURD.A.Y.—Before Messrs T. Birchall (in the chair), B. T.</p>
        <p>_ . Fairclough and J. W. Hayward. CHARGE AGAINST AN BE-CONVICT.—ThOMAS Troy was charged that he being a convict on license failed to retort himself in the months sf December, January, February and March.—Sunerintendent Monaghan said the prisoner was a convict on license up to the 23rd of March. He was originally sentenced to five years penal servitude on the 14th of November, 1894. He was discharged on license on November 2[12.. 1893. On the 16th of January following he was arrested for stealing boots and was committed to the assizes for ttial and on February 12th he was sentenced to a month's im- prisonment and ordered to finish his time* He was released on the 20th December and it was his duty to have reported himself at the first place at which he settled but he Aid not do so. He had not teported himself anywhere since His release and had been tramping about the country. His license ex- pired in Mar3h. Witness had reason to believe that he had been associating with thieves and bad characters.—Prisoner stated tat directly he was released from Dartmoor on the doctor's recommendation he went into a hospital at B. 1401 and remained there 91 days. When he came out his license had expired so he did not think it necessary to report him- self.—The Chief Constable said that even if his time had ex- pired it was prisoner's duty as soma as he came out of hospital to explain to the police why he had not reported himself.— The Magistrates rein.inded the prisoner for a week in order that the police might ascertain if his statement was true or not.</p>
        <p>MoNDLY—Befere Mr. T. Birchall (in the chair), Councillor Smethurst, and Mr. S. Wright. A GGRavaTILD ASSAULT .—John Griffin or Stanton, 2, Ch pel-court. and John Niuiligan.Arnold's-yard,were charged with being arunk and disorderly in Wharf-st,at 3.50 on Satur- day afternoon and with committing an aggravated assault on Anne Blizabeth Moaele,, last from the Workhouse, at the same tirne.—Bvidence was given by P.C. Barnett, P.O. Bruen, the complainant and other witnesses to the effect that tne prisoners violently assaulted Moseley,disflguring her face. They were both under the influence of drink. —Griffin made hts 27th appearance and Milligan had been before the court fire times.—Prisoners were committed to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>FACT,IRY ACT PRCAI- E.C-UTION.—The Warrington and Dis- trict Mineral Water and Bottling Company Limited. Jockey street were summoned for neg,ecting to send to the Factory Inspector for the district,a written notice of their occupying a new factory on March 26th.—Major Roe, the Inspector tor the district prosecuted and 11,1 d that the firm left their old factory at the end of last year. The junior inspector was unable to trace them until the 26th of March when he found that they had been occupying a new factory for about three months. The traue of mineral watt r making was scheduled as a dangerous trade and the firm were under special rules and it was therefore more than ever necess ,ry that, they should give notice when they occupied the premises in order that the factory might be visited. • A little over a week after the Inspector's visit the defendants sent the required notice. It should have been sent in within a month of the date of occusiation.-4. repres ntative ot the firm stated that during the removal there was a great deal of extra work and the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Genuine Cure.</head>
        <p>A Genuine Cure.</p>
        <p>For Coughs, Bronchitis, Influenza, Asthma,</p>
        <p>and Whooping Cough.</p>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE Is the lIIOFt anccexaful remedy in England, vastly superior to the host of commou, cheap cough mixtures or any of the emultiona. It cures as it by some mysterious power, even in seemingly hopeless caue3. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH Colikand see you get it. is. lid. and 2s. 9,1. liold by Boots, Ltd., Knowles, Warrington ; Peake, Earles- towii and Newton; Weston itud Co., Runcorn; Foggitt, Widne3 ; and all Chemists,</p>
        <p>matter was overlooked—a :lite of Is. and costs (13s. in Ell) wa Inflicted.</p>
        <p>GAMBLING.—John qiirehall, 35, Watkin-street ; Henry Brethertorp 51 Winwiek-street. and Edward Hart, jun., 16. St maford-street, wera summoned for playing pitch and toss on April 21e1 et the back of Stamford-street.—P.C. Stuart proved the We —Defendants who said they were playing for marbles were dried iu the costs 3s 6d each.</p>
        <p>UNREGIiTEIIED LODGING HousE.—Mary O'Toole, 20, Ship- yard, WO summoned for ke ping an unregistered lodgiug- house, on April 21,3t.—Inspector Powell said he visited the Iroise of the defendant on April 211 t and found six persons sleeping there. He had previously warned the defendant about the matter,aud had slits applied for a license one would not have been granted because the house was not large enough.—She was fined 103 and 305t5. Alrfoß OFFENCES —Thomas Caldwell. 16?., Church-street was ordered to pay Is 'ld per week towards the maintenance of his son, who is in Hightown Cruaut School.—Thomas Joyce, of 70, tAngford-street, was fined Si and costs ou the evidence of PC though for having been drunk whilst in charge of a horse and lorry on April 23rd.—The following were fined for drunkenness i—lilizabeth Cartwright, :36, Liverpool-road. 103 ; Patrick Gilliok, 17 Bostock-atreet, 53 ; Peter Murray, 6, Morris-yard, lOs and COstS. TITESDIY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair), and Birchall.</p>
        <p>WIFE Asssuur —William Cosgrove, Birchall-street was charged on a warrant with assaulting his wife, Winifred Cosgrove on the 28th April.—Complainant stated that her husband kicked hr in the eye and about the body.—Prisoner was fined 108 and costs 21 2s in all). —Patrick War4l, Bank- street was charged with as-aulting his wife Elizabeth Ward on the 23rd of ilelarch.--Uomplaiaant gave evidence to the effect that prisoner struck her in the face and pulled some of her hair off.—Yrisoner who made his 11th appearance was bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs which amounted to 14s 6d.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAT.—Before Messrs: Gad:Us (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Birchall.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL ASSAULT ON A Wisc.—John Kelly, labourer, pleaded guilty to assauling his wife, Margaret Kelly, on Thesday.—Complainant stated thither husband thrashed her most unmercifully. She rau into Mr. Johnson's yard and he followed, knocked her to the ground and kicked her, ren- dering her insensible. She WAS black an i blue all over her body.—Frederick aukland, a boy in the employ of Mr. John- son, said he saw the prisoner thump his wife in the face, knock her dowse and kick her,—Bdward Barton corroborated. P.C. Stuart stated that he and P.C. Carter apprehsncied the prisoner at 9.45 on Tuesday night. They saw the complain- ant, who was in a very bad state and could scarcely walk. Prisoner was under the influence of drink but was not irunk. He was wearing heavy boots.— Prisone7 stated that on Mon- day mad Tuesday when he came horns from work no meals had been preg•arecl for him. He triought that Was enough to maks any man "wild." His wife went out drinking with other women. He gave her 21s. last week.—Superlntendent Monaghan said that that was the prisouer's ninth appearance. —Prisoner WAS co nmit'ecl t) ga I for three months with hard labour.Complainant applied for a separation order,which was graoted, but no allowance was made as the complainant was in receipt of wages and had no chil nom. PRESTON GIRLS IN TROUBLE. —ALLEGED ATTEMPF TO DEFRAUD THE L. AND N.-W. RAILWAY GoMPANY.—Two girls named Wiry Turner and Maria Snape, of Preston, were charged with travelling on the Lonlon and North-Western Railway from Preston to ;Varringtm witsiout previosisly pay- ing their fares with intent to avoid payment. They pleaded gudty.—lnspeotor Howells. prosecuted on behalf of the railway company, and stated that about ten o'clock on Tues- day night a telegram was received at 13 ink Quay Station from , Preston to the effect that the defendants had got into a third- class compartment at Preston as the train for Warrington was moving, without having purchased a ticket. • On the arrival of the train at Warrington, the prisoners were searched for and were found concealed behind the door of a lavatory. When they were asked for their tickets shey pro- duced Preston platform tickets ana said they wanted to go to London but had no 111011 e to pay the fare. There was a soldier in the same compartment. He did not wi*ll to press the case.—Prisoners said they were seeing the soldier off at , Preston and got into the train to speak to him when it moved off. —Mr. Hon e7ls consented to withdraw the charge on prisoners paying the tare. Dis magistrates agreed to this course and Mr. Taylor P.O. M. promised to sea them back to Preston.</p>
        <p>LIEa 49ra APPEAR&amp;NCE. —Julia An Shields (20), Dial- street, was charged with having been druu.: and disorderly in Dial-street on Tussday afternoon —..C. Leonard proved the case, and Supe,intendent Monaghan said that was prisoners 43t4 appearane.s. and sh-, has been la gaol ten months during the last two years.—She was fined 5s and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions,</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions,</p>
        <p>MONDLY.—Before Messrs. F J. Norman (presiding) and</p>
        <p>William Handley. tRUNKENNE99.—Peter Ferr t,a sailor who had to be locked up by P.C. WPson on Saturday last for safety,because he was so helplessly drunk, was fined 28 61 —Hugh Towie. for being drunk and disorderly in the highways at Sutton, his native place, was also fined 2s St STAFFORDSHIRE MEN INDULGE INA. FIGHT. —Thomas James and klatthew Wenlook. boatmen of Staff mishire pleaded guilty to fightinz, and to being (thank and disorderly on the canal bank ot the Bridgewater Canal at Runcorn on the 18th in4ant.—Oonstable Wilson said that drawn by a disturbance about half past eleven on the date 'given, to the back of the wash-housts near Top Locks, h, found the two defendants there drunk and creating a slisturbance. They wanted to fight. • 'James was the worst of toe two, and gave him con- siderable trouble.—Fined 55 and 8s 61 costs each. A PITIFUL T4LE.—ilarriet Baxter, a wel!-known charac- ter, pleaded guilty to a charge of being drunk and disorderly in Hughe's Court,on the 28th of April.—P.C. Wilding proved the ease, and stated that the behiviour of the woman was re slly disgraceful.—Defendant stated she load a letter from her daughter who resided in Manchester. The daughter had sent her money so that she csuld leave Dutton Workhouse, of which place she was an inmate, and go to live with her in Manchester. She got permission to leave the house, but missed her train, and having got a drop of drink, she acted as he had done. She was very aerry.and if the Bench would let her go, she would go faithfully to her daughter's at Man- chester.—The Chairman Will your daughter receive you after this ?—Defendaot : 0 yes. She is so good to me. The Chairman: ilemembet it will be your last chance You will be dismissed on that understanding.—Defendant: Thank you sirs.</p>
        <p>PROFANERS.—John Burns, and Ann Corcoran, both of Hughe's Court. were charged with using profane or obscene language on the 21st and 2?.nd of April, respectively.—The cases were proved by P.U. Bowyer.—Burns was fined ss. and and 89. 61. costs or 7 days; and Corcoran, who had ap- peared 28 tinvut previously, beforeithe court, was lined, 10s. and Bs. 6d. costs or 14 days.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>._,,,•._. WARRINGTON MILL GIRL IN A DILEMMA.</head>
        <p>._,,,•._. WARRINGTON MILL GIRL IN A DILEMMA.</p>
        <p>Strange indeed was the narrative unfolded to a War- rington reporter during an interview the other Saturday afternoen with a young girl who resides with her parents at 49, Orford-street, Warrington. Although only 14 years of age, she has had her fair share of trouble. Until abut 12 months ago she was as strong and active as any other girl of her age,when rheumatism developed suddenly in her feet and knees,and eventually she fond herself unable to walk, having, of curse, to give up her employment, which was that of a weaver at Messrs. Armitage and Itigby'e (Cockhedge Mills), within the shadow of which she resides. Miss Alice Cape, (a daughter of Mr. James Coupe, a well-known and re- spected EnaP.on) is an iatelligent young lady.and she told her experience to the ir.quiring pressman in a si nple and graphic manner. " I have to thank Mrs. Whittaker, a neighbour," she commenced, "for helping me ever my d,fficulty. I was laid up for about a month with rheu- matisrnsand I vainly uPed embrocations.cod-liver oil.and other remedies in trying to obtain relief. Then the neigh- bour I have mentioned to'd me of Dr.Williams' pink pills for wile people,and I resolved to try them,and after the</p>
        <p>first box I found I was regaining my strength. Thus encouraged, I continued ta take the pills, and after I had had three boxes I was able to return to my work. I have had two boxes since, and I am now as well as ever." This ended the short bit pregnant narrative.</p>
        <p>QuEstionel by the reporter, she said she had to dis- contieue her work at the mills for an )ut two months, being compelled to take to her bad for soma weeke,and was unable to eat anything prepared for her, Then she began to take the pills, and she was soon able not enly to get downstairs. but to enjoy her food ; in fact, to quote bar own words, she " was always ready for her meals, breakfast, dinner, or tea." Now she is as active as ever, both in the mill and at home, and she declares that she shall always feel grateful that her friend recom- mended her to try the efficacy of Dr. Williams' pink pills which she evidently highly appreciates,a box being shown to the pressman for his inspection. " Do you feel thoroughly restored to health ?" was one of the questions put to her, and her answer came quickly. " thoroughly. I used to feel so tired and de- pressed. Life to me is now quite pleasant. I used to lie in bed taking no interest in anything, but now I can sing (and she appeared to be very fond of music) with as much heartiness as ever. It was a great punishment to me to have to lie on the sofa, even when I was able to leave my bed." Still further interrogated, she said : "I have no doubt I owe my recovery entirely to these (exhibiting the box of pills), and I shall always recem- mend them to my friends, as I have done already to the ' girls' in the mill." Mr. and Mrs. Coupe,who ere naturally greatly rejsiced at the recovery of their danghter, were present during the above interview, and informed the reporter that they attributed Alice's recovery entirely to the use of the pills. "And you think you owe a duty to the public to make these facts known 2" " That is why," was the response, "we are teling yen all these facts. Our child has certainly benefited by the use of the pills, and we are so thankful that we wish others to be benefited also." Full permission was given to the pressman to make known this wonderful recovery, and he has done so ; and he can also persoually testify to the fact that when he saw Miss Coupe on the afternoon of the 10th February she was " the picture of health."</p>
        <p>:</p>
