      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>St. Helens First Holders. RUNCORN V. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>St. Helens First Holders. RUNCORN V. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The W!chaes football ground was well patronised, on Monday eveniwg last, whtn, the Runcorn team met St. Helens in the final for the S mth West Lancashire and Berder Towns Cup. Runcorn were minus J Faulkner Wilkinson, Lightfoat and Reece, of their regular team ; but the Saints were almost fully reprtleated. At 6.30 the teams presented themselves and lined out as under: St. Helens Foulkes, back ; Barnes, Traynor, Liver- sage, Doherty, three-quarters ; Appleton and Boyle. half backs ; PrescAt, Green, Briars, Renney, MAryn, Dale, Thompson, and Whitehead, forwards. Runcorn : Houghton, back; C Butterworth, J Butter- worth, T Warder, and Jones. three-quarters; Richardson and Jolley, half backs; 0 Hugnes, Farmer, Taylor, Walker, Darlington, Gayter, Tomlinson, and S Langley, forward. Referee: Mr. McCutcheon•</p>
        <p>Traynor commenced operations, against sun and wind before about 4,000 spectators, and at once evidences ef a good game were visible, for it was a considerable time before one side could claim any real advantage. F iulkes w..s called upon to save, once he kicked dead, and again he dashingly got away with a most useful effort. A series of brilliant efforts on the part of Liversage, Bane and their backs found theiit dangerously near the Run corn line, though it was not long before the visit was returned and Runcorn secured a footing near the Saints 25 yards line. Jolley was ualucky enough to get his right leg injured, but resumed shortly after. At this point Runcora made a general hash of things and though posses,ing the support of the elements, by far the best dispaay of the game was given by S. Helens. As time wore on, however, a little im- provement was noticeable, and the first chance was secured by Warder who got close enough to tp.zard a drop. Boyle, Traynor and Doherty were then notice- able fora fine concerted movement on the right wing. rraynor again performed yeoman eervice,but Houghton at length beginning to realise what was required of hie., responded grandly and Foulkes was for the second time compelled to kick dead. Runcorn then held a slight advantage and scrummaging was very prevalent near the Sainta' posts. Farmer retired to the dressing-room for repairs, having had one of his ribs broken. The game lagged on with little or no appreciable difference and return after return was made. Exciting play by S. fle!ens was in progress, when Houghton took upon him- self to shine, and finished up a capital piece of work just as the wuist!e souaded "interval." The teams then crossed ever with a clean sheet.</p>
        <p>Restarting, with Farmer m :sing from the front rank, it was not long before St. Helens !tecured a chance to score from a penalty. 'rreynor'e eff et, however, went wide. Doherty was very instrumental and ran sp!en- di ; but, though he gainee a goo I po3i lose for the Seints, they failed t ) improve things. This was as much due perhaps to the capital defence Runcorn displayed at that time. Jelley head dan attack upen tee Sc. Helens' goal, but no long stey eves made, Foulkes proving a werthy custedian. Footwork ot a very hign order re-estabished the Saints in a goed quarter, and capital passing resulted between the whole of tle.ir backs. Tne Rune irn defence responded finely. Witte remarkable energy the Ruecorn fourteen then brought about an invasion of the Sainte' lines, which was admirably sus- tained for a few moments. Neither side could gain an advantage, and mid-hell play fel owed for a considerable time. Tomlinson got his knee badly hart, and theugh reduced to thirteen men, Rene an for a time more than held their own. A superb effort en the part of toe Run- corn forwards further improved matters, yet every me could see the state of Chines could not last long. Jones an-I Jolley also fell victims t 3 bruises, but after a few momenta of attention re,umed. Play then gravit a ted to the centre and bonqurS were even. Totnlea;ou returned when there was but tour minutes to play and immediately Feifikes was called upon to defend. St. Helens headed by Doherty and Briars then rushed to the Runcorn end, oely, however, to be sent to the right about. St. Helens presd aud though they had a read, good chance, failed on account of the splendid defeeee opposed to them. Relieving the pressure Runcern were returning to the attack, but a capital couat.er-m3ve on the part of Doherty nullified things. Several scru,n- magas then took place right upoe toe Runcorn line and thiegs looked ugly when time came as a happy release. At the conclusion of the regular time neither Seam had scored.</p>
        <p>It WaS decided to play an extra fifteen minutes—(7i each way) Runcorn fac.cl tne inevitable with but 12 men—Fert.ner, Darlington and Tomlinson being unable to resume—aud it was not to be wondered at, that St. Helens ran in two tries. Appleton bins! responsible for the first, and Birnes for the mecmd. Neither were con- verted. Tee final result vvas 8;. Helms 2 tries (6 points); Runcorn nit.</p>
        <p>THE PRESENTATION OF THE OUP. After the match Mr. J. El. Smith, Widnes, made the presentation of the beautiful cup to the whiners. In doing so he said : In the absence of Mr. Underwood, the President of the South Lancashire and Border Towns cup competition, be had been asked to come for- ward and present the cup to the winneri. (App'ause). He had been called upon to perform th,t duty at a moment's notice, therefore his remarks would necessarily be short. The competition they had just witnessed re- minled him of the old day s—(ctieers. apd " Good old Smith.")--He thought it would not bo too much to say that that competition had done mach for football in the South West Laueashire District, when Rugby football was in its infancy, and there were tut five or six clubs in the district. (Applause.) It had been started too late perhaps that year to be as suncessful as it might have been, but still he could not help thinking there was room for such a competition, and it would be better next year. (Loud applause.) He asked Mr. Foulkes (the St. Helens captain) to accept the cup for the first tune it had bean won. (Cheers.)—Mr. Foulkes responding said he was very pleased to held the map. It was the 'first ho had ever had as captain, though he exp,octcd it would not be the last. (Laughter and hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The gathering then broke up, and as the:St. Helens men went off the ground they were most enthusiastic- ally cheered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CHESHIRE COUNTY UNION.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE COUNTY UNION.</p>
        <p>Junior Challenge Cup Competition</p>
        <p>Runcorn Recs. v Runcorn Hornets.</p>
        <p>Oa Monday evening these local tea:ns contested the right to the Juni)r Challenge Cup which has been pre- -B.ntd for competition among the junior clubs of Cheshire by the Northern Rugby Union. The match teak place on the Irwell-lane eecio,ure, before a goo.l atteedanee of spectators, having regard to the fact that the Runeorn tfam were engaged with St. Helens on the other side of the river for possession of the West Lancashire and Border Towns Chat. lenge Cup. The teams faced each other under the direction of Mr. F. Turner, of Warring- ton, their cenapresition being as follows: Runcorn Recs., Thomas back ; Kenyon, Moron, Molyneox, legle3field, three-quarters ; Wright and Brown, hall-backs ; Mac- donald, Peacock, Wiiliamaon, F JOII-23, Boyle, Davies, T Faulkner, and Joyce, forwerds : Runcorn Hornets : Johnsen, back ; Urrnson, Woods, Green and Huehes, three-quarters ; Sefton and Ellis, half-backs ; Johns Moulton, Robinson, Garrett, Banner, Shaw, Darr and Hargreaves, forwards. The Hornets kicked off, the ball going into touch. From the kick out play was carried into the Hornets' half. A series of scrurninagee ensued in close proximity to the line, and Brown scored aUgoal. The Rtrs quickly chang-d the scene, and the Hornets' lines were again assaulted, one of the Rees' players almost gttting over. The game continued to be fairly even, beth ends being visited in turn,the Rns. having got into their opponents' half when the whistle called half-time. The Recs. were then leading by one goal to nil. After the interval the Recs. attacked strongly, and following considerable scrummaging they knocked over the corner flag in the struggle for a try. The Hornets forced the game to the other end, and a smart piece of combination saw Woods get over at the corner. The goal-kick failed. The game ended in a win for the Hornets by the narrow margin of one point.—At the cone:whoa of the match the spectators gathered round the stand to witness the presentation of the eup, and as the winning team took their seats they were vociferously cheered. Mr. W Carroll on behalf of the Cheshire c)unty complimented Runcorn Hornets on becoming the proud possessors of this cup.</p>
      </div>
