      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>„S"MfM:Z*;,I</head>
        <p>„S"MfM:Z*;,I</p>
        <p>i_MSI,Cr,*FSI"?;?77,',V,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>CLUB.</p>
        <p>REVIEW OF THE SEASON.</p>
        <p>[By "Oval i sal . Th nigh they have not wound up the season in a blaze of glery, the Warrington Football Club have made a alight improvement on the previous year's record, scoring two points more in the Lancashire League, and having a vastly improved point average to show. Rather more progress too, was made in the cup-ties, but, of course, the cup did not come Warrington way. Teem can be no do-abt, either, that the team has been the best the Warrington Club have put in the field since the inauguration of the Northern Union. These are, briefly, the principal sources for any self-congratulation the Club are en- titled to on the season's work. They have not yet got a credit balance of performances,for the failures still outnumber the successes, and they will probably continue to do so until there is more keenness shown to annex honours as well as points. Somehow or other Warrington are always bad starters. Instead of opening the season in their best form they perform so erratically that hope of finishing at the top is extinguished almese by the end of the first mouth. Some people would not heeitate to say that there never was any such hope or prospect, but they are mistaken. Giver' a capable team and the requisite determination any club in the League may win its way to the championship. I always feel inclined to blame the Warrington club for the evident lack of interest they show in the destina- tion of the Lancashire cup. They are satisfied to occupy a position in the upper half of the table, and appear to have no ambition to achiev e anything more than an improvement on the previous season's record. It is here that the whole ground for com- plaint lies. The club should start each season with a full determination to try for the championship. It is not hopeless by any means, and the tact that the teams of all the talent (imported), are not bound to win is proved by the success of the home-grown and moderetely-paid Run- eeeniane. What Runcorn have done Warring- ton can do if they only show the same determina- tion and dauntless pluck. But to succeed they will have to start the season with the intention of throw- ing uo chances away, instead of completing the fix- tures as they come with no particular keenness about the result. In the season just concluded War- rington made the customary indifferent start, win- ning two, and drawing one in the first month. The opening match was at St. Helens, where a draw was not at all a discreditable performance, but Leigh came to Warrington the following Saturday and plunged the home supporters into sorrow. Thus thiege went on, fortunes varying from week to week. Strange to say Warrington lost their title to fame as a powerful home club, for between October 28 and February 21 not a single competition match was won by the Warsiugton men on their own ground. Nearly half the seeson and not a single point to cheer the hearts of the people who wentjto watch at Wilderspool ! Had it not been for an equally surprising measure of success away from nome the hearts of tee local lovers of the game would have been very low indeed. Almost invari- ably, Warrington played twice as strongly and skil- fully away from their own excellent enclosure, and many of the supporters never really saw the team ac its best. Victories on the grounds of the Leigh, Wigan, Stockport, Tyldeeley, and Broughton Rangers clubs indicate Warrington's prowess when away. The run of ill-luck at home was broken on February 21 by the defeat of Oldham who were emetically put out of reach of the cup by the brilliant performance of the Warriagtonians en this occasion. They were quite at their best and Oldham had a very poor chance of success from the commencement. This, and the victories at Leigh and Broughtou were about Warrington's best per- formances in the League. The Northern Union Cup ties brought Warrington and St. Helens to- gether for the second time at Knowsley-road, and this time Warrington proved their superiority in unmistakeable fashion. Round 2 saw the Goole team at Warrington,and the homesters took the liberty of piling on their record score in competition football. Seven goals, ten tries, 44 points,was the total at the olose, the hapless visitors being allied in the bar- gain. Then came that woeful third round in the Rochdale swamp when Warrington lost their really good chance of reaching the final by a try to noth- ing. Just when the season appeared at an end from a Warrington point of view, the old West Lanca- shire Cup Competition was revived, and Warring- ton entered the arena. They ran right through the Competition without a defeat, but they did not get the cup. Why? Well, they never played. Swin- ton cancelled the first round, Warrington withdrew from the next (the semi-final) and the cup was won by St. Helens, who defeated Runcern. The Com- petition was really farcical, owing to the idiotic mismanagement of it by those in authority. So chaotic were the arrangements that half the people in South Lancashire were in doubt on the very evening of the final whether it was to be at Warrington or Widnes. Of course it was at Widnes. We don't get finals at Warrington. The Warrington Committee do not come out very creditably in this competition. Had they not been so particular aoout their full share of gate-money the semi-final would have been played, won, and I doubt not we should have had the Cup in town by now. There was the old fault in evidence; not sufficient ambition. It would have been a capital , climax to an indifferent season if the Club had annexed even the West Lancashire Cup,but it went a-begging. This is not the way to win support— which has been very loyal all the season—and the committee would have been well-advised had they forfeited something in order to become cup-winners. Turning from performances to play, I think it will be generally admitted that the style of the Warrington team has much improved durieg the season just closed. Better football and more scientific methods have been very apparent, and nothing indicates the improvement more plainly than a comparison between the points for and against in the two last seasons. In 1898-9, Warrington scored 16 goals 34 tries, 134 points, against 32 goals 51 tries 217 points. This year they have finished up with 24 goals 42 tries, 174 points against 16 goals, 32 tries, 128 points. The balance is at last on the right side, and it is entirely due to the improved play of practically the same team as did duty in the previous year. Fish has mode the most rapid strides, and as a wing-threequarter I think he stands unsurpassed in the County Palatine. Isherwood and he together are perhaps the best com- bined wing Warrington have ever possessed. Among the new players of the season are Mereweather (Bristol), a sterling forward, Smith and Jones (Wales) threequartere, and Burgess (Flixton) half back-. The latter is the pick of the new backs and has made a capital partner for Bate dur- ing the latter end of the season. Smith and Jones were excellent during the short time they played together, and if the former could have kept steady the team would have been much strengthened. The same team, if available next season, ought to do honour to the club, especially as we may hop,e. to see the In eakneeees which still exist, remeeied.. Another point to the credit of the club is the revi- val of the Works Competition, for which 23 local teams entered. The interest shown by players and spectators was conclusive evidence of the value of such a competition in the interests of the Warring- ton Club and Rugby football locally. Messrs. Monks Hall's team* won the medals after: a bard fight with the employees of Iviessrs. liichmond and Co.</p>
        <p>The following statistics in connection with the club will be of interest:— Altogether, includinz friendlies and cup ties with the League gamos, Wlrrington played 37 rnatches,of which 20 were woo, 16 lost, and 1 drawn. The total score for</p>
