      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 4 1900</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 4 1900</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE IN WARRiNGTON•</p>
        <p>In presenting the 23rd annual report of the School Attendance Committee, the Committee states that it presents no special features. The average attendance shows a slight but steady increase; sickness among childien of school age having been the main factor in keeping down the returns.</p>
        <p>The Chief Inspector reports that the prosecutions ',don the magistrates were 303, being an increase of 93 compered with the previous year. This increase is more apparent than real, in consequence of the large number of cases adjourned for improvement and afterwards withdrawn, each CASO thereby count- ing as two. An examination for labour certificates was lied at the Wycliffe School, Bewsey-street ; 181 children were examined, of whom 109 passed, the percentage of passes being about 80 the same as last year. There is a great falling off in the number of applicants to sit at this annual examination, there being a general dipeosition on the part of the parents to keep the children at school until 13 years of age or over, when they claim the certificate of previous due attendance. Over 700 of these certificates h ave been issued during the year. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The number of sOhildieu for whom accommodation is provided in the elementary schools of the Borough is 13,647. an increame of 48 as compared with last year. More accommodation is sadly needed in the Latchford, Orford, and St. John's Wards, nearly every sohool in these districts being overcrowded, owing to the extensive building operations which have taken place during the past two years. In the centre of the town there is a surplus of accommodation for nearly 1,500 children, but parents will not send their children to these schools if they can get accommodation nearer home. There is no doubt that the question of increased acaoremodation will have to be faced at an early date if this rapid increase of eopulation continues. The average attendance for the year is 9,903, being an increase of 32 compared with last year. The number on the books is 12,414, being an increase of 357 on I.st year, and giving a percentage of 30. There are 1,730 children under the age of five years who ci..) not make 50 per cent, of attendance, and over whom the committee have no jurisdiction,and who assist in materially redw- ing the percentage of attendance. There are also 447 children attending the Grammar School, Blue Coat School, and private schools within the borough, rnakine a total of 13,271. On the other hand, there are 180 cnildren attending public or private schools within the borough who reside outside, and this number should be dediseted from the total, giving an attendance of chil- dren residing within the borough of 13,091. There is a slieht inerea.se in the.nutnl,Pr of free places, the numbers being 4,774 against 4,647 last year, an increase of 125.</p>
        <p>In October last, after the usual Labour Examination, the Chief Inspector commenced a labour bureau for boys and girls in order to assist boys and girls qualified for employment in obtaining situations: He issued a circular to the shopkeepers and tradesmen of the town, acquainting them of the same. A very sati3fictory re- sult had been obtained, over 200 boys and girls having obtained situations by this method. It had also been the means of preventing children being employed who had not obtained the nqcessary certificate. At the present time the demand for boys far exceeds the sup- ply."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSEUM. MUSEUM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSEUM. MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>Prosh Loans and Other Additions.</p>
        <p>A temporary addition of a very interesting character has just been made to the Art Gallery by the placing there of an oil painting by G L Seymour, entitled "Irene," which has been lent for exhibition by the Mayor of Warrington. The picture his only recently been acquired by its present owner, who has kindly per- mitted it to be placed in the g.llery for a short time before it is hung upon his own walls.</p>
        <p>As a pendant—to-the three cases of Persian, Indian and Japinose pottery on loan from the Victoria and Albert Museum, South Kensingten, a case has been filled with Chincse pottery and porcelain from the cabinets of Mr J S Sherratt and Mr R Proud. This opportune loan enables the Museum boshow at one time a representation of each of the four Ocat classes of Oriental ceramic art.</p>
        <p>The oppertunity 'May be taken to refer to 50... e other additions recsntly made to tee Museum : A cast of Warrington Wood's "Oberon and Titania," presented by Lady Mary Crosse, has been placed in the Art Gallery. In the department of local antiquities a case has been set apart for the uniforms and accoutrements of the local regiments of Volunteers of a century ago, and to the epeeimens previously exhibited have been atided uniforms of the Warrington Volunteer Infantry of 1803 (lent by Mr. Lyon, of Appleton Hall), and the Newton Volunteers of the same period (presented by Mr B Pierpoint M.P.) Considerable additions have been made to the ethnographical department, a number of musical inetruments,beat models and other specimens having been recently acquired which has enabled a more expanded arrangement to be made. In zoology fresh arrangements hsve been effeeted to illustrate the two great classes of arthropoda arid batrachla by means of selected specimens, descriptive labils, and coloured diagrams, the idea being to provide such a summary of the characters of the animals included in each of those groups as will be interesting and intelligible to any sixth standard schoolboy.</p>
      </div>
