      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>The Victory at Houtnek, Enemy's Losses. Telegram from Lord Roberts. From Lord Roberts to Secretary of State for War. BLOBMFONTEIN, MAY 2, 1-30 P.m. General lan Hamilton met with considerable suc- cess yesterday, and drove the enemy out of the position they had taken up at Houtnek with com- paratively small loss to us. The Boers dispersed in several directions, mainly east and north, leaving 26 prisoners in our handp, including one commandant and 16 other wounded men.</p>
        <p>Hamilton is now encamped at Jacobsrust. As his men needed rest after having been fighting for seven out of the last ten days, I ordered him to halt for to-day. Broadwood's Brigade of cavalry arrived on the scene in time to afford valuable assistance by threatening the enemy's rear, and during the after- noon lan Hamilton was joined by Bruce Hamilton's Brigade of Infantry. The enemy admit to having Ltwelye killed and -forty wounded yesterday. Among the former is Lieutenant Gunther, a Ger- man officer belonging to the 55th Regiment; amongst the latter Musumoff, the Russian com- mander of the Foreign Legion. Twenty-one out of fifty-three of the enemy's casualties occurred amongst the members of that legion. Two Frenchmen were amongst the killed. Hamilton speaks in high terms of the good ser- vice performed by the Bt-,h Hussars under Colonel Clowes, and a made up regiment of Lancers, which came into Broadwood's Brigade and assisted in making the Boers evacuate their position. 'Poe final stroke to the enemy's rout was given by the Gordons and two companies of the Shropshire Light Infantry, cheering loudly when they got to within 200 yards of the position, Kitchener's Horse is also spoken of in terms of praise.</p>
        <p>Mafeking Practically Relieved. • A Central News Telegram dated Lourenco Mar- quez,Tuesday, says :—The relief of Mafeking is stated to be practically completed by the with- drawal of Commandant Eloff and a large number of Boers from the investing force. Commandant Eloff has returned to Pretoria. The War Office has issued the following : Lord Roberta to Secretary for War. BLOEMFONTEIN, MAY 2, News received from Baden-Powell, April 20 :" Al! well."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Warrington Volunteers Enter Barracks.</head>
        <p>Warrington Volunteers Enter Barracks.</p>
        <p>The contingent of volunteers of the Ist South Lanca- shire Regiment who have been selected for service in South Aftica entered Orford Barracks,Warrin2ton,where they will undergo training prior to embarkatiora, on Monday morning. The men paraded at the Drill Hall, at ten o'clock in marching order with helmets and serges and were inspected by Major Boerne, the adjutant. Headed by the band of the battalion they then marched to the Barracks,large crowds of people having assembled on the route. The men presented a very smart appear- ance which was generally commented upon. Major Buckten and Lieut. Roberts were present at the Drill Hall.</p>
        <p>Liverpool Volunteers at Warrington. The Sib V.B. Special Service Draft from Liverpool numbering twenty men, with Lieutenant Mahoney in command, arrived at Warrington on Monday afternoon and went into Orford Barracks for training prior to embarkation for South Africa. The special service draft of the sth (Irish) V B. lac, Liverpool for Warrington in the evening and also went into Barracks. They num- bered 20 men and had Lieutenant J. J. L. Ferris in com- mand. Both sections had a roost enthusiastic send-off om Liver pool.</p>
        <p>Letter from Lieutenant Lewis. Major Lewis, Widnes, has received a letter from his son, Lieut. Lewis, who went out to South Africa in command of the 2nd V.B.S.L.R. The letter is written from Elandslaagte Camp, and is dated April 5. In the course of his letter Lieut. Lewis states that ho "had the Erst taste of real work in Ladysmith Camp." The cm- pasty was engaged on outpost duty. It was not very exciting, as we knew there were no Boers within 15 or 20 miles of the spot, but sleeping out and tumbling off kopjes (you can't sail it walking) trying to find sentries in the dark was quite exciting enough to begin with. The brigade is under General Wynne, and forms part of Sir Charles Warren's Division. In conclusion,Lieutenant Lewis says all the Widnes and St. Helens men are pretty Et.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Man in Hospital. Writing from Pietermaritzburg to his brother who lives at Warrington, Private William Farrington, of the let South Lancashire Regiment says :—"I have been in three engagements and gct through them with only a slight wound on the left knee. When we relieved Lady- smith I was one who assisted in capturing 54 Boers under a heavy fire. We got away with 52 in less than half an hour and there were two wounded. They were asking us to put down our arms and we were asking them to put down theirs. Presently a shower of bullets came whizzing by us. We got down to it and fairly wiped them out. Fifty four were left and 25 of us took them to General Warren. He threw his bat up in the air and shontsd " elood old Ladcashires." We made a ring around them for the night. I was "perished" with cold after the hard fighting. My shirt dried on me. Next morning we took the prisoners half way to Durban. Another regiment took them the remainder. We were fairly done up and when we got back I was bad with fever and I had to go in hospital. I got blanket and a stretcher/ for a bed for nine days. I thought I was finished. I told the doctor I would rather be shot than die there. Next day they sent me to Pietermaritzburg. When I got in bed I thought I was in heaven after being for two months in trenches with it raining in torrents. We were up to the knees in water some nights. I never saw it rain in the day time. It i 4 a curiotis thing that some men who got shot as far back as December have been doing duty as their wounds were?, healed and now they have come back to have the bullets taken out. They have the Rontgen Rays here. They should have taken them out before now so they will have to go through more punishment."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Warrington Policemen near Bloemfontein.</head>
        <p>Warrington Policemen near Bloemfontein.</p>
        <p>The following is a copy of a letter sent by P.C. Howard, a member of the Warrington Police Force, who is serving under Lord Roberts with the Grenadier Guards in the Free State :—"Grenadier Guards, South Africa, March 25th, 1900. Dear comrade.—Just a line in answer to your letter. I was very pleased with the papers that yott sent me, as we don't get much news here. We have been on the march for about four weeks, and letters have been following us about all the time. The Boers are all over the show like a flock of sheep and won't stand their ground for us. When they heard that we were close on Bloemfontein they all left the town, which made it easier for us, and we marched straight into the place. We were in an awful state ; we had not had a shave for months and had been without a wash for about three weeks. The troops were worn out. I expect we will get new uniforms at this place, i as those we are wearing are n rags. Hurst (another Police Reservist) was employed on the railway about i three weeks ago, and I fancy he s going to stay there until the war is over. I think he 1”cl his last pop at the Boers, but lam still doing the ordinary duty. We are at a place called Glen, about 15 miles from Bloemfontein and are with Lord Roberts. We had another bit of bad luck about two days ego. Our Commanding Officer (grenadiers), adjutant, and a lieutenant went out of came) and were attacked by a party of Boers. The ad- jutant was killed and the other two severely wounded. This is the second adjutant we have bad killed. I have not much more news to give you. We have bad no fighting since the Boers blew up the railway bridge at this place. We are building a temporary structure to be ready for the next advance, so that we will have the trains brineine our food up with Us. I can sea we will have to fiel.t all the way to Pretoria, but there will be no Boer Republics when we are done with them, as we will wipe them off the face of the earth. They are a dirty lot. Th et is all I have to say at present. I send my best wiehe.a to all the members of the Force, and re- main yours sincerely, SAMUEL HOWARD.'</p>
        <p>What the 2nd Cheshire are Doing. One of them Writes from the Front.</p>
        <p>They had a rough passage for a start, tut on reaching Capetown the men on too re-nolo wers in good heath. Upon landing, the men were given a bag of grapes by the recertion committee which Droved a eery welcome item. The Cheeteres teen pr ceded to " pitch " th, ir camp at a place called Greeu Point, about half a mile from Capetown. There they were stationed for eight days and had stiff work to lace, being teken out every morning for a route march of about 10 miles with full kit on. As most of this was over mountains there was a lot of grumbling, but on the whole the men got used to it and the writer says it makes a man's limbs as strong as a horse. Next the Cheshires were ordered up couatry to Nieuwpoort Camp, Cape Town being left by train-8 men in each compartment. There they had to eat, and sleep for three days and two nights. The food on the jour- ney was bully beef and biecuits which went down all right. The journey proved very interesting, especially when one could jump nut of the train and pick up melons and grapes whilst in motion. The country passed through was very mountainous, and often the Company had to alight, whilst the train ascended the hills vrich small portions of the men at a time. Upon arrivine at Naiesee- peort the company found a camp of about 10,000 men, including wounded and prisoners. The place was very unhealthy, and it was a common thing to see 3or 4 funerals every day. One night thee buried one chip whilst his brother was looking on not knowine at thetime he was watching his brother's funeral. After four days at that camp the Cheshires ceere ordered to Bloem- fontein. They started on the 28th of Mach, and after four hours' ride reached Norvals Port—one of the ferriss across the Orange River —where they found that the bridge across the river which is 475 yards long and 265 feet high (all iron) had been blown to pieces by the Boers in the centre, and a gap of 100 yerds was made which stopped the treffic altogether. Camp was pitched and with the 6,000 already there, the gallant sons of Cheshire entered ugon the arduous duties of assisting the engineers to build a railway pontoon. This work of loading stone and cutting rivets is fittingly described as "hard graft" the consolation for which is visions of more pay. Talking about the food Private J Fennah (the writer) says they are only allowed Ilb of bread a day and that seon goes sour. Stewed" Billy goat," and mule constitutes dinner, and it aint much to work on" ii Fennah's experience. As the writer addresses his letter to Mr. Outhwaite (the Runcorn Volunteer Bandmaster), it is only natural he should con- clude -wishing the band every success, and hoping to be back in the autumn to have another rasp at his favourite " Brass trombone.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Wesleyan Missionary's Interesting Letter.</head>
        <p>A Wesleyan Missionary's Interesting Letter.</p>
        <p>The following letter was reeeiyed by Mr. Daniel Big- brown, Runcoru, from the Rev. S. H Ravens.croft (his brother-in-law), who ;s engaged as a Wesleyan Mis- sionary in S -iuth Africa :—" Indaleni, via Richinceid, April 3rd, 1900. My dear brother and sister,—We like you are tired of the war, but we are prepared to suffer further inconvenience, if needs be, in order that the conflict may be brought to a satisfactory conclusion. We are indignant with a great deal of the "stop the war " party. If their ideas were carried out no English- man with any self-respect could remain in South Africa, for the Boers have not yet ben driven over the border from Natal. We owe the present war very largely to the stopping of the Boer war of 1880-1. If we had con quered them,before we gave back the Transvaal to them they would have respected us., As it was they despised us, and were ready to try conclusions with us again. The war should be carried on until the British flag Iles in Pretoria. There should be no more independent republics side by side with British Colonies. If a shred of independence is left to the B &gt;ers, there will be a more bloody II war than the present one later. Both the Transvaal and the Free State have failed to make a right use of the powers given to them by Britain, so that power (the power to mis-govern) must be takee away from them. If S:ead and Co., had lived here for 20 years, as I have done, and if they had had their houses looted to say nothing of many other troubles which we have passed through, they would write and speak differently of the Beers and their ways. Our friend—the enemy—wants the world to believe that Natal and Cape Colony were invaded to guard the independence of the Dutch Republic. Surely we must be living near to the descendants of Ananias and Sapphire. I sent an article to the Editor of the " Recorder " entitled " Some Medern Battle Fields in Natal." I told him that if he had not space for it he must send it to you. If you should vceive it, you can get it printed in one of the local papers. The visit des- scribed was full of interest. All here are well. Remem- ber us to Runcorn friends. I may have to retire from here. If so, I will let yen know. Lizzie joins in love to you all, and I remain, your affectionate brother, SAMUEL H. RAVENSCROFT."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>_-(4..,._......., MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>_-(4..,._......., MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Captain Silverthorne, reserve of officers, who was a short time ago serving at Warrington, as Adju- tant of the Ist Volunteer Battalion South Lanca- shire Regiment, has taken over the appointment of adjutant of the Ist Battalion Royal Northern Reserve Regiment, while Captain Paine, 3rd Bat- talion Cheshire Regiment has become adjutant of the 2nd Battalion Royal Northern Reserve Regi- ment. Lieutenant Denham, Liverpool Regiment, who has just been transferred from the Depot Companies at Warrington to the Service Companies, has now been formally promoted to the rank of Captain and appointed to command a company in the new 4th Battalion Liverpool Regiment. Sergeant-Major P. Hackett, 2nd Battalion Liver- pool Regiment and who prior to becoming a war- rant officer served for a time at the Depot at War- rington, has been given a commission as Quarter- master with the honorary rank of Lieutenant, becoming Quartermaster of tbe new 4th Battalion of the regiment now being formed. Lieutenant Hackett became Sergeant-Major of the 2nd Bat- talion in December 1895 after a long period of service in the ranks. He is a smart soldier who has well earned his commission. Lieutenant Colonel Curteis, who has just com- pleted his term of service in command of the 2nd Battalion Cheshire Regiment has been promoted Brevet Colonel and will retain command of his corps until the end of the Boer war. Lieutenant- Colonel Graham will then take over the command of the post of second in command and the battalion will be given to Major W. C. Neville who is best known locally as a former adjutant of the old let Cheshire Militia. Captain G S Haines will also now be promoted major in the Cheshire Regiment and he was lately serving in this district as adjutant of the old 2nd Cheshire Militia Battalion.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Jupp has taken over the appointment of Inspector of army schools in this district on transfer from Dover. Major Prendergast, 2nd Battalion South Lanca- shire Regiment being now employed on the staff in India Lieutenant Green, will it is anticipated be promoted to the rank of Captain in the South Lan- cashire Regient and will take over command of a company in the 2nd battalion. 11e joined the South Lancashire Regiment in December 1891. The detachment of the 2nd Battalion South Lan- cashire Regiment left behind when the corps went to South Africa and which detachment is now at Bradford will be located at St:ensall camp near York during the summer months. All available recruits from the depot at Warrington are being sent to join the detachment which has just supplied a draft of about 100 men to reinforce the corps on active service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Local Musical Successes.</head>
        <p>Local Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>The result of the examinations held by the Lon- don College of Music (Liverpool Centre) at Liver- pool on the 6% and 7th ult. is issued. 'I here were 113 entrants, of whom 83 passed and 30 failed. The following is a list of the local successes.the teachers' names being given in parentheses :—Practical ex- amination (all pianoforte unless otherwise stated), senior section second-class pass, Lily Green (Mr ICharles 11 Dawson), intermediate section, honours, Lily Roberts, winner of prize (Miss A F Sutton, A.L.C.M.)..first class pass, Cissie Giles, and Luceika Turton (Miss Sutton) ; elementary section, Nellie Dainteth violin, and Annie Dainteth (Miss Cooper); theoretical examination. senior peas section, second class pass, John William Handley (Mr E Wilkes, A.L.C.M.); junior honours section, second class pass, James Morris (Mr E Wilkes), and Lily Green (Mr Charles H Dawson) ; junior pass section, Sissie Gillies, Maggie Allanson,and Luceika Turton (Miss Sutton) ; second class pas, Polly Plinoton (Miss SuAon), and Ethel Dale (Mr Dawspn), elementary section, first class pass, Martha Butcher (Mr Daw- son), and Willy 11 Pickles (Miss Sutton), eecond class pass, Leonard Sutton), Lucy Clara and James Walsh (Miss Sutton). CADBURY'S Cocos, on the testimony of the Lancet "represents the standard of highest purity." It is entirely free from all foreign substances, such as kola, malt, hops, Sc., nor is akkali used to darken the colour (and so deceive the eye). Dr. Andrew Wilson, in a recent article in the Illustrated London News writes : "Cocoa is in itself a perfect food, and requires no addition of drugs whatever." CAD- BURY'S Cocos is absolutely pure, and should be taken by old and young, at all times and in all seasons ; for Children it is an Heal beverage, promoting healthy growth and development in a remarkable degree. Insist on having CADBURY'S, as other Cocos are often substituted for the sake of extra profit. Sold only in Packets and Tins. Advt. 3</p>
        <p>LINSEHD COMPOUND" for Coughs and Colds allays irrittition. Gives immediate, relief.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>EXAMINI4.IIV FRIDAY, MAY 4. 1900</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINI4.IIV FRIDAY, MAY 4. 1900</p>
        <p>hey could cxpEct from the Canal,and he could add so far as the chemical trade of Runcorn was concerned, it had never been b3tter. Morro wages had been paid at %Vigo I</p>
        <p>Works during the last few weeks than for many a year. (Applause.) And there was every likelihood that it would increase. (Renewed applause). Mr. W. Jones proposed "The President." He was only sorry that it was not entrusted to abler hands than his so that full justice could be done to the toast. They would all agree with him that Mr Taylor was never so happy as when he had friends around him. (Applause.) Not only was he happy himself, but he tried—and suc- ceeded too—in making othera happy. (Hear, hear.) Mr Taylor was very generous and ever ready to help a good cause. A charity, concert, committee, swimming gala,or an) teingeldr Taylor could be found at the head ef it. (Ap- plause) When the Football Club wanted a President Mr. S Taylor's name was before it, and it was the unanimous wish of everyone that Mr Taylor should be president. (Applause). Indeed, one gentlema a had said, whatever you do get Mr. Teylor and you will never look behind— (applause)—and it looked like it for net only had they won the cup but the club was out of debt. (Cheers). The toast was put and given with eau3inal honours. Mr. Paylor, who received quite an ovation, expressed how much be felt the kind expressions used in propos- ing the toast, and assured all if there was o,se p.r3on present who was having very happy times, that person was himself. (Hear, hear.) Whatever he gave and whatever he hau to do he liked to s9e people enjoy them- , selves. He had had it in his min 1 for years and • the older he got it grew more upon him that this life was so short that they could not afford if possible to have one moment's unhappiness. (Hear, hear and applause.) Therefore when he was saw people enjoying themselves it gave him pleasure, if it was done in a way which was real enj eyment to them. Waen he was asked to take the preeioency of the Runcorn Football Club, three eentlemen visited his house, and he must confess he told those gentlemen that there was someone else in the town who would help them much better. (No ! 1‘1) !) He was still of that opinion—the two gentlemen preseut on his left —Mr. Norman and Mr, 'rearm : Wan°. Well before ho gave ' the gentlemen his answer he sent them to see those gentlemen. Mr. Norman said he woule not listen, and Mr. Imieon said "You. go back to Taylor." So he accepted. Now he was very pleased indeed to he president of the club—(applause)—and there was no one connected with it to whom it gave more pleasure than to himself to contemplate noon the sosson they had just gone through and co me off so victorious. (Applause.) They had now lifted them- selves ihto a position from which, he hoped they woehl never look behind. (Renewed applause.) He was always willing to help forward as far as lay in his power all good causes. Recreation for people, fron,. the youngest child even to old age would have to be provided one way or another, and he was sure their toxin of Runcorn Footballers had given extreme pleasure to thousands of people from sheer enjoyment watching th m. One had only to stand in the crowd or sit upon the stand to toe the happy smiling e iunterances of spectators when Runcora was carryine all before them. (Hear, hoar.) Last Saturday tie was present at the football ground and had the pleasere of handing media to the winners of the Works Competition; and as he had r..marked then, he would like to see a silver cap provided for that campetition.lt would be but a trifling matter for the employers of trade in Run- corn to provide such a eup,and only a matter of arrange- ment. Mr. ?Inman and Mr. Meadowcroft were present and he threw the suggestion out to .hern—of course no one would tell them—(iaughter)—still he hoped it would be the means of providing a cup far that competition by next season. (Applause.) The toast of "The Football Club" was propased by Mr. H. Hughes (whom Mr. Taylor described as the father of Runcorn footbail). He said it gave him great plessure to propose the health of the" Runcorn Football Club,' for when he asked them to drink the health of tin"" Runcorn Football Club" he was asking them to drink the health of the club with the best record in the kingdom. (Loud applause.) Mr. W. Carroll respond d. The toast of the ev .ning " The Players and Officials " was Riven by Mr. J R Frith, who was an old president of the Runcorn Club. said he felt proud to be with them. He was with the Club in its infancy, and saw its rise to a good position. B3ing an old man then, he left it because he thought be ought to leave it to younger hands. (Applause). Mr. Taylor was young, and ever would be young. (Hear, hear.) He left the Club in a prospereus condition. It was then beginning to make its way in the football world- Those were the good old times—though he was not one of those who believed that the good old times we,e better than the present date. (Hear, bear.) Oh no! He rather thought the Runcorn Football Club had gone through the good old times to better —(hear, hear)—and was now at the top of the field of tae Northern Union. Hayin7, paid a very hits compliment to the players and officials, Mr Frith coupled the toast with the names of Mr. S. Houghton and Mr. C Pawnell. Mr. S. Houghton (captain of the team) whf charac- teriz 'd himself as a better eater that- speaker—(laushter) thanked them for the toast, and Mr. Taylor especially for the way in which he had treated the team. (Applause.) He must tay they had a right loyal team, and in his opinion the finest team in England bar none, and he thought the team which was going to take the cup off them would have to get to:work early. (Loud applause). Mr. Pownah also responded, and said he was the first secretary and treasurer the Football Club had. Just before csming to that gathering he had been looking up the old records and found that in 1878, they were play- ing a club called Panketh. It was thought advisable that an attempt should be made to acquire a little gate money therefore tickets were printed with the result that the total gate money reached 4s 6d. (Laughter and hear, hear.) Be was glad, however, to remind them that the year just past had seen them at the summit as re- gards gate receipts for the amount taken at the Oldham match (£360 odd) was their record. (Applause.) The club was now in a good position having paid all its debts. (Cheers.) The previous year their late President had had to go guarantee in the bank for them, and things had not looked bright. Yet notwithstanding all they had confidence in the players—(hear, hear)—and as a matter of fact the players had stood loyally by them. 'Their players htd been very modest throughout—and though they bad been so—the club had never asked them to play for nothing. (Hear, hear.) And he was also sure that the Runcorn players had done better than any other players, for when the Runcorn Club had any funds they had:been pleased to give them a share of them. (Applause.) The toast was honoured musically and the company sang "They are jolly good fellows,' and heartily cheered the players and officials who remained seated. The" Press" was proposed by Mr E Imison and re- sponded to by Mr F Whalley. During the evening Mr Hunsphrey's sang" I seek for thee in every flower " very pleasantly ; Mr Johnson gave " Tommy Atkins " which was well received. Mr A G Davies (Liverpool) contributed an impromptu tin- , musical sketch, and the songs" 0 let It be soon," " A ' peculiar sort of a feeline," and the " Bounders ootball Team." He was inimitable and caused no end of laughter. A duet by Messrs Humphreys and Hopwood "Larboard Watch " brought to a close the musical pro- gramme. The Chairman conveyed a hearty vote of thanks to the caterer Mrs Latham, for the admirable cooking and serving of the banquet, which was responded to on her behalf byalser father Mr Ind. The proce,dings then terminated with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>HONOUTVING THE LANCASHIRE CUP</head>
        <p>HONOUTVING THE LANCASHIRE CUP</p>
        <p>WINNERS AT RUNCORN. Presentation of Medals by Mr. S. Taylor.</p>
        <p>Dinner to the Football Team.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Taylor, J.P., Ryelal M mut, Runcorn, on Wednesday evening entertained the Runcorn Football Club members to dinner at the Wilson's Hotel, Bridge- street. In addition to the President, there were present : WJones(chairman of committces),F J Norman, J.P., E Imison, C.C., J Meadowcroft, J W Lightburn. Captain G F Ashton, Messrs J R Frith, M J Frith, W Carroll, T C Haddock, J Wilding, J H Weston,A Light- burn, C Pownall (treasurer), J Ellison (secretary), S Houghton (captain), James Tudor (Weston), la Hughes, Jonathan Horsefield, J St ikeF, Henry Presents (station- master), W Collier, T Jones, F Johnson, Superintendent °kelt, Dr McLennan, Captain Rowl Inds, T Robinson, the members of the League and " A" Teams of the foot- ball club, and other friends. The Menu was as follows :—Soups, tomato, kidney;</p>
        <p>fish, boiled halibut, sauce aux crevettes ; entrees, roast turkey and sausage, kidney and steak pie ; j tints, roast beef, roast and boiled mutton, onion sauce, caper sauce; vegetables, boiled and baked potatoes, cauli- flower, spring cabbage; sweets, Runcorn champion pudding, jellies,blanc mange, cheese and salad ; descert. After the above had been done full justice to and the Royal toasts had been duly honoured The Chairman proposed the " Army, Navy, and Volunteers." He was sure there was no toast through- out the British Empire, at the present time, that was re- ceived with more pleasure and greater honour than the toast of the " Army, Navy, aid Volunteers." (Ap- plause.) He coupled with the toast the name of Captain Ashton. (Applause.) Captain Asistan said he was very proud indeed to be , allowed to respond to that toast. At the same time he must say it was with a great deal of diffidence that he did so. He did so as regards the "Army and Navy" feeling very much the same way as a boy would who was responding on behalf of his father. Considering that he was only connected with the Voluntoers it would be presumption for him to a certain extent to respond on bthalf of the Army and Navy. So far as they were concerned it would be sufficient to men- tion the work and reat deeds they wore doing in South Africa:as a sufficient response for them. (Applause.) So fer as the volunteers were concerned up to the present year they had certainly felt that they had not been ap- t reciated by the Government, as they felt they migat have been. (Hear, hear.) There was no doubt tail had they been shown the feeling that had been shown to them throughout the whole country during the pro- grets of the present war, there were matte young men who would no doubt have joined that rnovom sot, whilst through the laxity of the Government they had only looked upon volunteeriag as playing at soldiers. He thought that new, however, through the present war; by deeds done by the Colonial Forces, and the Home Volueteers in South Africa; and also by the number of Volunteers who had given in their names as willing to go to the front, the result would be that the Government would in future show better appreciation of them. (Applause.) He trusted that all young men, in future, who felt they could give time would join the Volunteers and do their level best to keep out any such thing as conscription in England. (Heir, tear.) tie thanked them very much for the kind manner in which they had accepted the toast. Mr. Taylor said his mxt duty was one in which he took extreme pleasure—the presentation of medals ta "our football team." (Hear, hear and applause.) He felt he could not do duty in speaking of the Runcorn i Football team. The high manner in which they had acquitted themselves throughout the Lancashire Senior Competition was A credit ; and especially 80 seeing that everyone of them were Runcorn lads. (Appliusn) When he thought that in a matter of about 2,000 in- babicants or less they were ableto select a teem ot young men as footballers who could aceorriplidt that,he thought it spoke for itself. (Applause.) Their lads in comnotiee with all Lancashire bad played 26 matches and only lost two. Were he to speak for an hour —if he was able to do so—the very fact that they had brought the cup to Rurcarn—(applaus.s)—would be eaough to say what he wished to express, and that too after facing such worthy tams as Oelhem and Salford and others. Why it was creditable beyond expression. (Hear,hear and applause.) There were in that competition large towns with some- thing like 140,000 inhabitants, and they had to go to other places to import players from all p arts of the country to face these "lads of ours." (Applause). I There veal, he felt convinced, no one in Runcorn, either a football eethusiast or one adverse to the sport, but delighted in knowing that they had won the cup. (Applause.) And as long as they possessed such a team as they did last year, he felt sure if he only lived to see it, that the cup would not be long in coming again. He was further pleased to see that the male inhabitants of Runcorn were not the only people that appreciated the gallant perform- ances of its players. (Applause.) There were lady admirers, and he bad a sum of money put into his hand which had been subscribed by the lady admirers of the team so that they might drink their health out of the ' Northern Union cup. (Applause). He would ask the host to fill the cup, and whilst he was doing so would 'be pleased to present the medals to the players. After that he would ask them all to join him in drinking the health of the "lady spectators" of Runcorn's gallant team. (Cheers.) Mr. Taylor then presented the beautiful medals to the following :—Messrs S Houghton (captain), T Wilkinson. J Butterworth, T Warder, C Butterworth, D JOOO3, J Faulkner, J Jolley. 8 Walker, W Lightfoot, F Gayter, A Taylor,J Langley,J Tomlinson, 0 Hughes, F Darlineton, H Farmer, and D Reece (the two latter were absent through injuries). Mr Taylor also asked Mr W Jones (the chairman of the committee) to accept a Northern Union medal, which hal been subscribed for by the members and players of the Runcorn Club, as a small token of their appreciation of his services to them. (Applause.) He hoped Mr.Joues would live a long time to use the medal which gave him so many privelges. (Applause.) Mr. W. Jones accepted the medal as a gift he would treasure, and after his football days were over it would be something to remind him of them and their happi- ness. (Applause.) Mr Jones then dealt briefly with the history of the Runcorn Club, and spoke of its advent into the Northern Union as one of the best things that could have happened. Now they were in a position to pay their players not only broken time but as much as their purse could afford, a thing they could not have done had they stuck to the old Rugby Union. He re- ferred also to the spirit of generosity and charity that actuated footballers. For during the last season £250 had been granted by the Northern Union to the War Funds; and only the other day a grant of 200 guineas was voted to the Indian Famine Fund. (Loud applause.) He was very pleased with the medal he had received. Mr. A leghtburn said it was his duty to keep up the Presentation by asking Mr. S Taylor to accept mom the Runcorn Football Club, a Northern Union Medal, as a slight token of their regard for him. They hoped it would be a gentle reminder to Mr Taylor that during his first year of presidency the club had carried off the cup for a second time (Cheers). They wished Mr. Taylor long life and happiness, and hoped he would continue amongst them and carry on the good works he did in Runcorn. (Cheers). The presentation was loudly applauded and the assembly sang " For he's a jolly goad fellow " and gave three hearty cheers for the president and one for Miss Taylor. Mr. Taylor responding, said he scarcely knew what to do. In fact, he considered it rather premature. He was a very young colt as far as taking an active part in foot- ball was concerned. He had only been president of the club for a short time and if he was not as old as he was, he might get vain and full of pride and kick over the traces at such an honour being conferred upon him. (Laughter and applause.) He appreciated the kindness of the committee and could assure them that the medal would always be a source of pleasure to him. Indeed he hoped to hand it down to his people, and he had such confidence in his family that he felt they would, even when the old man was gone, highly appreciate it. (Loud applause.) Messrs. C. Pownall and J. Ellison, the treasurer and secretary of the club were next the recipients of a Northern Union Medal, an honour which both highly appreciated and each viea with the other as to who was the happiest. The presentations being over the health of the Runcorn Lady Sepporters was drunk out of the silver cup, and honoured musically. Mr. J. Mesdowcroft then gave the "Town and Trade of Runcorn" and in doing RO referred to the benefit the Ship Canal was proving to Runcorn. He failed,however to see any connection between that toast, and the congratulation of the Runcorn Football Club, at first, but it had dawned upcn him afterwards that there was a connection since the town of Runcorn depended upon the trade of Runcorn. and the trade of Runcorn dependel to a very great extent upon its employers and workmen. The two were bound up and connected together, and unless both worked amictbly together they could not succeed. (Hear, hear.) To him, perhaps, belonged the honour of following the Runcorn Football Club longer than any other person present—or at least as long. Anyhow be remembered its inception and was pleased at its present achievenn nts. (Applause.) Having impressed upon the meeting the fact that football had done much to elevate the working men of Runcorn and that there was nothing like an interest on the part of employers in the doings of worhingmen to brims, about good trade results, Mr. Meadowcroft concluded by pieeging himself to do what he could for the town at all timts. (epplause.) Mr. J. Norman responded. He referred to the hope- ful outlook he and a tow others had held during the dark days of 5 and 6 years ago,when there was every appear- ance of all the trade and works leaving Runcorn, and urged all to leek forward and make the best use of the present opportunities when Runcorn appeared better •,; than ever. Mr. Meadowcroft had told them of what</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
