      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EARLESTOWN 8c NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN 8c NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall Newton, on Monday morning before Mr. Gillespie, Michael Byron, and Harry Boughton wars charged with being drunk,ancl were each fined 23 61 and costs. James Curley and Joseph Curry for a similar offence were also mulcted in a fine of 28 6d and costs each. • B.A.YDOCK BOWLING Cian3. The Haydock Club opened their green on Satur- day week,with a sweepstake amlugst the members which was continued during last week and com- pleted. There were a good number of entries, and although toe members might be considered to be out of form, some very good bowling was seen. The first prize of a guinea was taken by R Whittle, and the second prize of 133 by J L3B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>CHILD DROWNED AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>CHILD DROWNED AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the Town Hall, Earles- town on Tuesday evening by Mr. F. A. Jones on the body of a child Eliza Roberts aged 2 years,daughter ot William and Maria Roberts of 9, Wood-street, Earlestown, who WAS found drowned in a washing mug containing water, on Saturday morning last. It appeared from the evidence that the washing mug was in the yard and contained washing water. The child was playing in the yard at 11-45, and shortly after this time Annie Roberts, a sister of the deceased,went into tne yard and then called out '`Oh ,Aammie she's dead." Mrs. Roberts ran into the yard and found the child in her sister's arms, and quite dead. The witness Annie Roberts said Ishe found her sister head first in the washing mug. She lifted her out and called to her mother. A ver- dict of "accidental death' was returned. STACK FIRE AT NBWTOIST.</p>
        <p>Shortly before two o'clock on Tuesday morning P.C. Parry discovered an outbreak of fire in the stack yard of the farm occupied by Mr. Thomas Hayward, farmer, Newton. On investigation he found that the fire had got a goo.' hold of two stacks of straw and one of hay, and he promptly raised an alarm. Roses were pro- cured and water was obtained from the main hydraunts. P.S. Ling and P.C. Linney who saw the fire frooa a distance hurried to the scene, aod gave Parry what assistance they could, but it was at once seen that there was no poszible hope of saving the burning stacks, and their efforts were directed to preventing the fire spreading. In this they were successful, but the three stacks, valued at about £75 were entirely destroyed. No reason for the outbreak can be given. The loss is covered by insurance with the Royal Fire Insurance Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS HOSPITAL:</p>
        <p>Sir David Gambk, C. 8., presided at the annual meet ing in connection with the St. Heens Hospital on Mon- day. The rfleort and balance-sheet showed a highly satisfactory state of affairs the debt on the enlargement fund having been cleared Off by a transference of £909 16, 111 to the fund from £l,OOO which had been received from the legacy left by Miss Garton. Councillor Martin moved j the adoption of the report, and said it was the moat satisfactory one ever issued. Oae of the best features was the increase in the number of id aweek subscribers, who now numbered between 7,000 and 8,000. The remaining £23,000 would shortly be received from Miss G'irton's legacy. Alderman Sinclair secsodfd, and the report was alopted. Mr. W. Gamble subse- quently explained that the money from the legacy would go to toren an end° ornent fund tor the hospital,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>TERRIBLE CYCLING FATALITY AT</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE CYCLING FATALITY AT</p>
        <p>LATCH FORD.</p>
        <p>Report of the Inquest.</p>
        <p>Latchford was the scene of a terrible accident on Monday evening when Mr. John Tinsley, a gentleman in the emp:oy ot Mr. Grime. draper, Hedge-street, col- lided with a trap while cycling and sustained injuries which terminated fat ally a few hours later. Mr. Tinsley was struck in the chat by the shaft of the trap, his ribs being fractured, causing laceration of the lungs. and he also received a scalp wound in falling. He was well-known in Warrington and the accident created a painful sensation. Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, con- ducted an enquiry into the circumltances of the sad affair at the Police Station on Wednesday evening. Mr. W. Rsb-rts was the foreman ot the jury, which included many personal friends of the deceased. Toe first witness called was William Tinsley, brother of the deceased who said the latter was 44 last birthday. He had not seen deceased for twelve months as be (witness) did not live in War- rington. Deceased had good eyesight. Harold Clarke, MS) employed with Mr. Grime, sa;el ' he was in company with the deceased when the accident took place. They had been as far as Hon Green on the Keutsford-road and returned the same way, crossing the swing bridge at Latchford a few minutes before 9 o'clock. They had previously had some discu,sion about the road borne neither desiring to ride over Latchford- road as It was so rough. Mr. Tinsley however said the other was not much better and they decided ts go on and ride slowly in order to avoid the bumping. The 'bus had just started for town as they reached the end of the bridge, and they overtook it just beyond the rail ,ay bridge. Tney were going about five or six miles an hour, the 'bus was well on the left of the road, and Mr. Tinsley was pas;ing it in the middle of the road. As he passed he turned to the right and glanced down Mr. flenshall's drive—looking at the trees or something. Witness was close behind and noticed the trap coming up in front. He shouted, and so did somebody from the trap,but deceased did not hear until too late and the trap ran into him. The trap had lights and so had Mr Tinsley. ' All were on the correct side of the road, and witness cOuld not attach blame to ahyone but the deceased. He was not looking at the time, and witness considered it a pure accident.—By the Coroner : He could not say how far the trap was from Mr. Tinsley before the driver saw him ; it would only be a few yards. He was a most careful rider. . Mr. Critchley (a juror) ; It is a good wide piece of road, The Foreman : Did someone attract his attention. Witness: No. He passed Mr. Hershall when we were going and perhaps thought he would see him as he went back. I saw nobody in the garden. In reply to questions from the Coroner witness added that he ran to Mr. Tinsley immediately the accident happened and found him bleeding from the wound in his head. Dr. Leadbetter C 41119 out and deceased was carried into his house, and a cab sent for. The doctor washed the wound and stitched it. In fact everything as done that could be done, and deceased was taken to the Infirmary as soon as his head was at.tched. Joseph Shakeshaft, Lymm, maid on Monday night he ' 'was driving a horse and trap, and had a friend named l Lowe with him. He saw the 'bus when some distance ' from it,and kept well on the correct side of the road. , When he get level with the 'bus witness suddenly caught • sight of the bicycle light coming from b,hind the 'bus and a couple of yards in front of • the mare. He pulled up with all his might, but felt the wheel go over something. Tne point of the right-hand shaft must have caught the deceased. From the time witness first saw the deceased until the accident happened he had no time to get out of the way, though he was only driving at a moderate speed. Mr. , Shakeshaft added that he had known the dscl'ased 25 , years. , Kathleen Bowyer, nurse at the Infirmary, ' said de- ceased was admitted s. t 9-15 sin Monday night, and died . at 12-30 on Tuesday morning. . This was all the evidence, and after a momentary</p>
        <p>consultation the jury brought in a verdict of "Accidental death," attaching blame to no one. . The Foreman said he should like also to say on behalf of the jury how deeply they regretted the accident to deceased, who was a personal flitsal of almost all of them. Speaking for himself, Mr. Roberts said he bad known Mr. Tinsley 30 years, not only as a next door neighbour but they were both appreatined to the same business, and a more exemplary man he had not known in Warrington. As a cyclist he believed deceased was most careful in every respect. They all ex.,:ressed tly ir deep s7mpathy with the relatives of the deceased and 3113 with Mr. Shakesha,ft, whom be kiev had felt the affsir most acutely. They were glad to be able to say they acquittei him of any blame whatever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL</p>
        <p>CAMPANOLOGY --On Friday, at the church of St. I'Vilfrid, for practic,l 720 Oxford treble bob minor by H T Moston, treble F Jones 2, F T Spence (conductor) 3, J PtieStiPC7 4, R Duckworth 5, R G Lewis tenor. This is the first 720 in the method for Messrs Jones, Priestley, vnd Duckwortn, who hail fiotn Dareabury ; also 594 grandsire triplets by J INlainwarina treble, R Duckworth 2, H T M cston 3, R G. Lewis 4, 8 Horsfall 5, F 1' Spenen (conductor) 6, W Bebington 7, J E Ashcroft tenor. W Bebington is from Warrinvton, and it is his loryzest len.th in the method. On Saturday for practice 720 Kent treble bnlo mincr by H T Moston 1, F Jones 2, R worth 3, J Priestley 4, F T Spence (conductor) 5, R G Lewis tenor. Great credit is due to Messrs Jones, Duckworth, and Priestly for the manner in which they handed their bells, as this was their first 720 in the method, and was rung at the first attempt. Weight of tenor, 12cwt.</p>
