      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>TRADING WITHOUT A LICENSE AT</head>
        <p>TRADING WITHOUT A LICENSE AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. A Wholesale Dealer Fined.</p>
        <p>Francie George Burns, of 65, High-street, Runcorn, whole3ale wine and spirit merchant, &amp;c., pleaded guilty to selling a dozen half pint bottles of beer, on the 15th of March last, at Runcorn, without having a retail license.—The information was laid by Mr John Donnel- lan. one of H.M. Officers of Inland Revenue.—Mr Edward Lay (supervitor of Inland Revenue Chester), said from information received at the office of the Inland Revenue of certain procedures of the de- fendant, an assistant of the Inland Revenue Office, visited the premises of Mr. Burns, at 65. High-street, Runcorn, on the date in question, and obtained a dozen halt pint bottles of what was called oatmeal stout, and a doz-n half pints of the best bitter ale. He directed them tolt be sent to a certain Mrs. Mote, in Runcarn, with whom he wee lodging. Toe a-Big:ant paid the money and obtained a receipt for it so that the contract was comple'.ed there and thrn. Be (Mr. Lowy) might sly that Mr. Burns held a wholesale license to sell beer in quantities of not less than four ealloas, or two dozen quart bottles. On account of Mr. Burns net acting strictly in accord with the license they had previously had to caution him, so that no want of knowledge could be pleaded on his part. Under tho‘e circurnetances lie (Mr Lowy) asked for a substantial penalty, for it was an offence against the retail traders of the town, as well as against the Revenue.—Mr Burns, in extenuation of the offence, explained that the order was served not upon the order of the young man who had come to the shop, but upon the order of Miss Moss, who was a customer of his, and the voting man was simply treated as a messenger of hers.—Mr: Lowy said that would not do, as it practically amounted to a plea of net guilty.—Mr. Burns then went on to say that simply a teehn'uil offence had been committed, brouglit abeart by want of knowledge on the part of his assistant. He was no in the shep himself, and had he been there, it would not have occurred ; for he knew perfectly well the order could not be taksn.—The Bench impo3ed a fine of 503.</p>
        <p>ALL COUNTRY PEOPLE should read The Rural World id. weekly, which contains articles or qcueee, Cattle, thps Miry, Poultry, Bees, Rabbits, Pigs, Gardening, Natural History, Botany of the Farm and Garden, the Home, Foreign and Colonial Intelligence. Anwers to Correspondents, etc. It is profusely illustrated and is altogether, a most interest- ing and instructive farming and country paper. A good rn,dium for servants requiring situations. The Manager offers to send a specimen tree. The address is 110 and lie. STIt A.ND. Londm, W.—Advt. ' LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds Asthma and Bronchitis Of Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
