      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The Warrington eleven played their second match of the 3 ea3orx at Bolton on Saturday. Only a small number of spectators witnessed the game, which was rendered rather unpleasant by the cold wind. Bolton played a strong team which included Ward, who has done ser- vice for Lancashire, Pointon, late professional to the llorwich Club, and Ellis, who has occasionally played for Lancashire, whilst Warrington placed the same team that made a draw with Sefton in the field.</p>
        <p>The spin of the coin was in favour of thn visitors. and Thewlis and B. Silcock batted first to the deliveries of Ellis and Pointon. Play commenced quietly, Silcock having a narrow escape of being run out. Runs came singly, and with the score at tee, Brearly took the ball from Pointon. Thewlis had knocked up six when he attempted to drive Ellis, who succeeded in stopping the ball high up and bringing off a brilliant catch. J Callon followed, hut did not remain long, Brearlev getting past his defence with the score stand- ing at 15. E Connor opened in vigorous style and with a couple off Brearley"„he causedl2o to be hoisted. Silcock played very cautiously at Ellis, and with eight to his eta dit Le was beaten, Connor and G Clarke then going to the wicket. Connor hit out freely and he was unfor- tunate in being given out lbw with three two's to his credit. With the score still standing at 26. Clarke was dismissed by a catch at third man, and J Thompson. who joined J W Bibby, opened with a cauple of boun- daries. He had, however, only added another run when Brearley shot down his middle stump, and with six men gone for 36, Warrington wore in anything but a favour- able position. Bibby, who was partnered by Callon played free cricket, and with 40 up Ellis was taken off for the first time in favour of S Slater. Callon was very ut easy, and he gave Brearley an easy catch at mid on with only two to his credit. Runs still came steadily, and the half-century hsd been pissed when Podrnore gave a s mple return to Slater. Bibte, soon reached double figures, but he was run out as a result of a fine piece of fielding. G Hampson ani E Gibson made a capital stand for the last wicket, the latter nulling a fast one from tirearley to the leg bound- ary, but with the score at 73 Hampson badly ran himself out. Bolton commenced their task with Ellis and G Knowles, E Connor and Triewlis sharing the bowlinz. Bad luck attended Warrington in the opening over, for• the amateur had the misfortune to sprain his right leg. and had to retire. J W Bibby took up the attacia, but the first wicket fell to credit of Thewlis, the Warrington captain bringing off a fine catch in the slip% Pointon came next. ,and fora time stow cricket was witnessed . With the score at 17, Knowles was caught at cover slip, and Ward filled the vacancy. Both batsmen exhibited the greatest caution, and little could be done with either bowler. Eventually Ward forced the game, and with 'runs coming freely, G Clarke relieved Bibby. The new bowlers' first over was expensive,whilst a capital c• ance of ranning out Pointon was spoiled by a will return. Ward continued to bat in splendid style and practically settled the issue of the match. The Warrington total was passed without further loss, and near the call of time Pointon was brilliantly taken by Bibby, the total reading 103 for the loss of three wickets, Ward taking out his bat for a capital 52.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-04_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY,</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY,</p>
        <p>This annual event occurred on Sunday and dispite the unfavourable weather of the morning and after- noon was attended with success. The collections for the day amounted to £4B 19s id, which exceeded anticipations. As on former occasions, the accom- modation for the choir had been increased. Within the communion rails there was a choice display of flowers and plants. These had been arranged with much taste, and noticeable amongst the collec- tion were some beautiful lilies daffodils and covi- slips. The morning service was conducted by Mr J A Yonge, M.A., principal of the Northern Con- gregational School, Silcoates, Wakefield, and eldest son of the respected pastor of Wycliffe Church. Mr Yonge addressed himself more particularly and by the Close attention given to him it was evident that his remarks were highly appreciated. The subject of his address was "The Journey or a Christian Life." He -pointed out that it was a hill of difficulty which must be climbed, and proceeded to give his hearers four rules which he thought would be helpful on the journey. These were :---(1) start at once; (2) lay aside every weight; (3) go on climbing; (4) keep to the Guide. The choir sang the beautiful anthem "As the hart pants." The children's voices were heard to advantage in several of the hymns. Mr W H Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ at all theleervices with his I accustomed ability. "The Building of the Temple"was the title cf a ser- vice of sang which was given in the afternoon by the scholars, assisted by members of the church choir. There was an "open meeting" of the P.S.A. Society and the church was well filled. Mr. G Wallington, the superintendent of the school presided over the proceedings, and briefly introduced the Mayoress (Mrs. K. Roberts) who read the scriptural passages relating to the building of Solomon's temple and which formed the theme of the cantata. Solo pass- ages were efficiently interpreted by Mrs. A. Long 7, shaw and Miss Travis (contraltos). Mrs Gleave and Miss C Hill (sopranos), Mr A Griffiths (tenor), and Mr WII Wallington (bass). The children, who were under the conductorship of Mr William Brown, very sweetly sang choruses and the choir also Con- tributed some attractive pieces. Not the least in- teresting passages were those contributed by the male voices, entitled "in Thy Providence, 0 Lord" and "Stroke by stroke our axes fall." The service concluded with the singing of "Now thank we all our God."</p>
        <p>There was a large congregation at the evening service when the pulpit was occupied by the Rev C. Leach, D.D., of Cavendish-street, Chapel, Man- chester. Dr. Leach delivered a vary practical and instructive discourse from the text Where is the flock that was given thee; thy beautiful flock " ? (Jeremiah xiii. 20). He applied the text to the responsibility of the Sunday school teacher who was entrusted with a "flock" of boys and girls. Of course they could net take over the responsibility of the parents,but were responzible for the spiritual welfare of the children who were placed under their care. The teachers were like sculptors,chipping off the useless corners until the object was achiever], and they wpuld have to give an account of that work when asked where were their flocks. The choir ably rendered the anthem, "Hear my Prayer" (Mendelssohn), Mrs. R. 11. Pearson taking the Dolo portion.</p>
        <p>Proposed Memorial to the Late Mr. John Thompson. A scheme is on foot to provide a memorial to the late 'fr. John Thompson, surveyor to the Warring- ton Highway Board, in the shape of a drinking fountain at Winwick. The road men and other employ 63 of the late gentleman have originated the idea and are now appealing for subscriptions. Mr. B. S. Newcombe, Bewsey Chambers,the present sur- veyor, h attending to the matter in the Warrington area and will be pleased to receive suivcriptions. Will of the Late Mr. T. G. Rylands.</p>
        <p>Probate of the will has been granted in London, and the estate has been valued at £156,181 17s 51 gross and £150,063 18s 5d net, of Mr Thomas Glaze- brook Rylands, of Highfield, Thelwall, J.P . F.S.A. F.R.A S., F.L.S. Mayor of Warrington, 1853-59 and. of Rylands Bros., Limited, wire manufacturers War- rington, who died on the 14th February last, aged St years, son of the late John Rylands of Warrington. By his will of the 3rd January, 1898, with a codicil of the 16th February, 1893, the testator bequeathed to his wife,Mrs. Elizabeth Rylands, £350. hiscollec- tion of coins (other than current cein), his tele- copes, naicrosaopes, and scientific collections, the use and enjoyment of his household effects, the use and enjoyment during her wPowhood of Highfields or £l5O a year in lieu thereof, the income during her life of a sum of £3,000, and an annuity during her widowhood of £2,000. Mr Rylands bequeathed his printed and written books to the Liverpool University College, and he left his residuary estate in trust as to four-twelfths thereof each tor his sons John Paul Rylands and William Harry Rylends as to three-twelfths for his daughter Martha Glazebrook Rylands and as to tie remaining one-twelfth for his granddaughter Phyllis Dorothea Mason. TO TWA DEAF.—A rentlemaq who cured himself of Deafness and Noises in the Head after fourteen sears suffering will gladly send full particular 3 of the remedy, on receipt of stamped addressed envel.lpe.-10. CLIFTON. Amberle House, Strand, London. W.C.— 4clut. LADI-93 BEST (HACH BOOTS AND SHONSsUNDAR THE CLOCK. RIZIDG 11-STRE 13T.—Advt. UNDRIITH OLOOK ron GOOD BOOTS.—Adyt</p>
